    "title": "Ahaziah's accession age",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Kings gives Ahaziah’s age at his accession as twenty-two; the KJV and the Hebrew of Chronicles give forty-two. The Greek of Chronicles gives twenty.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 8:26: “Two and twenty years old was Ahaziah when he began to reign; and he reigned one year in Jerusalem. And his mother's name was Athaliah, the daughter of Omri king of Israel.” Hebrew (WLC): בֶּן־עֶשְׂרִ֨ים וּשְׁתַּ֤יִם שָׁנָה֙ אֲחַזְיָ֣הוּ בְמָלְכֹ֔ו [twenty-two years old was Ahaziah when he became king]. KJV 2 Chronicles 22:2: “Forty and two years old was Ahaziah when he began to reign, and he reigned one year in Jerusalem. His mother's name also was Athaliah the daughter of Omri.” Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 22:2: בֶּן־אַרְבָּעִ֨ים וּשְׁתַּ֤יִם שָׁנָה֙ אֲחַזְיָ֣הוּ בְמָלְכֹ֔ו [forty-two years old was Ahaziah when he became king]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2 Chronicles 22:2: Ὢν ἐτῶν εἴκοσι Ὀχοζίας ἐβασίλευσε [being twenty years old, Ochozias began to reign]. Brenton Greek 2 Kings 8:26: Υἱὸς εἴκοσι καὶ δύο ἐτῶν Ὀχοζίας [twenty-two years old was Ochozias]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/8/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/22/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH22.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI08.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The same named mother and one-year reign identify the parallel accounts. Both ages cannot describe the same accession in the ordinary sense. The KJV follows the Hebrew in each book: twenty-two in Kings, forty-two in Chronicles. Brenton’s Greek has twenty-two in Kings and twenty in Chronicles, so in Chronicles it differs from the KJV and from Kings.",
    "scope": "This extends the published record of the same ID with the Hebrew and Greek wording; the finding between the two KJV verses is unchanged. G applies at 2 Chronicles 22:2 only. No claim is made about which number is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
