    "title": "Forty-eight Levite towns: stated totals against the names",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Joshua 21 names 48 Levite towns and totals 48. 1 Chronicles 6 states the same group totals, 13, 10, 13 and 12, but names 11, 8, 13 and 10. The Aleppo-based digital text served at the supplied address omits four Reubenite towns.",
    "text": "Joshua 21:41 (KJV): “forty and eight cities”; its lists name 13, 10, 13 and 12 towns. 1 Chronicles 6:60 (KJV): “All their cities throughout their families were thirteen cities”, after eleven names in verses 57–60 (Juttah and Gibeon of Joshua 21:16–17 are not there). 1 Chronicles 6:61: “ten cities”; verses 67–70 name eight (no Eltekeh or Gibbethon). 1 Chronicles 6:63: “twelve cities”; verses 77–81 name ten (two towns from Zebulun where Joshua 21:34–35 has four). The thirteen Gershonite towns are all named (6:71–76). Named total: 42. Joshua 21:25 (KJV) gives Manasseh “Gathrimmon”, the town just listed for Dan in 21:24; the Hebrew (WLC) has גַּת־רִמֹּ֖ון, the Brenton Greek has τὴν Ἰεβαθὰ [Iebatha], and 1 Chronicles 6:70 (KJV) has “Bileam”. Joshua 21:36–37, the four Reubenite towns (“four cities”): the Unicode/XML Leningrad Codex edition prints both verses with a mark [X]. The supplied excerpt does not include the key defining that mark. The Aleppo-based consonantal edition served by bolls.life (HAC) has 43 verses in the chapter, goes from Zebulun straight to Gad, and still totals ערים שתים עשרה [twelve towns] for Merari after eight names. The Brenton Greek includes the Reubenite towns (πόλεις τέσσαρες). Fetched from https://tanach.us/Server.txt?Josh21:35-21:38, https://bolls.life/get-text/HAC/6/21/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/6/21/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOS21.htm.",
    "reasoning": "This is arithmetic inside each list. Chronicles’ stated subtotals exceed its own names by six. Joshua’s names reach 48 only with verses 36–37, which the KJV includes; without them the Hebrew names 44 and Merari has eight against a stated twelve.",
    "scope": "Different spellings or names for what may be the same town (Holon/Hilen, Ain/Ashan, Almon/Alemeth, Kibzaim/Jokmeam and others) are not counted here as contradictions. A shortfall can come from loss in copying, abbreviation or an earlier form of the list; none is shown by these texts. The G tag covers only the different town name in the Greek of 21:25. The [X] marks are visible in the supplied Unicode/XML excerpt, but their definition is not provided there. The 43-verse count describes how one website serves the Aleppo-based edition; neither observation establishes what every manuscript contains. A supplied critical-edition reference would be needed for a claim about the physical codices. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text and the Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient translation tradition. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees, and neither edition settles which reading is earlier.",
