    "title": "The angel’s name: “secret” or “wonderful”?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV has the angel refuse his name because “it is secret”. The Hebrew word is from the root the KJV renders “wonderously” in the next verse, and the Septuagint says “it is wonderful”.",
    "text": "KJV Judges 13:18: “Why askest thou thus after my name, seeing it is secret?”. Hebrew (WLC): וְהוּא־פֶֽלִאי [and it is wonderful]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): καὶ αὐτό ἐστι θαυμαστόν [and it is wonderful]. Next verse, KJV: “the angel did wonderously”; Hebrew וּמַפְלִ֣א לַעֲשֹׂ֔ות, from the same root. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/7/13/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JDG13.htm.",
    "reasoning": "This Greek edition describes the name as wonderful, whereas the KJV calls it secret. The Hebrew name description and the following action share a root associated with wonder, but that alone does not settle the description’s contextual meaning.",
    "scope": "A shared root does not settle context: the Hebrew word can carry the sense “beyond understanding”, which lies near “secret”, so the difference is one of emphasis more than of fact. No H tag is used because the admitted overlap with “beyond understanding” does not establish a Hebrew meaning error. The G tag records the different description in the named Greek edition. No doctrine about the angel’s identity is drawn from it. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text and the Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient translation tradition. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees, and neither edition settles which reading is earlier. English glosses in brackets are the researcher’s plain renderings, not lexicon entries.",
