    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, following the Hebrew, says the concubine “played the whore against him”. The Septuagint says only that she “went away from him”.",
    "text": "KJV Judges 19:2: “his concubine played the whore against him, and went away from him”. Hebrew (WLC): וַתִּזְנֶ֤ה עָלָיו֙ פִּֽילַגְשֹׁ֔ו [and his concubine was unfaithful against him]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): Καὶ ἐπορεύθη ἀπʼ αὐτοῦ ἡ παλλακὴ αὐτοῦ [and his concubine went from him]. KJV 19:3: the husband went “to speak friendly unto her, and to bring her again”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/7/19/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JDG19.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Greek has no charge of sexual unfaithfulness: the woman leaves, and the man goes to win her back. In the Hebrew and KJV she is accused first.",
    "scope": "This is a difference between two text traditions about the same sentence. It does not show which is earlier, and it does not bear on responsibility for what happens to her at Gibeah. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text and the Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient translation tradition. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees, and neither edition settles which reading is earlier. English glosses in brackets are the researcher’s plain renderings, not lexicon entries.",
