    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV and Hebrew the Levite says he is going to “the house of the Lord”. In the Septuagint he says “to my house”, and the story does end at his own house.",
    "text": "KJV Judges 19:18: “I am now going to the house of the Lord”. Hebrew (WLC): וְאֶת־בֵּ֤ית יְהוָה֙ אֲנִ֣י הֹלֵ֔ךְ [and to the house of the LORD I am going]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): εἰς τὸν οἶκόν μου ἐγὼ πορεύομαι [to my house I am going]. KJV 19:28–29: he “gat him unto his place” … “when he was come into his house”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/7/19/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JDG19.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The two traditions give the traveller different destinations in the same sentence. Nothing later in the chapter takes him to a sanctuary; he goes home.",
    "scope": "A man may have meant to visit a sanctuary on the way home, so the KJV wording is not contradicted by 19:29. Do not claim which reading is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text and the Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient translation tradition. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees, and neither edition settles which reading is earlier. English glosses in brackets are the researcher’s plain renderings, not lexicon entries.",
