    "title": "Who killed Goliath the Gittite: David, or Elhanan?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "1 Samuel 17 says David killed Goliath. At 2 Samuel 21:19 the KJV says Elhanan “slew the brother of Goliath”, but the Hebrew and the Greek have no words for “the brother of”: they say Elhanan struck down Goliath the Gittite.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Samuel 21:19: “Elhanan the son of Jaareoregim, a Bethlehemite, slew the brother of Goliath the Gittite, the staff of whose spear was like a weaver's beam”. Hebrew (WLC): וַיַּ֡ךְ אֶלְחָנָן֩ בֶּן־יַעְרֵי֙ אֹרְגִ֜ים בֵּ֣ית הַלַּחְמִ֗י אֵ֚ת גָּלְיָ֣ת הַגִּתִּ֔י [and Elhanan son of Jaare-oregim the Bethlehemite struck Goliath the Gittite]. Aleppo-based edition (HAC): ויך אלחנן בן יערי ארגים בית הלחמי את גלית הגתי. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): ἐπάταξεν Ἐλεανὰν υἱὸς Ἀριωργὶμ ὁ Βαιθλεεμίτης τὸν Γολιὰθ τὸν Γεθαῖον [and Eleanan son of Ariorgim the Bethlehemite struck Goliath the Gittite]. KJV 1 Samuel 17:50: “So David prevailed over the Philistine with a sling and with a stone, and smote the Philistine, and slew him”. KJV 1 Chronicles 20:5: “Elhanan the son of Jair slew Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite”; Hebrew (WLC): לַחְמִי֙ אֲחִי֙ גָּלְיָ֣ת הַגִּתִּ֔י [Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/21/, https://bolls.life/get-text/HAC/10/21/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2SA21.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/13/20/.",
    "reasoning": "In the Hebrew of 2 Samuel 21:19 the object of “struck” is Goliath the Gittite himself. The words “the brother of” stand in the Hebrew of 1 Chronicles 20:5 but not in 2 Samuel 21:19; the KJV supplies them in Samuel. Without them, 2 Samuel 21:19 credits Elhanan with the killing that 1 Samuel 17:50 credits to David.",
    "scope": "The KJV’s added words match the parallel verse in Chronicles. This comparison does not establish the translators’ intent or the italic marking of printed KJV editions; the packaged text contains no such markup. No claim is made about which of Samuel or Chronicles has the earlier wording, or whether two men named Goliath or two names for one man are meant. The Greek agrees with the Hebrew against the KJV, so both H and G apply. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text, with the consonants-only Aleppo-based edition as a second witness. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined, and the KJV translators’ source for their wording is not established here. English glosses in brackets are the researcher’s plain renderings, not lexicon entries. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint.",
