    "title": "Michal’s five sons: “brought up”, or “bare”?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "The KJV says Michal “brought up” five sons for Adriel. The Hebrew and the Greek say she bore them. Elsewhere it is Merab who was given to Adriel, and Michal “had no child unto the day of her death”.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Samuel 21:8: “the five sons of Michal the daughter of Saul, whom she brought up for Adriel the son of Barzillai the Meholathite”. Hebrew (WLC): חֲמֵ֗שֶׁת בְּנֵי֙ מִיכַ֣ל בַּת־שָׁא֔וּל אֲשֶׁ֥ר יָלְדָ֛ה לְעַדְרִיאֵ֥ל [the five sons of Michal daughter of Saul whom she bore to Adriel]. Aleppo-based edition (HAC): חמשת בני מיכל בת שאול אשר ילדה לעדריאל. The same verb earlier in the verse, אֲשֶׁ֣ר יָלְדָ֣ה לְשָׁא֔וּל, is rendered by the KJV “whom she bare unto Saul”. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): πέντε υἱοὺς τῆς Μιχὸλ θυγατρὸς Σαοὺλ, οὓς ἔτεκε τῷ Ἐσδριὴλ [the five sons of Michol daughter of Saul whom she bore to Esdriel]. KJV 1 Samuel 18:19: “she was given unto Adriel the Meholathite to wife”, said of Merab. KJV 2 Samuel 6:23: “Therefore Michal the daughter of Saul had no child unto the day of her death.” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/21/, https://bolls.life/get-text/HAC/10/21/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2SA21.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew verb is the ordinary word for giving birth, and the KJV translates it “bare” earlier in the same verse. “Brought up” is a different act. With the Hebrew wording, 2 Samuel 21:8 says Michal bore five sons to Adriel, against 1 Samuel 18:19 (Adriel married Merab) and 2 Samuel 6:23 (Michal had no child).",
    "scope": "The KJV wording removes the conflict with 2 Samuel 6:23 but still names Michal rather than Merab. No claim is made about whether “Michal” in the Hebrew is a copying slip for “Merab”; the named Hebrew and Greek editions both print Michal. The Greek agrees with the Hebrew against the KJV, so both H and G apply. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text, with the consonants-only Aleppo-based edition as a second witness. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined, and the KJV translators’ source for their wording is not established here. English glosses in brackets are the researcher’s plain renderings, not lexicon entries. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint.",
