    "title": "Hannah’s offering: three bullocks, or one calf three years old?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says Hannah went up “with three bullocks” and then “they slew a bullock”. The Septuagint says she went up with one calf of three years.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Samuel 1:24: “with three bullocks, and one ephah of flour, and a bottle of wine”; 1:25: “And they slew a bullock”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:24: בְּפָרִ֤ים שְׁלֹשָׁה֙ [with bulls, three]; 1:25: וַֽיִּשְׁחֲט֖וּ אֶת־הַפָּ֑ר [and they slaughtered the bull]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 1:24: ἐν μόσχῳ τριετίζοντι [with a three-year-old calf]; 1:25: ἔσφαξε τὸν μόσχον [and he slaughtered the calf]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/9/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1SA01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and Hebrew count three animals in verse 24 and one in verse 25. The Greek has one animal in both verses, three years old.",
    "scope": "Killing one of three bullocks is possible, so the KJV’s two verses do not conflict with each other; the difference recorded is the number of animals in the Greek against the KJV. The Greek verse also adds loaves and is longer in 1:25; those additions are not the finding. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient translation tradition, and the Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees, and neither edition settles which reading is earlier. English glosses in brackets are the researcher’s plain renderings, not lexicon entries.",
