    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV David becomes Saul’s armourbearer and Saul sends to Jesse by name (16:21–22); yet after the fight Saul asks Abner whose son David is (17:55–58). Brenton’s Greek Septuagint does not contain 17:55–58.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Samuel 16:21: “he loved him greatly; and he became his armourbearer”; 16:22: “And Saul sent to Jesse, saying, Let David, I pray thee, stand before me”. KJV 17:55: “Abner, whose son is this youth? And Abner said, As thy soul liveth, O king, I cannot tell.” KJV 17:58: “Whose son art thou, thou young man?” Hebrew (WLC) 16:21: וַֽיְהִי־לֹ֖ו נֹשֵׂ֥א כֵלִֽים [and he became his bearer of gear]; 17:55: בֶּן־מִי־זֶ֥ה הַנַּ֖עַר [whose son is this, the youth]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): 16:21 has ἐγενήθη αὐτῷ αἴρων τὰ σκεύη αὐτοῦ [he became his bearer of gear]; the page for chapter 17 prints verses 1–11, 32–40, 42–49 and 51–54 and no others, and the page for chapter 18 begins at verse 6. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/9/16/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/9/17/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1SA16.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1SA17.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1SA18.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV, Saul previously knows David and sends to Jesse by name; after the battle Saul asks whose son David is, and Abner says he cannot tell. The earlier verses do not establish that Abner knew David’s father, and Saul’s question need not prove he had forgotten. In the named Greek edition the later questions are absent, together with 17:12–31, 17:41, 17:50 and 18:1–5.",
