    "title": "“It repenteth me” and “he is not a man, that he should repent”",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "In one chapter the LORD says “It repenteth me that I have set up Saul”, Samuel says God “will not … repent: for he is not a man, that he should repent”, and the narrator says “the LORD repented that he had made Saul king”.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Samuel 15:11: “It repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king”. KJV 15:29: “the Strength of Israel will not lie nor repent: for he is not a man, that he should repent”. KJV 15:35: “the Lord repented that he had made Saul king over Israel”. Hebrew (WLC) 15:11: נִחַ֗מְתִּי; 15:29: לֹ֥א יְשַׁקֵּ֖ר וְלֹ֣א יִנָּחֵ֑ם כִּ֣י לֹ֥א אָדָ֛ם ה֖וּא לְהִנָּחֵֽם; 15:35: וַיהוָ֣ה נִחָ֔ם. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/9/15/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV uses “repent” in all three verses, and the Hebrew uses forms of one verb in all three. Verses 11 and 35 say the LORD did it about making Saul king; verse 29 says he does not do it.",
    "scope": "Verse 29 is Samuel’s speech about the decision to remove Saul; verses 11 and 35 are about the earlier decision to appoint him. A shared Hebrew root does not settle context, and the verb can cover regret, sorrow or a change of course. The record shows the wording contrast and does not decide whether the senses differ. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text; a digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees.",
