    "title": "The shewbread priest: Ahimelech, or Abiathar?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "1 Samuel says David came to Ahimelech the priest for the bread, and that Abiathar was Ahimelech’s son. Mark 2:26 says it happened “in the days of Abiathar the high priest”.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Samuel 21:1: “Then came David to Nob to Ahimelech the priest”. KJV 22:20: “one of the sons of Ahimelech the son of Ahitub, named Abiathar, escaped”. KJV Mark 2:26: “How he went into the house of God in the days of Abiathar the high priest, and did eat the shewbread”. Hebrew (WLC), where the verse is numbered 21:2: וַיָּבֹ֤א דָוִד֙ נֹ֔בֶה אֶל־אֲחִימֶ֖לֶךְ הַכֹּהֵ֑ן [and David came to Nob, to Ahimelech the priest]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus, printed without accents in this edition) Mark 2:26: επι αβιαθαρ του αρχιερεως [in the time of Abiathar the high priest]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/9/21/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/2/.",
    "reasoning": "The priest who gives David the bread in 1 Samuel is Ahimelech; Mark names Abiathar, who in 1 Samuel is that priest’s son and escapes afterwards.",
    "scope": "The KJV matches the Greek of Mark here, so no G tag applies; this is a difference between Mark and 1 Samuel. Mark’s phrase could be read loosely as “in the lifetime of Abiathar, who was later high priest”, but 1 Samuel gives Ahimelech as the priest in office. Hebrew and Greek verse numbers in 1 Samuel 21 run one ahead of the KJV. The texts quoted are the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text and a digital Textus Receptus; a digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees.",
