    "id": "david-sons-and-ira-chief-rulers-or-priests",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "2 Samuel 20:26",
    "appliesTo": [
      "2 Samuel 20:26"
    ],
    "title": "Ira: “a chief ruler”, or priest?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV calls Ira “a chief ruler about David”; Brenton’s Greek calls him a priest. The Hebrew noun is also translated “priests” elsewhere, but its meaning in these court lists remains a contextual question.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Samuel 8:17: “Zadok the son of Ahitub, and Ahimelech the son of Abiathar, were the priests”. KJV 8:18: “and David's sons were chief rulers”. Hebrew (WLC) 8:17: כֹּהֲנִ֑ים [priests]; 8:18: וּבְנֵ֥י דָוִ֖ד כֹּהֲנִ֥ים הָיֽוּ [and the sons of David were priests]. Aleppo-based edition (HAC) 8:18: ובני דוד כהנים היו. KJV 20:25: “Zadok and Abiathar were the priests”. KJV 20:26: “And Ira also the Jairite was a chief ruler about David.” Hebrew (WLC) 20:25: כֹּהֲנִֽים; 20:26: הָיָ֥ה כֹהֵ֖ן לְדָוִֽד [was a priest to David]. HAC 20:26: היה כהן לדוד. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 8:18: καὶ οἱ υἱοὶ Δαυὶδ αὐλάρχαι ἦσαν [and the sons of David were chiefs of the court]; 20:25: ἱερεῖς [priests]; 20:26: ἦν ἱερεὺς τῷ Δαυίδ [was priest to David]. KJV 1 Chronicles 18:17: “and the sons of David were chief about the king”. Hebrew (WLC) 1 Chronicles 18:17: וּבְנֵי־דָוִ֥יד הָרִאשֹׁנִ֖ים לְיַ֥ד הַמֶּֽלֶךְ [and the sons of David were the first at the king’s side]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/8/, https://bolls.life/get-text/HAC/10/8/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/20/, https://bolls.life/get-text/HAC/10/20/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2SA08.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2SA20.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/13/18/.",
    "reasoning": "Brenton’s Greek 20:26 uses the priest title where the KJV says chief ruler. The Hebrew noun occurs in adjoining verses translated priests, but that repetition alone does not establish identical meaning in different offices or prove the KJV mistranslates the court title. Chronicles and Brenton’s Greek 8:18 use court titles for David’s sons.",
    "scope": "G applies only at 20:26. No H tag is retained: the supplied Hebrew passages demonstrate the shared noun, but do not settle its contextual sense in the court lists. The bracketed Hebrew priest glosses are provisional. At 8:18 Brenton’s Greek has a court title, not priests, so this record is not placed there. No claim is made about what duties David’s sons or Ira performed, or about how their office relates to the priesthood of Aaron’s line. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text, with the consonants-only Aleppo-based edition as a second witness. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
