    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "The same pursuit starts “from Geba” in 2 Samuel and “from Gibeon” in 1 Chronicles. The Greek of 2 Samuel has Gabaon, the form it also has in Chronicles.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Samuel 5:25: “smote the Philistines from Geba until thou come to Gazer”. Hebrew (WLC): מִגֶּ֖בַע [from Geba]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 5:25: ἀπὸ Γαβαὼν ἕως τῆς γῆς Γαζηρά [from Gabaon as far as the land of Gazera]. KJV 1 Chronicles 14:16: “smote the host of the Philistines from Gibeon even to Gazer”. Hebrew (WLC) 1 Chronicles 14:16: מִגִּבְעֹ֖ון [from Gibeon]. Brenton Greek 1 Chronicles 14:16: ἀπὸ Γαβαὼν ἕως Γαζηρά. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/5/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2SA05.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/13/14/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1CH14.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both verses close the same battle with the same end point, Gazer. The starting point is Geba in the KJV and Hebrew of Samuel and Gibeon in the KJV and Hebrew of Chronicles. Brenton’s Greek prints the same name, Gabaon, in both books, so in Samuel it differs from the KJV.",
    "scope": "The record compares names as printed. It does not argue where either town was, and it does not rely on the two names sharing letters. No claim is made about which name is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
