    "title": "David’s anger at Amnon: the Greek adds that he did not grieve him",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says only that David “was very wroth” when he heard what Amnon had done to Tamar. The Greek goes on: he did not grieve Amnon, because he loved him as his firstborn.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Samuel 13:21: “But when king David heard of all these things, he was very wroth.” Hebrew (WLC), end of the verse: וַיִּ֥חַר לֹ֖ו מְאֹֽד [and he was very angry]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 13:21: καὶ ἐθυμώθη σφόδρα, καὶ οὐκ ἐλύπησε τὸ πνεῦμα Ἀμνὼν τοῦ υἱοῦ αὐτοῦ, ὅτι ἠγάπα αὐτὸν, ὅτι πρωτότοκος αὐτοῦ ἦν [and he was very angry, and he did not grieve the spirit of Amnon his son, because he loved him, because he was his firstborn]. The KJV verses on either side: 13:20 “So Tamar remained desolate in her brother Absalom's house”; 13:22 “And Absalom spake unto his brother Amnon neither good nor bad”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/13/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2SA13.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew end the verse with David’s anger. Brenton’s Greek continues with a statement about what David did not do and why. The KJV verses before and after speak of Tamar and of Absalom, not of David, so nothing in the nearby KJV text states what the Greek adds.",
    "scope": "The Greek does not contradict the KJV; it says more. The comparison is with KJV 13:20–22, and the packaged KJV of chapter 13 was searched for “firstborn” and “grieve”, which do not occur in it. No claim is made about whether the longer text is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
