    "title": "After the dedication: the people sent away on the eighth day, or the twenty-third?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "1 Kings has a feast of “fourteen days” and then sends the people away “on the eighth day”. 2 Chronicles holds an assembly on the eighth day and sends them away on the twenty-third of the month. The Greek of 1 Kings has seven days, not fourteen.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 8:65: “before the Lord our God, seven days and seven days, even fourteen days”. KJV 8:66: “On the eighth day he sent the people away”. Hebrew (WLC) 8:65: שִׁבְעַ֥ת יָמִ֖ים וְשִׁבְעַ֣ת יָמִ֑ים אַרְבָּעָ֥ה עָשָׂ֖ר יֹֽום [seven days and seven days, fourteen days]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 8:65, end of the verse: εὐφραινόμενος ἐνώπιον Κυρίου Θεοῦ ἡμῶν ἑπτὰ ἡμέρας. [rejoicing before the Lord our God seven days]. Brenton Greek 8:66: Καὶ ἐν τῇ ἡμέρᾳ τῇ ὀγδόῃ ἐξαπέστειλε τὸν λαόν [and on the eighth day he sent the people away]. KJV 2 Chronicles 7:9: “And in the eighth day they made a solemn assembly: for they kept the dedication of the altar seven days, and the feast seven days.” KJV 2 Chronicles 7:10: “And on the three and twentieth day of the seventh month he sent the people away into their tents”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 7:10: וּבְיֹ֨ום עֶשְׂרִ֤ים וּשְׁלֹשָׁה֙ לַחֹ֣דֶשׁ הַשְּׁבִיעִ֔י שִׁלַּ֥ח אֶת־הָעָ֖ם [and on the twenty-third day of the seventh month he sent the people away]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/8/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI08.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/7/.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV of Kings a fourteen-day feast is followed by a dismissal on the eighth day. In Chronicles the eighth day is an assembly and the dismissal is dated later. Brenton’s Greek of Kings has a single seven days, so it differs from the KJV in the length of the feast.",
    "scope": "G applies at 1 Kings 8:65 only. In Kings “the eighth day” may be counted from the second seven days; a dismissal announced on the twenty-second and a departure on the twenty-third would then fit both books. Neither book explains its count this way. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
