    "title": "The cities: Solomon gave them to Hiram, or Huram gave them to Solomon?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In 1 Kings Solomon gives Hiram twenty cities. In the Hebrew and the Greek of 2 Chronicles Huram gives cities to Solomon. The KJV of Chronicles says Huram “had restored” them.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 9:11: “king Solomon gave Hiram twenty cities in the land of Galilee”. KJV 9:12: “the cities which Solomon had given him; and they pleased him not”. Hebrew (WLC) 9:12: אֲשֶׁ֥ר נָתַן־לֹ֖ו שְׁלֹמֹ֑ה [which Solomon gave him]. KJV 2 Chronicles 8:2: “That the cities which Huram had restored to Solomon, Solomon built them”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 8:2: וְהֶעָרִ֗ים אֲשֶׁ֨ר נָתַ֤ן חוּרָם֙ לִשְׁלֹמֹ֔ה [and the cities which Huram gave to Solomon]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2 Chronicles 8:2: καὶ τὰς πόλεις ἃς ἔδωκε Χιρὰμ τῷ Σαλωμὼν [and the cities which Chiram gave to Solomon]. Brenton Greek 1 Kings 9:12: τὰς πόλεις ἃς ἔδωκεν αὐτῷ Σαλωμών [the cities which Solomon gave him]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/9/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/8/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH08.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI09.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew uses the same verb in the same form in both verses, and the Greek uses one verb for both. The KJV translates it “had given” in Kings and “had restored” in Chronicles. Restoring is giving back; the Hebrew and Greek of Chronicles say only that Huram gave. With the Hebrew wording, the gift runs from Solomon to Hiram in Kings and from Huram to Solomon in Chronicles.",
    "scope": "H and G apply at 2 Chronicles 8:2 only; the Greek agrees with the Hebrew against the KJV. The Hebrew sentence in Chronicles has no word for “back” or “again”. Chronicles does not say its cities are the twenty of Kings. Hiram disliked the twenty (KJV 9:12), and a return of them would fit both books; neither book reports one. The record does not call the two Hebrew statements incompatible. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
