    "summary": "The KJV makes Rehoboam forty-one when he became king, with a reign of seventeen years. The Greek says the same at 14:21, but in a passage the KJV does not have it makes him sixteen, with a reign of twelve years.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 14:21: “Rehoboam was forty and one years old when he began to reign, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem”. Hebrew (WLC): בֶּן־אַרְבָּעִ֣ים וְאַחַ֣ת שָׁנָה֩ רְחַבְעָ֨ם בְּמָלְכֹ֜ו וּֽשֲׁבַ֨ע עֶשְׂרֵ֥ה שָׁנָ֣ה [forty-one years old was Rehoboam when he began to reign, and seventeen years]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 14:21: υἱὸς τεσσαράκοντα καὶ ἑνὸς ἐνιαυτῶν Ῥοβοὰμ ἐν τῷ βασιλεύειν αὐτόν· καὶ ἑπτακαίδεκα ἔτη ἐβασίλευσεν ἐν Ἱερουσαλὴμ [forty-one years old, seventeen years]. Brenton Greek, verse printed as 12:24a: υἱὸς ὢν ἑκκαίδεκα ἐτῶν ἐν τῷ βασιλεύειν αὐτὸν, καὶ δώδεκα ἔτη ἐβασίλευσεν ἐν Ἱερουσαλήμ. Brenton’s English translation of 12:24a: “being sixteen years old when he began to reign, and he reigned twelve years in Jerusalem”. KJV 1 Kings 12:24 ends: “and returned to depart, according to the word of the Lord.” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/14/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI14.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI12.htm and https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/1KI12.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Brenton’s Greek prints a long passage after 12:24, lettered 24a to 24z, which the KJV and the Hebrew do not have. Its first verse gives Rehoboam’s age and reign, and both figures differ from 14:21: sixteen against forty-one, and twelve years against seventeen.",
    "scope": "G only, for a statement in the Greek that the KJV nowhere has. Brenton’s Greek contains both sets of figures, so it also differs from itself; at 14:21 it agrees with the KJV. The rest of the lettered passage was not compared. No claim is made about which figures are original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
