    "title": "David’s obedience: “save only in the matter of Uriah”, or no exception?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV and the Hebrew say David obeyed in everything all his life, “save only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite”. The Greek ends the sentence without the exception.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 15:5: “and turned not aside from any thing that he commanded him all the days of his life, save only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite”. Hebrew (WLC): כֹּ֚ל יְמֵ֣י חַיָּ֔יו רַ֕ק בִּדְבַ֖ר אוּרִיָּ֥ה הַחִתִּֽי [all the days of his life, only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 15:5, end of the verse: οὐκ ἐξέκλινεν ἀπὸ πάντων ὧν ἐνετείλατο αὐτῷ πάσας τὰς ἡμέρας τῆς ζωῆς αὐτοῦ. [he did not turn aside from all that he commanded him all the days of his life]. The Brenton Greek page prints verse 5 and then verse 7; it has no verse 6. Fetched from https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI15.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/15/.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV and Hebrew the praise of David carries one stated exception. Brenton’s Greek has the praise and stops at “all the days of his life”, so it states David’s obedience without any exception.",
    "scope": "G only. The record compares the wording of this verse and does not set it against the accounts of David’s life elsewhere. No claim is made about which form is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
