    "title": "Abijam’s mother: Maachah daughter of Abishalom, or Michaiah daughter of Uriel?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "1 Kings names the king’s mother Maachah, daughter of Abishalom. 2 Chronicles 13 names her Michaiah, daughter of Uriel of Gibeah. The Greek of that Chronicles verse has Maacha.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 15:2: “his mother's name was Maachah, the daughter of Abishalom”. Hebrew (WLC): וְשֵׁ֣ם אִמֹּ֔ו מַעֲכָ֖ה בַּת־אֲבִישָׁלֹֽום [and his mother’s name was Maachah daughter of Abishalom]. KJV 2 Chronicles 13:2: “His mother's name also was Michaiah the daughter of Uriel of Gibeah.” Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 13:2: וְשֵׁ֣ם אִמֹּ֔ו מִיכָיָ֥הוּ בַת־אוּרִיאֵ֖ל מִן־גִּבְעָ֑ה [and his mother’s name was Michaiah daughter of Uriel of Gibeah]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2 Chronicles 13:2: καὶ ὄνομα τῇ μητρὶ αὐτοῦ Μααχὰ, θυγάτηρ Οὐριὴλ ἀπὸ Γαβαών [and his mother’s name was Maacha, daughter of Uriel of Gabaon]. KJV 2 Chronicles 11:20: “Maachah the daughter of Absalom; which bare him Abijah”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/15/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/13/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH13.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The two verses open the same reign with the same three-year length and name the king’s mother differently, in her own name and in her father’s. Brenton’s Greek of 2 Chronicles 13:2 has the name Maacha with the father Uriel, so it differs from the KJV of that verse in her name.",
    "scope": "G applies at 2 Chronicles 13:2 only. The king is Abijam in Kings and Abijah in Chronicles; the record does not rely on that. If “daughter” in one verse means a granddaughter, a woman could be linked to both men; neither book says so, and that would not account for the two personal names. 2 Chronicles 11:20 is quoted from the KJV only. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
