    "title": "Asa’s mother: Maachah, the same as his father’s mother, or Ana?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV gives Abijam and his son Asa a mother with the same name and the same father: Maachah, daughter of Abishalom. The Greek names Asa’s mother Ana.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 15:2, of Abijam: “his mother's name was Maachah, the daughter of Abishalom”. KJV 15:8: “and Asa his son reigned in his stead”. KJV 15:10, of Asa: “his mother's name was Maachah, the daughter of Abishalom”. KJV 15:13: “And also Maachah his mother, even her he removed from being queen”. Hebrew (WLC) 15:10: וְשֵׁ֣ם אִמֹּ֔ו מַעֲכָ֖ה בַּת־אֲבִישָׁלֹֽום. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 15:2: καὶ ὄνομα τῆς μητρὸς αὐτοῦ Μααχὰ, θυγάτηρ Ἀβεσσαλώμ [and his mother’s name was Maacha, daughter of Abessalom]. Brenton Greek 15:10: καὶ ὄνομα τῆς μητρὸς αὐτοῦ Ἀνὰ, θυγάτηρ Ἀβεσσαλώμ [and his mother’s name was Ana, daughter of Abessalom]. Brenton Greek 15:13: Καὶ τὴν Ἀνὰ τὴν μητέρα ἑαυτοῦ [and Ana his own mother]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/15/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI15.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV and Hebrew the father’s mother and the son’s mother are described in identical words eight verses apart. Brenton’s Greek keeps the father Abessalom but has a different woman’s name for Asa’s mother, at 15:10 and again at 15:13.",
    "scope": "G only, at 15:10 and 15:13. The KJV wording would hold if “mother” at 15:10 means grandmother; the verse does not say so, and the record does not call the KJV statements impossible. No claim is made about which name is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
