    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "1 Kings says that under Asa “the high places were not removed”. 2 Chronicles 14 says twice that he took them away, from all the cities of Judah. 2 Chronicles 15 says they were not taken away out of Israel.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 15:14: “But the high places were not removed: nevertheless Asa's heart was perfect with the Lord all his days.” Hebrew (WLC): וְהַבָּמֹ֖ות לֹא־סָ֑רוּ [and the high places were not removed]. KJV 2 Chronicles 14:3: “For he took away the altars of the strange gods, and the high places”. KJV 2 Chronicles 14:5: “Also he took away out of all the cities of Judah the high places and the images”. Hebrew (WLC), where these verses are numbered 2 Chronicles 14:2 and 14:4: וַיָּ֛סַר אֶת־מִזְבְּחֹ֥ות הַנֵּכָ֖ר וְהַבָּמֹ֑ות [and he removed the foreign altars and the high places]; וַיָּ֙סַר֙ מִכָּל־עָרֵ֣י יְהוּדָ֔ה אֶת־הַבָּמֹ֖ות [and he removed from all the cities of Judah the high places]. KJV 2 Chronicles 15:17: “But the high places were not taken away out of Israel”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 15:17: וְהַ֨בָּמֹ֔ות לֹא־סָ֖רוּ מִיִּשְׂרָאֵ֑ל [and the high places were not removed from Israel]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/15/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/14/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/15/.",
    "reasoning": "Kings sums up Asa’s reign by saying the high places stayed. Chronicles 14 says he removed them and names all the cities of Judah. The noun is the same in each Hebrew verse, and the verb is from the same root, once denied and twice affirmed.",
    "scope": "Chronicles itself has both statements. Its second adds “out of Israel”, which may mean the northern kingdom, or a removal early in the reign may have been followed by a return; neither book says either. The Kings verse has no such qualifier. Brenton’s Greek of 1 Kings 15:14 also says the high places were not removed, so no tag is proposed. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
