    "title": "Omri: twelve years from Asa’s thirty-first year, yet his son succeeds in the thirty-eighth?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Omri begins to reign in Asa’s thirty-first year and reigns twelve years; his son Ahab succeeds him in Asa’s thirty-eighth year, seven years later. The Greek dates Ahab by a different king: the second year of Jehoshaphat.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 16:15: “In the twenty and seventh year of Asa king of Judah did Zimri reign seven days in Tirzah.” KJV 16:16: “wherefore all Israel made Omri, the captain of the host, king over Israel that day in the camp”. KJV 16:23: “In the thirty and first year of Asa king of Judah began Omri to reign over Israel, twelve years”. Hebrew (WLC) 16:23: בִּשְׁנַת֩ שְׁלֹשִׁ֨ים וְאַחַ֜ת שָׁנָ֗ה לְאָסָא֙ מֶ֣לֶךְ יְהוּדָ֔ה מָלַ֤ךְ עָמְרִי֙ עַל־יִשְׂרָאֵ֔ל שְׁתֵּ֥ים עֶשְׂרֵ֖ה שָׁנָ֑ה [in the thirty-first year of Asa king of Judah Omri reigned over Israel, twelve years]. KJV 16:29: “And in the thirty and eighth year of Asa king of Judah began Ahab the son of Omri to reign over Israel”. Hebrew (WLC) 16:29: בִּשְׁנַ֨ת שְׁלֹשִׁ֤ים וּשְׁמֹנֶה֙ שָׁנָ֔ה לְאָסָ֖א מֶ֣לֶךְ יְהוּדָ֑ה [in the thirty-eighth year of Asa king of Judah]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 16:29: Ἐν ἔτει δευτέρῳ τοῦ Ἰωσαφὰτ βασιλέως Ἰούδα, Ἀχαὰβ υἱὸς Ἀμβρὶ ἐβασίλευσεν ἐπὶ Ἰσραὴλ [in the second year of Josaphat king of Juda, Achaab son of Ambri reigned over Israel]. Brenton Greek, verse printed as 16:28a: Καὶ ἐν τῷ ἐνιαυτῷ τῷ ἑνδεκάτῳ ἔτει τοῦ Ἀμβρὶ βασιλεύει Ἰωσαφὰτ υἱὸς Ἀσὰ [and in the eleventh year of Ambri, Josaphat son of Asa begins to reign]. KJV 22:41: “And Jehoshaphat the son of Asa began to reign over Judah in the fourth year of Ahab king of Israel.” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/16/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI16.htm.",
    "reasoning": "From the thirty-first year of Asa to the thirty-eighth is seven years, not twelve. Twelve years ending in the thirty-eighth would begin about the twenty-seventh, when verse 16 has the army make Omri king; but verse 23 dates his reign from the thirty-first. Brenton’s Greek dates Ahab’s accession after Asa’s death, in his son’s second year, and has Jehoshaphat begin in Omri’s eleventh year, where the KJV has Jehoshaphat begin in Ahab’s fourth.",
    "scope": "G applies at 1 Kings 16:29 only. Verses 21–22 report a contest between Omri and Tibni. If the twelve years are counted from the army’s choice and the thirty-first year marks Omri’s rule without a rival, the figures fit; verse 23 does not say so. The record does not argue about counting part-years. No claim is made about which dating is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
