    "title": "The wrecked ships: Jehoshaphat refused Ahaziah, or joined with him?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In 1 Kings Jehoshaphat builds the ships, and when Ahaziah asks to send his men in them, Jehoshaphat refuses. In 2 Chronicles Jehoshaphat joins with Ahaziah to build the ships, and they are wrecked because of that partnership.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 22:48: “Jehoshaphat made ships of Tharshish to go to Ophir for gold: but they went not; for the ships were broken at Eziongeber.” KJV 22:49: “Then said Ahaziah the son of Ahab unto Jehoshaphat, Let my servants go with thy servants in the ships. But Jehoshaphat would not.” Hebrew (WLC), where the verse is numbered 22:50: וְלֹ֥א אָבָ֖ה יְהֹושָׁפָֽט [and Jehoshaphat was not willing]. KJV 2 Chronicles 20:35: “And after this did Jehoshaphat king of Judah join himself with Ahaziah king of Israel”. KJV 2 Chronicles 20:36: “And he joined himself with him to make ships to go to Tarshish: and they made the ships in Eziongaber.” KJV 2 Chronicles 20:37: “Because thou hast joined thyself with Ahaziah, the Lord hath broken thy works. And the ships were broken”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 20:36: וַיְחַבְּרֵ֣הוּ עִמֹּ֔ו לַעֲשֹׂ֥ות אֳנִיֹּ֖ות [and he joined him with himself to make ships]. The Brenton Greek page for 1 Kings 22 prints verses 1–46 and 51–54; it has no verses 47–50. Fetched from https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI22.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/22/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/20/.",
    "reasoning": "Both books report ships built at Eziongeber and broken before they sailed. Kings names Jehoshaphat alone as the builder and records only a refusal of Ahaziah. Chronicles makes the two kings partners in the building and gives the partnership as the reason for the wreck.",
    "scope": "Kings places Ahaziah’s request after the wreck (“Then”). A partnership before the wreck and a refusal of a second venture after it would fit both books; Kings does not mention a partnership and Chronicles does not mention a refusal. The destinations are worded differently in the two books; the record does not rely on that. Brenton’s Greek of 1 Kings 22 has no verses here to compare, so no tag is proposed. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
