    "title": "At the pool of Samaria: “they washed his armour”, or the harlots washed?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "After Ahab’s death the KJV says “they washed his armour”. The Hebrew says the harlots washed, and the Greek says the harlots washed themselves in the blood.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 22:38: “and the dogs licked up his blood; and they washed his armour; according unto the word of the Lord which he spake”. Hebrew (WLC): וַיָּלֹ֤קּוּ הַכְּלָבִים֙ אֶת־דָּמֹ֔ו וְהַזֹּנֹ֖ות רָחָ֑צוּ [and the dogs licked his blood, and the harlots washed]. The same noun earlier in the book, 3:16: שְׁתַּ֛יִם נָשִׁ֥ים זֹנֹ֖ות, KJV “two women, that were harlots”. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 22:38: καὶ ἐξέλιξαν αἱ ὗες καὶ οἱ κύνες τὸ αἷμα, καὶ αἱ πόρναι ἐλούσαντο ἐν τῷ αἵματι. Brenton’s English translation of the same: “and the swine and the dogs licked up the blood, and the harlots washed themselves in the blood”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/22/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/3/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI22.htm and https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/1KI22.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew clause has two words: a plural noun with the article, and the verb “washed”. The KJV translates that noun “harlots” at 3:16. In the KJV of 22:38 there are no harlots and the thing washed is armour; in the Hebrew there is no word for armour and the harlots are the ones who wash. Brenton’s Greek has the harlots too, and adds swine beside the dogs.",
    "scope": "H and G both apply: the Greek agrees with the Hebrew against the KJV. The Greek goes further than the Hebrew in two places, the swine and “in the blood”; those are G only. The Hebrew does not say what or where the harlots washed. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
