    "title": "Elijah’s drought: rain promised “in the third year”, or no rain for three years and six months?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "1 Kings has the word that ends the drought come to Elijah “in the third year”. Luke and James say the drought lasted three years and six months.",
    "text": "KJV 1 Kings 17:1: “there shall not be dew nor rain these years, but according to my word”. KJV 18:1: “And it came to pass after many days, that the word of the Lord came to Elijah in the third year, saying, Go, shew thyself unto Ahab; and I will send rain upon the earth.” Hebrew (WLC) 18:1: בַּשָּׁנָ֥ה הַשְּׁלִישִׁ֖ית [in the third year]. KJV Luke 4:25: “when the heaven was shut up three years and six months”. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus) Luke 4:25: επι ετη τρια και μηνας εξ [for three years and six months]. KJV James 5:17: “and it rained not on the earth by the space of three years and six months”. Textus Receptus James 5:17: ενιαυτους τρεις και μηνας εξ [three years and six months]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/11/18/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/42/4/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/59/5/.",
    "reasoning": "If the “third year” is the third year of the drought, the drought lasted less than three years. Luke and James each give three years and six months, in Greek words that match the KJV.",
    "scope": "Kings does not say from what point its “third year” is counted. If it is counted from Elijah’s stay at Zarephath or any point later than the last rain, both figures can stand; the book does not say so, and the record does not call the statements incompatible. The KJV matches the Hebrew of Kings and the Greek of Luke and James, so no tag is proposed. The Greek quoted is the Textus Receptus digital text, which prints no accents; other Greek New Testament editions were not compared. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
