    "id": "elijah-went-up-into-heaven-or-no-man-ascended",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "2 Kings 2:11",
    "appliesTo": [
      "2 Kings 2:11"
    ],
    "title": "Elijah “went up … into heaven”, or “no man hath ascended up to heaven”?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "2 Kings says Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven. John says no man has ascended up to heaven except the Son of man. The Greek of 2 Kings says Elijah was taken up “as it were” into heaven.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 2:1: “when the Lord would take up Elijah into heaven by a whirlwind”. KJV 2:11: “and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:11: וַיַּ֙עַל֙ אֵ֣לִיָּ֔הוּ בַּֽסְּעָרָ֖ה הַשָּׁמָֽיִם [and Elijah went up in the whirlwind to the heavens]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2:11: καὶ ἀνελήμφθη Ἠλιοὺ ἐν συσσεισμῷ ὡς εἰς τὸν οὐρανόν [and Eliu was taken up in a whirlwind as it were into heaven]. Brenton Greek 2:1: ὡς εἰς τὸν οὐρανὸν [as it were into heaven]. KJV John 3:13: “And no man hath ascended up to heaven, but he that came down from heaven, even the Son of man”. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus) John 3:13: και ουδεις αναβεβηκεν εις τον ουρανον ει μη ο εκ του ουρανου καταβας [and no one has gone up into heaven except the one who came down out of heaven]. Tischendorf’s edition has the same words: καὶ οὐδεὶς ἀναβέβηκεν εἰς τὸν οὐρανὸν εἰ μὴ ὁ ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ καταβάς. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/2/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI02.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/43/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/43/3/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew of 2 Kings say that Elijah went up into heaven. John has Jesus say that no man has gone up to heaven but the one who came down from it. As worded, the second statement leaves no room for the first. Brenton’s Greek of 2 Kings twice has a word the KJV lacks, “as it were” into heaven, in the verse itself and in the chapter’s opening line.",
    "scope": "G applies at 2 Kings 2:11 only: the Septuagint Greek there qualifies “into heaven” and the KJV does not. In John the KJV matches the Greek New Testament. The two KJV statements can both stand if “heaven” in Kings means the sky and in John the dwelling of God, or if John speaks only of one who went up and came back to report; neither book says so. The record does not decide why the Greek has “as it were”. The Textus Receptus digital text prints no accents; Tischendorf’s edition was also compared, and no other Greek New Testament edition was. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
