    "title": "War on Moab: “fell every good tree”, or “thou shalt not destroy the trees”?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Deuteronomy forbids destroying a besieged city’s food trees. In 2 Kings Elisha, speaking in the Lord’s name, tells Israel to fell every good tree in Moab, and they do.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 20:19: “When thou shalt besiege a city a long time, in making war against it to take it, thou shalt not destroy the trees thereof by forcing an axe against them: for thou mayest eat of them, and thou shalt not cut them down”. Hebrew (WLC) Deuteronomy 20:19: לֹֽא־תַשְׁחִ֤ית אֶת־עֵצָהּ֙ [you shall not destroy its trees]. KJV 20:20: “Only the trees which thou knowest that they be not trees for meat, thou shalt destroy and cut them down”. KJV 2 Kings 3:16: “And he said, Thus saith the Lord”. KJV 3:18: “he will deliver the Moabites also into your hand”. KJV 3:19: “And ye shall smite every fenced city, and every choice city, and shall fell every good tree”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:19: וְכָל־עֵ֥ץ טֹוב֙ תַּפִּ֔ילוּ [and every good tree you shall fell]. KJV 3:25: “and felled all the good trees”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/20/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/3/.",
    "reasoning": "The law allows only trees that are not for food to be cut down in a siege. Elisha’s word, which opens “Thus saith the Lord”, tells the army attacking Moab’s cities to fell every good tree, with no exception, and verse 25 reports that they felled all the good trees. Because the act is commanded in the story, this is recorded as a contrast between two commands and not as a law broken.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew in both passages, and no V is claimed. The Hebrew verbs differ in the two passages (“destroy” and “cut down” in Deuteronomy, “fell” in Kings); the comparison rests on the act, not on a shared word. Deuteronomy speaks of the trees of a city under a long siege; Kings does not say whether the “good” trees of Moab were food trees or stood by besieged cities, and if they were neither, the two commands do not meet. Kings does not mention the law. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
