    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew, and Brenton’s Greek has the same figures. The two dates fit if one counts the year in which a king comes to the throne and the other does not; the book does not say that it changes its way of counting. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
