    "title": "Jehu’s victims: forty-two “brethren of Ahaziah”, or sons of his brethren — after all his elder brothers were slain?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "2 Chronicles says raiders killed all Ahaziah’s elder brothers before he reigned, and that Jehu killed “the sons of the brethren of Ahaziah”. 2 Kings says Jehu killed forty-two men who called themselves “the brethren of Ahaziah”. The Greek of Chronicles has “the brethren”.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Chronicles 21:17: “so that there was never a son left him, save Jehoahaz, the youngest of his sons”. KJV 22:1: “made Ahaziah his youngest son king in his stead: for the band of men that came with the Arabians to the camp had slain all the eldest”. Hebrew (WLC) 22:1: כִּ֤י כָל־הָרִאשֹׁנִים֙ הָרַ֣ג הַגְּד֔וּד [for all the first ones the band had killed]. KJV 22:8: “and found the princes of Judah, and the sons of the brethren of Ahaziah, that ministered to Ahaziah, he slew them”. Hebrew (WLC) 22:8: וּבְנֵ֨י אֲחֵ֧י אֲחַזְיָ֛הוּ [and the sons of the brothers of Ahaziah]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 22:8: καὶ τοὺς ἀδελφοὺς Ὀχοζίου [and the brothers of Ochozias]. KJV 2 Kings 10:13: “Jehu met with the brethren of Ahaziah king of Judah, and said, Who are ye? And they answered, We are the brethren of Ahaziah”. Hebrew (WLC) 10:13: אֲחֵ֤י אֲחַזְיָ֙הוּ֙ אֲנַ֔חְנוּ [we are the brothers of Ahaziah]. Brenton Greek 10:13: ἀδελφοὶ Ὀχοζίου ἡμεῖς [we are brothers of Ochozias]. KJV 10:14: “and slew them at the pit of the shearing house, even two and forty men; neither left he any of them”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/22/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH22.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/10/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI10.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Chronicles leaves Ahaziah as the only surviving son before his reign begins, so by its account he had no living brothers for Jehu to meet. Kings has forty-two men say they are his brethren. Chronicles itself names the victims as sons of his brethren. Brenton’s Greek of Chronicles has no “the sons of” and so agrees with Kings against the KJV and the Hebrew of Chronicles.",
    "scope": "G applies at 2 Chronicles 22:8 only. “Brethren” can be used of kinsmen more widely, and if Kings uses it so, the two books agree; Kings does not say so. Chronicles 21:17 calls the surviving son Jehoahaz and 22:1 calls the youngest son Ahaziah; the record does not rest on the names. The two passages are treated as one event because both follow Jehu’s killing of the house of Ahab and both sets of victims are linked to Ahaziah; neither book refers to the other. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
