    "title": "Joash’s temple collection: no vessels made from the money, or vessels made from it?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "2 Kings says no bowls, trumpets or vessels of gold or silver were made from the money brought into the temple. 2 Chronicles says the rest of the money was made into vessels of gold and silver.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 12:13: “Howbeit there were not made for the house of the Lord bowls of silver, snuffers, basons, trumpets, any vessels of gold, or vessels of silver, of the money that was brought into the house of the Lord”. Hebrew (WLC), numbered 12:14: אַךְ֩ לֹ֨א יֵעָשֶׂ֜ה בֵּ֣ית יְהוָ֗ה סִפֹּ֥ות כֶּ֙סֶף֙ [but there were not made for the house of the LORD basins of silver] … מִן־הַכֶּ֖סֶף הַמּוּבָ֥א בֵית־יְהוָֽה [from the money brought to the house of the LORD]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek), numbered 12:14: Πλὴν οὐ ποιηθήσονται οἴκῳ Κυρίου θύραι ἀργυραῖ [only there shall not be made for the house of the Lord silver doors]. KJV 12:14: “But they gave that to the workmen, and repaired therewith the house of the Lord.” KJV 2 Chronicles 24:14: “And when they had finished it, they brought the rest of the money before the king and Jehoiada, whereof were made vessels for the house of the Lord, even vessels to minister, and to offer withal, and spoons, and vessels of gold and silver.” Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 24:14: אֶת־שְׁאָ֣ר הַכֶּ֗סֶף וַיַּעֲשֵׂ֨הוּ כֵלִ֤ים לְבֵית־יְהוָה֙ [the rest of the money, and he made it into vessels for the house of the LORD]. Brenton Greek 2 Chronicles 24:14: τὸ κατάλοιπον τοῦ ἀργυρίου, καὶ ἐποίησαν σκεύη εἰς οἶκον Κυρίου [the remainder of the money, and they made vessels for the house of the Lord]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/12/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI12.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/24/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both passages follow the same chest of offerings in the reign of Joash. Kings says no vessels of gold or silver were made from the money brought in; it went to the workmen. Chronicles says the money left over was made into vessels of gold and silver.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew in both books, and the Greek of each book makes the same statement as its Hebrew (with “silver doors” for the first item in Kings, which does not affect the point). Both statements can stand if Kings speaks only of the time while the repairs lasted and Chronicles of a surplus afterwards; Kings does not limit its statement so, and does not mention a surplus. In chapter 12 the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek number each verse one higher than the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
