    "title": "Joash’s burial: “with his fathers”, or “not in the sepulchres of the kings”?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "2 Kings says Joash was buried with his fathers in the city of David. 2 Chronicles says he was buried in the city of David but not in the sepulchres of the kings.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 12:21: “smote him, and he died; and they buried him with his fathers in the city of David”. Hebrew (WLC), numbered 12:22: וַיִּקְבְּר֥וּ אֹתֹ֛ו עִם־אֲבֹתָ֖יו בְּעִ֣יר דָּוִ֑ד [and they buried him with his fathers in the city of David]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek), numbered 12:22: καὶ ἔθαψαν αὐτὸν μετὰ τῶν πατέρων αὐτοῦ ἑν πόλει Δαυίδ [and they buried him with his fathers in the city of David]. KJV 2 Chronicles 24:25: “and slew him on his bed, and he died: and they buried him in the city of David, but they buried him not in the sepulchres of the kings”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 24:25: וַֽיִּקְבְּרֻ֙הוּ֙ בְּעִ֣יר דָּוִ֔יד וְלֹ֥א קְבָרֻ֖הוּ בְּקִבְרֹ֥ות הַמְּלָכִֽים [and they buried him in the city of David, and they did not bury him in the tombs of the kings]. Brenton Greek 2 Chronicles 24:25: καὶ οὐκ ἔθαψαν αὐτὸν ἐν τῷ τάφῳ τῶν βασιλέων [and they did not bury him in the tomb of the kings]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/12/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI12.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/24/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Joash’s fathers were the kings of Judah. Kings places him with them in burial; Chronicles says he was kept out of the kings’ tombs.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew, and the Greek agrees in each book. Both books name the city of David. If “with his fathers” means only the same city and not the same tombs, the statements agree; Kings does not say so. In chapter 12 the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek number each verse one higher than the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
