    "title": "Jehoahaz: seventeen years from Joash’s twenty-third year, yet his son succeeds in the thirty-seventh?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Jehoahaz begins to reign in the twenty-third year of Joash of Judah and reigns seventeen years. His son Jehoash begins in the thirty-seventh year of Joash, fourteen years later.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 13:1: “In the three and twentieth year of Joash the son of Ahaziah king of Judah Jehoahaz the son of Jehu began to reign over Israel in Samaria, and reigned seventeen years.” Hebrew (WLC) 13:1: בִּשְׁנַ֨ת עֶשְׂרִ֤ים וְשָׁלֹשׁ֙ שָׁנָ֔ה לְיֹואָ֥שׁ [in the twenty-third year of Joash] … שְׁבַ֥ע עֶשְׂרֵ֖ה שָׁנָֽה [seventeen years]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 13:1: Ἐν ἔτει εἰκοστῷ καὶ τρίτῳ ἔτει τῷ Ἰωὰς [in the twenty-third year of Joas] … ἑπτακαίδεκα ἔτη [seventeen years]. KJV 13:10: “In the thirty and seventh year of Joash king of Judah began Jehoash the son of Jehoahaz to reign over Israel in Samaria”. Hebrew (WLC) 13:10: בִּשְׁנַ֨ת שְׁלֹשִׁ֤ים וָשֶׁ֙בַע֙ שָׁנָ֔ה לְיֹואָ֖שׁ מֶ֣לֶךְ יְהוּדָ֑ה [in the thirty-seventh year of Joash king of Judah]. Brenton Greek 13:10: Ἐν ἔτει τριακοστῷ καὶ ἑβδόμῳ ἔτει τῷ Ἰωὰς [in the thirty-seventh year of Joas]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/13/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI13.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Seventeen years from the twenty-third year of Joash reach his thirty-ninth or fortieth year. The son’s accession is dated to the thirty-seventh. The Hebrew and the Greek have the same three numbers as the KJV.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew, and Brenton’s Greek has the same figures. The figures fit if Jehoash reigned beside his father for the last two or three years; the chapter does not report that. The record does not argue about counting part-years. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
