    "title": "Azariah: king from Jeroboam’s twenty-seventh year, when his father died about Jeroboam’s fifteenth?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Jeroboam begins in Amaziah’s fifteenth year and Amaziah reigns twenty-nine years, so Amaziah dies about Jeroboam’s fifteenth year. Azariah, made king in his father’s place, is dated to Jeroboam’s twenty-seventh.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 14:2: “He was twenty and five years old when he began to reign, and reigned twenty and nine years in Jerusalem.” Hebrew (WLC) 14:2: וְעֶשְׂרִ֤ים וָתֵ֙שַׁע֙ שָׁנָ֔ה מָלַ֖ךְ בִּירוּשָׁלִָ֑ם [and twenty-nine years he reigned in Jerusalem]. KJV 14:17: “And Amaziah the son of Joash king of Judah lived after the death of Jehoash son of Jehoahaz king of Israel fifteen years.” KJV 14:21: “And all the people of Judah took Azariah, which was sixteen years old, and made him king instead of his father Amaziah.” KJV 14:23: “In the fifteenth year of Amaziah the son of Joash king of Judah Jeroboam the son of Joash king of Israel began to reign in Samaria, and reigned forty and one years.” Hebrew (WLC) 14:23: בִּשְׁנַת֙ חֲמֵשׁ־עֶשְׂרֵ֣ה שָׁנָ֔ה לַאֲמַצְיָ֥הוּ [in the fifteenth year of Amaziah]. KJV 15:1: “In the twenty and seventh year of Jeroboam king of Israel began Azariah son of Amaziah king of Judah to reign.” Hebrew (WLC) 15:1: בִּשְׁנַ֨ת עֶשְׂרִ֤ים וָשֶׁ֙בַע֙ שָׁנָ֔ה לְיָרָבְעָ֖ם מֶ֣לֶךְ יִשְׂרָאֵ֑ל מָלַ֛ךְ עֲזַרְיָ֥ה בֶן־אֲמַצְיָ֖ה [in the twenty-seventh year of Jeroboam king of Israel, Azariah son of Amaziah reigned]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 15:1: Ἐν ἔτει εἰκοστῷ καὶ ἑβδόμῳ τῷ Ἱεροβοὰμ [in the twenty-seventh year of Jeroboam]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/14/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/15/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI15.htm.",
    "reasoning": "If Jeroboam begins in Amaziah’s fifteenth year and Amaziah reigns twenty-nine years, Amaziah’s last year is about Jeroboam’s fifteenth. Verse 17 gives the same span another way: Amaziah outlived Jeroboam’s father by fifteen years. Azariah is made king in his father’s place, yet 15:1 dates him twelve years later, in Jeroboam’s twenty-seventh year.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew, and Brenton’s Greek has the same figure at 15:1. The figures fit if Judah had no king for about twelve years, or if Jeroboam’s years are counted from a time before his father died; the book reports neither. No claim is made about which figure is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
