    "summary": "2 Kings 15:30 has Hoshea take the throne in the twentieth year of Jotham. Three verses later Jotham reigns sixteen years. 17:1 has Hoshea begin in the twelfth year of Jotham’s son Ahaz.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 15:30: “and slew him, and reigned in his stead, in the twentieth year of Jotham the son of Uzziah”. Hebrew (WLC) 15:30: בִּשְׁנַ֣ת עֶשְׂרִ֔ים לְיֹותָ֖ם בֶּן־עֻזִיָּֽה [in the twentieth year of Jotham son of Uzziah]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 15:30: ἐν ἔτει εἰκοστῷ Ἰωάθαμ [in the twentieth year of Joatham]. KJV 15:33: “Five and twenty years old was he when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem.” Hebrew (WLC) 15:33: וְשֵׁשׁ־עֶשְׂרֵ֣ה שָׁנָ֔ה מָלַ֖ךְ בִּירוּשָׁלִָ֑ם [and sixteen years he reigned in Jerusalem]. KJV 17:1: “In the twelfth year of Ahaz king of Judah began Hoshea the son of Elah to reign in Samaria over Israel nine years.” Hebrew (WLC) 17:1: בִּשְׁנַת֙ שְׁתֵּ֣ים עֶשְׂרֵ֔ה לְאָחָ֖ז מֶ֣לֶךְ יְהוּדָ֑ה מָ֠לַךְ הֹושֵׁ֨עַ בֶּן־אֵלָ֧ה [in the twelfth year of Ahaz king of Judah, Hoshea son of Elah reigned]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/15/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI15.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/17/.",
    "reasoning": "A king who reigned sixteen years has no twentieth year. The same accession is also dated by the next king of Judah, to his twelfth year. The Hebrew has the same figures as the KJV, and Brenton’s Greek has the twentieth year at 15:30.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew and the Greek. The “twentieth year of Jotham” would fall in the fourth year of Ahaz if counted on past Jotham’s sixteen; the book does not say it counts so, and that still differs from the twelfth year of Ahaz in 17:1. A reign shared between Jotham and Ahaz, or a second start to Hoshea’s reign, would ease the figures; neither is reported. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
