    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In 2 Kings Ahaz pays the king of Assyria to save him, and the king hearkens, takes Damascus and kills Rezin. In 2 Chronicles the king of Assyria comes, distresses Ahaz, and does not help him.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 16:7: “come up, and save me out of the hand of the king of Syria, and out of the hand of the king of Israel”. KJV 16:8: “and sent it for a present to the king of Assyria”. KJV 16:9: “And the king of Assyria hearkened unto him: for the king of Assyria went up against Damascus, and took it, and carried the people of it captive to Kir, and slew Rezin.” Hebrew (WLC) 16:9: וַיִּשְׁמַ֤ע אֵלָיו֙ מֶ֣לֶךְ אַשּׁ֔וּר [and the king of Assyria listened to him]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 16:9: Καὶ ἤκουσεν αὐτοῦ βασιλεὺς Ἀσσυρίων [and the king of the Assyrians listened to him]. KJV 2 Chronicles 28:16: “At that time did king Ahaz send unto the kings of Assyria to help him.” KJV 28:20: “And Tilgathpilneser king of Assyria came unto him, and distressed him, but strengthened him not.” Hebrew (WLC) 28:20: וַיָּ֥צַר לֹ֖ו וְלֹ֥א חֲזָקֹֽו [and he distressed him and did not strengthen him]. Brenton Greek 28:20, end of the verse: καὶ ἔθλιψεν αὐτόν [and he afflicted him]. KJV 28:21: “and gave it unto the king of Assyria: but he helped him not”. Hebrew (WLC) 28:21: וְלֹ֥א לְעֶזְרָ֖ה לֹֽו [and not for a help to him]. Brenton Greek 28:21: καὶ οὐκ εἰς βοήθειαν αὐτῷ ἦν [and it was not for a help to him]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/16/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI16.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/28/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH28.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both books tell of Ahaz sending temple and palace treasure to the king of Assyria for help. Kings reports that the request was granted and Ahaz’s enemy was killed. Chronicles reports that the Assyrian king brought trouble and no help.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed. Brenton’s Greek of 28:20 ends at “afflicted him” and lacks “but strengthened him not”, but its next verse still says there was no help, so the Greek makes the same point as the KJV. Both statements can stand if Assyria removed Syria and then oppressed Judah; Chronicles does not mention Damascus or Rezin’s death, and Kings does not mention distress. The Hebrew of 28:21 has “not for a help to him”; the KJV’s “he helped him not” is close and no H is claimed. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
