    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV and the Hebrew make Ahaz twenty when he began a sixteen-year reign, and his son Hezekiah twenty-five at his own accession. The Greek of Chronicles makes Ahaz twenty-five.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 16:2: “Twenty years old was Ahaz when he began to reign, and reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem”. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2 Kings 16:2: Υἱὸς εἴκοσι ἐτῶν ἦν Ἄχαζ [twenty years old was Achaz]. KJV 2 Chronicles 28:1: “Ahaz was twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 28:1: בֶּן־עֶשְׂרִ֤ים שָׁנָה֙ אָחָ֣ז בְּמָלְכֹ֔ו [twenty years old was Ahaz when he became king]. Brenton Greek 2 Chronicles 28:1: Υἱὸς εἴκοσι καὶ πέντε ἐτῶν ἦν Ἄχαζ [twenty-five years old was Achaz]. KJV 2 Kings 18:2: “Twenty and five years old was he when he began to reign”. Hebrew (WLC) 18:2: בֶּן־עֶשְׂרִ֨ים וְחָמֵ֤שׁ שָׁנָה֙ הָיָ֣ה בְמָלְכֹ֔ו [twenty-five years old was he when he became king]. Fetched from https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI16.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/28/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH28.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/18/.",
    "reasoning": "Twenty years and a sixteen-year reign make Ahaz thirty-six at his death. Hezekiah, who followed him, was then twenty-five, so by the KJV’s figures Ahaz was about eleven when his son was born. Brenton’s Greek of Chronicles gives Ahaz twenty-five years at his accession, which would make him about sixteen at the birth; the Greek of Kings has twenty, as the KJV does.",
    "scope": "G applies at 2 Chronicles 28:1 only. The record states a difference of wording between the Greek and the KJV; it does not say the KJV’s figures are impossible, and it does not say which figure is original. Other verses date Hezekiah’s accession by a king of Israel; they are not used here. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
