    "title": "Hezekiah and the envoys of Babylon: he “hearkened unto them”, or “was glad of them”?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "The same sentence appears in 2 Kings and in Isaiah. In Kings Hezekiah “hearkened unto” the envoys; in Isaiah he “was glad of” them. The Hebrew words differ by one letter. The Greek has “was glad” in both books.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 20:13: “And Hezekiah hearkened unto them, and shewed them all the house of his precious things”. Hebrew (WLC) 20:13: וַיִּשְׁמַ֣ע עֲלֵיהֶם֮ חִזְקִיָּהוּ֒ [and Hezekiah listened to them]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 20:13: Καὶ ἐχάρη ἐπʼ αὐτοῖς Ἐζεκίας [and Ezekias rejoiced over them]. KJV Isaiah 39:2: “And Hezekiah was glad of them, and shewed them the house of his precious things”. Hebrew (WLC) Isaiah 39:2: וַיִּשְׂמַ֣ח עֲלֵיהֶם֮ חִזְקִיָּהוּ֒ [and Hezekiah rejoiced over them]. Brenton Greek Isaiah 39:2: Καὶ ἐχάρη ἐπʼ αὐτοῖς Ἐξεκίας [and Ezekias rejoiced over them]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/20/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI20.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/23/39/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA39.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The two verses are the same sentence about the same visit. The KJV follows the Hebrew of each book, and the two Hebrew verbs differ only in their last letter. Brenton’s Greek of 2 Kings has “rejoiced”, the reading of Isaiah, so in Kings it differs from the KJV.",
    "scope": "G applies at 2 Kings 20:13 only. A man may both listen and be glad, so the two statements are not incompatible; the record notes that one sentence is worded two ways. No claim is made about which verb is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
