    "title": "Huldah in the Greek: Shallum’s wife, or his mother? Keeper of the wardrobe, or of the commandments?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV calls Huldah the wife of Shallum, with a keeper of the wardrobe mentioned in the same verse. The Greek of 2 Kings calls her Shallum’s mother. The Greek of 2 Chronicles calls her his wife and says she kept the commandments.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 22:14: “Huldah the prophetess, the wife of Shallum the son of Tikvah, the son of Harhas, keeper of the wardrobe”. Hebrew (WLC) 22:14: אֶל־חֻלְדָּ֨ה הַנְּבִיאָ֜ה אֵ֣שֶׁת ׀ שַׁלֻּ֣ם בֶּן־תִּקְוָ֗ה בֶּן־חַרְחַס֙ שֹׁמֵ֣ר הַבְּגָדִ֔ים [to Huldah the prophetess, wife of Shallum son of Tikvah son of Harhas, keeper of the garments]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 22:14: πρὸς Ὄλδαν τὴν προφῆτιν μητέρα Σελλὴμ υἱοῦ Θεκουαὺ υἱοῦ Ἀρὰς τοῦ ἱματιοφύλακος [to Olda the prophetess, mother of Sellem son of Thekuau son of Aras, the keeper of the garments]. KJV 2 Chronicles 34:22: “Huldah the prophetess, the wife of Shallum the son of Tikvath, the son of Hasrah, keeper of the wardrobe”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 34:22: שֹׁומֵ֣ר הַבְּגָדִ֔ים [keeper of the garments]. Brenton Greek 2 Chronicles 34:22: πρὸς Ὀλδὰν τὴν προφῆτιν, γυναῖκα Σελλὴμ υἱοῦ Θεκωὲ υἱοῦ Ἀρὰς, φυλάσσουσαν τὰς ἐντολὰς [to Olda the prophetess, wife of Sellem son of Thekoe son of Aras, her who kept the commandments]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/22/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI22.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/34/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH34.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 2 Kings 22:14 Brenton’s Greek makes Huldah the mother of Shallum where the KJV and the Hebrew make her his wife. At 2 Chronicles 34:22 the Greek has “wife”, but in place of a keeper of the wardrobe it has a feminine phrase, “her who kept the commandments”, said of Huldah.",
    "scope": "G applies at both verses, for a different word in each: “mother” at 2 Kings 22:14, “kept the commandments” at 2 Chronicles 34:22. The KJV matches the Hebrew in both. No claim is made about which form is original. The spellings of the names differ between the books in every text and are not part of the finding. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
