    "title": "Josiah’s death: slain at Megiddo and carried away dead, or brought wounded to Jerusalem, where he died?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "2 Kings says Pharaoh slew Josiah at Megiddo and his servants carried him dead from there. 2 Chronicles says he was wounded, moved to another chariot and brought to Jerusalem, “and he died”.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 23:29: “and king Josiah went against him; and he slew him at Megiddo, when he had seen him”. Hebrew (WLC) 23:29: וַיְמִיתֵ֙הוּ֙ בִּמְגִדֹּ֔ו [and he put him to death at Megiddo]. KJV 23:30: “And his servants carried him in a chariot dead from Megiddo, and brought him to Jerusalem, and buried him in his own sepulchre.” Hebrew (WLC) 23:30: וַיַּרְכִּבֻ֨הוּ עֲבָדָ֥יו מֵת֙ מִמְּגִדֹּ֔ו [and his servants drove him, dead, from Megiddo]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 23:30: νεκρὸν ἐκ Μαγεδδὼ [a dead man, from Mageddo]. KJV 2 Chronicles 35:23: “And the archers shot at king Josiah; and the king said to his servants, Have me away; for I am sore wounded.” KJV 35:24: “His servants therefore took him out of that chariot, and put him in the second chariot that he had; and they brought him to Jerusalem, and he died, and was buried in one of the sepulchres of his fathers.” Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 35:24: וַיֹּולִיכֻ֙הוּ֙ יְר֣וּשָׁלִַ֔ם וַיָּ֕מָת [and they brought him to Jerusalem, and he died]. Brenton Greek 2 Chronicles 35:24: καὶ ἤγαγον αὐτὸν εἰς Ἱερουσαλὴμ, καὶ ἀπέθανε [and they brought him to Jerusalem, and he died]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/23/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI23.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/35/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH35.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Kings places the death at Megiddo and has a dead man carried to Jerusalem. Chronicles has a wounded man speak, be moved, and be brought to Jerusalem, and only then says he died.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew in both books, and the Greek agrees in each. Chronicles does not say in so many words where Josiah died; the order of its clauses puts the death after the journey. If the Hebrew word rendered “dead” in Kings is taken as “dying”, the accounts draw closer; the record does not decide that, and verse 29 still says he was put to death at Megiddo. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
