    "title": "Zedekiah: Jehoiachin’s uncle, his brother, or his son?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "2 Kings calls the new king Jehoiachin’s father’s brother. 2 Chronicles calls him Jehoiachin’s brother. The Greek of Kings says “his son”, and the Greek of Chronicles says “his father’s brother”.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 24:17: “And the king of Babylon made Mattaniah his father's brother king in his stead, and changed his name to Zedekiah.” Hebrew (WLC) 24:17: אֶת־מַתַּנְיָ֥ה דֹדֹ֖ו תַּחְתָּ֑יו [Mattaniah his uncle in his place]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 24:17: τὸν Βατθανίαν υἱὸν αὐτοῦ ἀντʼ αὐτοῦ [Batthanias his son in his place]. KJV 2 Chronicles 36:10: “and made Zedekiah his brother king over Judah and Jerusalem”. Hebrew (WLC) 2 Chronicles 36:10: אֶת־צִדְקִיָּ֣הוּ אָחִ֔יו [Zedekiah his brother]. Brenton Greek 2 Chronicles 36:10: Σεδεκίαν ἀδελφὸν τοῦ πατρὸς αὐτοῦ [Sedekias, brother of his father]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/24/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI24.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/14/36/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2CH36.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV follows the Hebrew in each book and so gives the same man two relationships to Jehoiachin: uncle in Kings, brother in Chronicles. Brenton’s Greek differs from the KJV in both places. In Kings it has “his son”; in Chronicles it has “brother of his father”, which is what the KJV has in Kings.",
    "scope": "G applies at both verses: “his son” at 2 Kings 24:17 and “brother of his father” at 2 Chronicles 36:10. “Brother” can be used of a kinsman more widely, and if Chronicles uses it so, the two KJV verses agree; Chronicles does not say so. No claim is made about which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
