    "title": "The exile under Jehoiachin: the eighth year and ten thousand captives, or the seventh year and 3,023?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "2 Kings dates the capture of Jehoiachin to the eighth year of the king of Babylon and counts ten thousand captives. Jeremiah dates a deportation to the seventh year and counts 3,023. The Greek of Jeremiah lacks the verse.",
    "text": "KJV 2 Kings 24:12: “and the king of Babylon took him in the eighth year of his reign”. Hebrew (WLC) 24:12: בִּשְׁנַ֥ת שְׁמֹנֶ֖ה לְמָלְכֹֽו [in the eighth year of his reign]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 24:12: ἐν τῷ ὀγδόῳ ἔτει τῆς βασιλείας αὐτοῦ [in the eighth year of his reign]. KJV 24:14: “And he carried away all Jerusalem, and all the princes, and all the mighty men of valour, even ten thousand captives, and all the craftsmen and smiths”. Brenton Greek 24:14: δέκα χιλιάδας [ten thousand]. KJV 24:16: “And all the men of might, even seven thousand, and craftsmen and smiths a thousand”. Hebrew (WLC) 24:16: שִׁבְעַ֣ת אֲלָפִ֗ים [seven thousand]. KJV Jeremiah 52:28: “This is the people whom Nebuchadrezzar carried away captive: in the seventh year three thousand Jews and three and twenty”. Hebrew (WLC) Jeremiah 52:28: בִּשְׁנַת־שֶׁ֕בַע יְהוּדִ֕ים שְׁלֹ֥שֶׁת אֲלָפִ֖ים וְעֶשְׂרִ֥ים וּשְׁלֹשָֽׁה [in the seventh year, Jews, three thousand and twenty-three]. The Brenton Greek page for Jeremiah 52 prints verses 1, 4–14, 16–27 and 31–34; it has no verses 28–30. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/12/24/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/2KI24.htm and https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/24/52/.",
    "reasoning": "Kings puts the first deportation in the Babylonian king’s eighth year and gives ten thousand captives in one verse and seven thousand with a thousand craftsmen in another. Jeremiah’s list puts a deportation in the seventh year and gives 3,023. The year and the count both differ. Brenton’s Greek of Jeremiah does not have the list at all.",
    "scope": "G applies at Jeremiah 52:28 only, where the Greek lacks a verse the KJV has. Jeremiah does not name Jehoiachin in this verse; if its seventh-year figure belongs to an earlier or smaller removal, or counts only one class of people, both books can stand, and neither says so. A different way of counting a king’s first year could explain seven against eight; it would not explain the totals. The Hebrew of 2 Kings 24:14 has a written and a read form of “ten”; both mean ten, and the Hebrew of that verse is not quoted. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
