    "title": "Who was Cain’s wife, and where did she come from?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Genesis says Cain knew his wife and she bore Enoch. It does not name her or say who her parents were, and no woman other than Eve has been mentioned before this verse.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 4:1: “And Adam knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain”. KJV 4:16: “And Cain went out from the presence of the Lord, and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden.” KJV 4:17: “And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city”. Hebrew (WLC) 4:17: וַיֵּ֤דַע קַ֙יִן֙ אֶת־אִשְׁתֹּ֔ו [and Cain knew his wife]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 4:17: Καὶ ἔγνω Κάϊν τὴν γυναῖκα αὐτοῦ [and Cain knew his wife]. KJV 5:4: “And the days of Adam after he had begotten Seth were eight hundred years: and he begat sons and daughters”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/4/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN04.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The wife appears in the story without a name, a parent or a place of origin. Before this verse the only woman mentioned is Eve, Cain’s mother. Whether Cain married before or after he went to Nod is not said either: verse 16 puts him in Nod and verse 17 speaks of his wife without saying where he found her.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Many people in the Bible are unnamed; the question here is her origin, since the book has not yet mentioned any family she could come from. Genesis 5:4 says Adam had daughters, without saying when; that Cain married a sister or a niece is a reading drawn from that verse, not a statement of the text. That she came from other people is another reading, also unstated. The record asserts neither. The city Cain builds in the same verse raises a like question about its inhabitants; it is not pursued here. Check made for an answer elsewhere: every verse of the packaged KJV outside Genesis that contains the name Cain was read (Hebrews 11:4, 1 John 3:12, Jude 1:11; Joshua 15:57 is a town). None mentions his wife. That is a word search, not a reading of the whole Bible. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
