    "title": "Did God end his work on the seventh day, or the sixth?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says God ended his work on the seventh day and rested on the seventh day. Brenton’s Greek says he finished on the sixth day and ceased on the seventh.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 2:2: “And on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made.” Hebrew (WLC) 2:2: וַיְכַ֤ל אֱלֹהִים֙ בַּיֹּ֣ום הַשְּׁבִיעִ֔י מְלַאכְתֹּ֖ו אֲשֶׁ֣ר עָשָׂ֑ה [and God finished on the seventh day his work which he had made]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2:2: Καὶ συνετέλεσεν ὁ Θεὸς ἐν τῇ ἡμέρᾳ τῇ ἕκτῃ τὰ ἔργα αὐτοῦ, ἃ ἐποίησε· καὶ κατέπαυσε τῇ ἡμέρᾳ τῇ ἑβδόμῃ [and God finished on the sixth day his works which he made; and he ceased on the seventh day]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/2/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The verse names a day twice. The KJV and the Hebrew quoted have “seventh” both times. Brenton’s Greek has “sixth” the first time and “seventh” the second, so that the finishing and the resting fall on different days.",
    "scope": "G applies at Genesis 2:2. This is a difference between two published texts, not a contradiction inside the KJV: “ended … on the seventh day” can be read as “had ended by the seventh day”. No claim is made about which number is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
