    "title": "“In the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die” — and Adam lived 930 years",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "God tells the man he will surely die in the day he eats of the tree. The man eats, and Genesis 5 gives his whole life as nine hundred and thirty years.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 2:17: “But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.” Hebrew (WLC) 2:17: כִּ֗י בְּיֹ֛ום אֲכָלְךָ֥ מִמֶּ֖נּוּ מֹ֥ות תָּמֽוּת [for in the day of thy eating of it, dying thou shalt die]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2:17: ᾗ δʼ ἂν ἡμέρᾳ φάγητε ἀπʼ αὐτοῦ, θανάτῳ ἀποθανεῖσθε [and in whatever day ye eat of it, ye shall die by death]. KJV 3:6: “and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat”. KJV 5:5: “And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.” Hebrew (WLC) 5:5: תְּשַׁ֤ע מֵאֹות֙ שָׁנָ֔ה וּשְׁלֹשִׁ֖ים שָׁנָ֑ה וַיָּמֹֽת [nine hundred years and thirty years, and he died]. Brenton Greek 5:5: τριάκοντα καὶ ἐννακόσια ἔτη· καὶ ἀπέθανεν [nine hundred and thirty years; and he died]. KJV 2:4: “in the day that the Lord God made the earth and the heavens”; Hebrew (WLC) 2:4: בְּיֹ֗ום עֲשֹׂ֛ות יְהוָ֥ה אֱלֹהִ֖ים אֶ֥רֶץ וְשָׁמָֽיִם [in the day of the LORD God’s making earth and heavens]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/2/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/5/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN02.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN05.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The warning ties the death to the day of eating. The story has the man eat, and the genealogy has him die at nine hundred and thirty. The KJV, the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek agree on the warning and on the number.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew, and Brenton’s Greek has the same sense. The two statements clash as worded but can be reconciled in more than one way: “in the day” may mean “when”, as the same Hebrew word is used at 2:4 of the making of the earth and the heavens; or the death meant may be something other than the end of bodily life, or a sentence passed that day and carried out later. The passage states none of these, and this record asserts none. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
