    "title": "What Cain said to Abel: the Greek gives words the KJV lacks",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says Cain talked with Abel and gives no words. Brenton’s Greek has Cain say, “Let us go out into the plain.”",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 4:8: “And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him.” Hebrew (WLC) 4:8: וַיֹּ֥אמֶר קַ֖יִן אֶל־הֶ֣בֶל אָחִ֑יו וַֽיְהִי֙ בִּהְיֹותָ֣ם בַּשָּׂדֶ֔ה [and Cain said to Abel his brother; and it came to pass, when they were in the field]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 4:8: Καὶ εἶπεν Κάϊν πρὸς Ἄβελ τὸν ἀδελφὸν αὐτοῦ, διέλθωμεν εἰς τὸ πεδίον [and Cain said to Abel his brother, Let us go through into the plain]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/4/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN04.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the Hebrew quoted, “said” is followed by no speech; the next words are “and it came to pass”. The KJV has “talked with”. Brenton’s Greek supplies what was said, and with it something the KJV does not state anywhere in the chapter: that Cain asked Abel to go out with him.",
    "scope": "G applies at Genesis 4:8. No H tag is proposed: “said to” and “talked with” are not shown to differ in meaning. No claim is made about whether the words stood in an older Hebrew text or were added in the Greek. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
