    "title": "Shem: a hundred years old two years after the flood?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Noah is five hundred when he begets Shem, Ham and Japheth, and six hundred when the flood comes. That makes a son born in Noah’s five-hundredth year a hundred at the flood. Genesis 11 says Shem was a hundred two years after it.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 5:32: “And Noah was five hundred years old: and Noah begat Shem, Ham, and Japheth.” Hebrew (WLC) 5:32: וַֽיְהִי־נֹ֕חַ בֶּן־חֲמֵ֥שׁ מֵאֹ֖ות שָׁנָ֑ה [and Noah was five hundred years old]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 5:32: Καὶ ἦν Νῶε ἐτῶν πεντακοσίων [and Noe was five hundred years old]. KJV 7:6: “And Noah was six hundred years old when the flood of waters was upon the earth.” Hebrew (WLC) 7:6: וְנֹ֕חַ בֶּן־שֵׁ֥שׁ מֵאֹ֖ות שָׁנָ֑ה [and Noah was six hundred years old]. Brenton Greek 7:6: Νῶε δὲ ἦν ἐτῶν ἑξακοσίων [and Noe was six hundred years old]. KJV 11:10: “Shem was an hundred years old, and begat Arphaxad two years after the flood”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:10: בֶּן־מְאַ֣ת שָׁנָ֔ה וַיֹּ֖ולֶד אֶת־אַרְפַּכְשָׁ֑ד שְׁנָתַ֖יִם אַחַ֥ר הַמַּבּֽוּל [a hundred years old, and he begat Arpachshad two years after the flood]. Brenton Greek 11:10: καὶ ἦν Σὴμ υἱὸς ἑκατὸν ἐτῶν, ὅτε ἐγέννησε τὸν Ἀρφαξὰδ, δευτέρου ἔτους μετὰ τὸν κατακλυσμόν [and Sem was a hundred years old when he begat Arphaxad, in the second year after the flood]. KJV 10:21: “Unto Shem also, the father of all the children of Eber, the brother of Japheth the elder”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/5/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/7/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/11/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN05.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN07.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN11.htm.",
    "reasoning": "From Noah’s five-hundredth year to his six-hundredth is a hundred years. If Shem was born in the five-hundredth year he was a hundred when the flood came and a hundred and two “two years after the flood”. If he was a hundred two years after the flood, Noah was five hundred and two when he was born. The numbers are the same in the KJV, the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the three texts agree. The figures can be reconciled if 5:32 gives the year in which Noah’s sons began to be born and Shem was not the first. The KJV at 10:21 has wording, quoted above, that is read that way by some; the text nowhere says in which year each son was born, and this record does not assert it. The verse does not say whether the two years run from the start of the flood or from its end; on either count the figures do not meet. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
