    "title": "Arphaxad begat Salah — or Cainan, who begat Salah?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "In the KJV of Genesis, Arphaxad begets Salah. In the KJV of Luke, Sala is the son of Cainan, who is the son of Arphaxad. Brenton’s Greek of Genesis has Cainan between the two, as Luke does, and gives Arphaxad’s age as 135 where the KJV has 35.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 11:12: “And Arphaxad lived five and thirty years, and begat Salah:” Hebrew (WLC) 11:12: וְאַרְפַּכְשַׁ֣ד חַ֔י חָמֵ֥שׁ וּשְׁלֹשִׁ֖ים שָׁנָ֑ה וַיֹּ֖ולֶד אֶת־שָֽׁלַח [and Arpachshad lived five and thirty years, and begat Shelah]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 11:12: Καὶ ἔζησεν Ἀρφαξὰδ ἑκατὸν τριακονταπέντε ἔτη, καὶ ἐγέννησε τὸν Καϊνᾶν [and Arphaxad lived a hundred and thirty-five years, and begat Cainan]. Brenton Greek 11:13: Καὶ ἔζησε Καϊνᾶν ἑκατὸν καὶ τριάκοντα ἔτη, καὶ ἐγέννησε τὸν Σαλά [and Cainan lived a hundred and thirty years, and begat Sala]; the KJV and the Hebrew of 11:13 have no such sentence. KJV 10:24: “And Arphaxad begat Salah; and Salah begat Eber.” Hebrew (WLC) 10:24: וְאַרְפַּכְשַׁ֖ד יָלַ֣ד אֶת־שָׁ֑לַח [and Arpachshad begat Shelah]. Brenton Greek 10:24: Καὶ Ἀρφαξὰδ ἐγέννησε τὸν Καϊνᾶν, καὶ Καϊνᾶν ἐγέννησε τὸν Σαλά [and Arphaxad begat Cainan, and Cainan begat Sala]. KJV Luke 3:35: “which was the son of Heber, which was the son of Sala”. KJV Luke 3:36: “Which was the son of Cainan, which was the son of Arphaxad”. Greek New Testament, Luke 3:36, TR: του καιναν του αρφαξαδ; TISCH: τοῦ Καϊνὰμ τοῦ Ἀρφαξὰδ [of Cainan (TISCH: Cainam), of Arphaxad]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/10/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN11.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN10.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/42/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/42/3/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV gives two lines for the same descent: Arphaxad, Salah in Genesis; Arphaxad, Cainan, Sala in Luke. The KJV follows the Hebrew in Genesis. Brenton’s Greek of Genesis has the extra generation at both 10:24 and 11:12–13, with ages for Cainan, and so agrees with Luke against the KJV of Genesis.",
    "scope": "G applies at Genesis 11:12, 11:13 and 10:24, where the Greek differs from the KJV. No Greek New Testament tag is proposed: the Greek of Luke has the name the KJV has. “Begat” can be used of a more distant ancestor, and on that reading the KJV of Genesis has passed over a generation without contradicting Luke; Genesis 11:12 gives Arphaxad’s age at the begetting and does not say so. No claim is made about which form of the list is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
