    "title": "Terah died at 205; Abram left Haran at 75, after his father’s death",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Terah is seventy when he begets Abram, Nahor and Haran, and dies at two hundred and five. Abram leaves Haran at seventy-five, and Acts says he left after his father died. Seventy and seventy-five make a hundred and forty-five, sixty years short.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 11:26: “And Terah lived seventy years, and begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran.” Hebrew (WLC) 11:26: וַֽיְחִי־תֶ֖רַח שִׁבְעִ֣ים שָׁנָ֑ה וַיֹּ֙ולֶד֙ אֶת־אַבְרָ֔ם אֶת־נָחֹ֖ור וְאֶת־הָרָֽן [and Terah lived seventy years, and begat Abram, Nahor and Haran]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 11:26: Καὶ ἔζησε Θάῤῥα ἑβδομήκοντα ἔτη, καὶ ἐγέννησε τὸν Ἄβραμ [and Tharrha lived seventy years, and begat Abram]. KJV 11:32: “And the days of Terah were two hundred and five years: and Terah died in Haran.” Hebrew (WLC) 11:32: וַיִּהְי֣וּ יְמֵי־תֶ֔רַח חָמֵ֥שׁ שָׁנִ֖ים וּמָאתַ֣יִם שָׁנָ֑ה וַיָּ֥מָת תֶּ֖רַח בְּחָרָֽן [and the days of Terah were two hundred and five years, and Terah died in Haran]. Brenton Greek 11:32: Καὶ ἐγένοντο πᾶσαι αἱ ἡμέραι Θάῤῥα ἐν γῇ Χαῤῥὰν, διακόσια πέντε ἔτη· καὶ ἀπέθανε Θάῤῥα ἐν Χαῤῥάν [and all the days of Tharrha in the land of Charrhan were two hundred and five years, and Tharrha died in Charrhan]. KJV 12:4: “and Abram was seventy and five years old when he departed out of Haran”. Hebrew (WLC) 12:4: וְאַבְרָ֗ם בֶּן־חָמֵ֤שׁ שָׁנִים֙ וְשִׁבְעִ֣ים שָׁנָ֔ה בְּצֵאתֹ֖ו מֵחָרָֽן [and Abram was seventy-five years old at his going out from Haran]. Brenton Greek 12:4: Ἅβραμ δὲ ἦν ἐτῶν ἑβδομηκονταπέντε, ὅτε ἐξῆλθεν ἐκ Χαῤῥάν [and Abram was seventy-five years old when he went out of Charrhan]. KJV Acts 7:4: “and dwelt in Charran: and from thence, when his father was dead, he removed him into this land”. Greek New Testament (TISCH) Acts 7:4: μετὰ τὸ ἀποθανεῖν τὸν πατέρα αὐτοῦ [after his father died]; the TR text has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/12/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN11.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN12.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/44/7/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/44/7/.",
    "reasoning": "If Abram was born when Terah was seventy and left Haran at seventy-five, Terah was then a hundred and forty-five and had sixty years still to live. Acts 7:4 puts the departure after Terah’s death, which by Genesis 11:32 was at two hundred and five. The numbers are the same in the KJV, the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed. Brenton’s Greek at 11:32 has the words “in the land of Charrhan” after “all the days of Tharrha”, which the KJV lacks; that difference is noted and not tagged, because the numbers are unchanged and the Greek still clashes with Acts 7:4 as worded. The figures can be reconciled if 11:26 gives the year in which Terah’s sons began to be born and Abram was born sixty years later; the text does not say which son was born when Terah was seventy, and this record does not assert it. Genesis by itself does not say that Abram left after Terah died; that statement is in Acts. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
