    "title": "Isaac: Abraham’s “only son”, or his “beloved” son?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, three times calls Isaac Abraham’s only son, though Ishmael was born before him and outlives Abraham. Brenton’s Greek has “beloved” each time, not “only”.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 22:2: “Take now thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest”. Hebrew (WLC) 22:2: אֶת־בִּנְךָ֙ אֶת־יְחִֽידְךָ֤ אֲשֶׁר־אָהַ֙בְתָּ֙ אֶת־יִצְחָ֔ק [thy son, thy only one, whom thou lovest, Isaac]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 22:2: τὸν υἱόν σου τὸν ἀγαπητὸν, ὃν ἠγάπησας, τὸν Ἰσαὰκ [thy son, the beloved, whom thou hast loved, Isaac]. KJV 22:12: “thou hast not withheld thy son, thine only son from me”; Hebrew: אֶת־בִּנְךָ֥ אֶת־יְחִידְךָ֖ מִמֶּֽנִּי [thy son, thy only one, from me]; Greek: τοῦ υἱοῦ σου τοῦ ἀγαπητοῦ [thy son, the beloved]. KJV 22:16: “and hast not withheld thy son, thine only son”; Hebrew: אֶת־בִּנְךָ֥ אֶת־יְחִידֶֽךָ [thy son, thy only one]; Greek, as the page prints it: τοῦ υἱοῦ σου τοῦ ἀγαπτοῦ [thy son, the beloved]. KJV 16:15: “And Hagar bare Abram a son”. KJV 21:12: “for in Isaac shall thy seed be called”. KJV 25:9: “And his sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him”. KJV Hebrews 11:17: “offered up his only begotten son”; Greek New Testament (TISCH): τὸν μονογενῆ προσέφερεν [he was offering up the only-begotten]; the TR text has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/22/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN22.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/11/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/11/.",
    "reasoning": "Abraham has two sons at the time of Genesis 22, and both are alive at his burial. The KJV’s “only” follows the Hebrew. Brenton’s Greek does not say “only” at any of the three places; it says “beloved”. Hebrews in the KJV has “only begotten”, which is closer to the Hebrew of Genesis than to Brenton’s Greek of Genesis.",
    "scope": "G applies at Genesis 22:2, 22:12 and 22:16. No H tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew. “Only son” beside a living brother clashes as worded and can be reconciled: Ishmael had been sent away, and 21:12 says the seed is to be called in Isaac, so “only” may mean the only son of Sarah or of the promise. Genesis 22 does not say in which sense it uses the word. At 22:16 the Greek word is quoted with the spelling of the page cited. No claim is made about which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
