    "title": "“God did tempt Abraham” and “neither tempteth he any man”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Genesis says God tempted Abraham. James says God tempts no man. The Greek of Genesis and the Greek of James use the same verb.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 22:1: “And it came to pass after these things, that God did tempt Abraham”. Hebrew (WLC) 22:1: וְהָ֣אֱלֹהִ֔ים נִסָּ֖ה אֶת־אַבְרָהָ֑ם [and God tested Abraham]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 22:1: ὁ Θεὸς ἐπείρασε τὸν Ἁβραὰμ [God tried Abraam]. KJV James 1:13: “Let no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of God: for God cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man:” KJV James 1:14: “But every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed.” Greek New Testament (TISCH) James 1:13: πειράζει δὲ αὐτὸς οὐδένα [and he himself tempts no one]; the TR text has the same words. KJV Exodus 16:4: “that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no”; Hebrew (WLC): לְמַ֧עַן אֲנַסֶּ֛נּוּ [that I may test them]; Brenton Greek: ὅπως πειράσω αὐτοὺς [that I may try them]. KJV Hebrews 11:17: “By faith Abraham, when he was tried, offered up Isaac”; Greek (TISCH): πειραζόμενος [being tried]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/22/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/16/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN22.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO16.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/59/1/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/59/1/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/11/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/11/.",
    "reasoning": "As the KJV words them, one verse says God did what the other says he does to no one. The Greek shows the same verb family in Genesis 22:1, James 1:13 and Hebrews 11:17. The Hebrew verb of Genesis 22:1 is the one the KJV renders “prove” at Exodus 16:4.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed. Not H: the Hebrew verb means to test, and “tempt” can carry that sense; one verb given two English renderings is not a mistranslation. Not G: Brenton’s Greek has the sense the KJV has. The two verses clash as worded. They can be reconciled if “tempt” in James means to entice to evil, as James 1:14 goes on to speak of lust and enticement, and in Genesis means to put to the proof; neither verse states that distinction, and the one Greek verb serves for both. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
