    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Jacob says he has seen God face to face and lived. Exodus says no man shall see God and live, and John says no man has seen God at any time.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 32:24: “and there wrestled a man with him until the breaking of the day”. KJV 32:30: “And Jacob called the name of the place Peniel: for I have seen God face to face, and my life is preserved.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 32:31 in that edition: כִּֽי־רָאִ֤יתִי אֱלֹהִים֙ פָּנִ֣ים אֶל־פָּנִ֔ים וַתִּנָּצֵ֖ל נַפְשִֽׁי [for I have seen God face to face, and my life is delivered]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek), printed as 32:31: εἶδον γὰρ Θεὸν πρόσωπον πρὸς πρὸσωπον, καὶ ἐσώθη μου ἡ ψυχή [for I have seen God face to face, and my life was saved]. KJV Exodus 33:20: “And he said, Thou canst not see my face: for there shall no man see me, and live.” Hebrew (WLC) Exodus 33:20: כִּ֛י לֹֽא־יִרְאַ֥נִי הָאָדָ֖ם וָחָֽי [for man shall not see me and live]. Brenton Greek Exodus 33:20: οὐ γὰρ μὴ ἴδῃ ἄνθρωπος τὸ πρόσωπόν μου, καὶ ζήσεται [for a man shall not see my face and live]. KJV John 1:18: “No man hath seen God at any time”; Greek New Testament (TISCH): θεὸν οὐδεὶς ἑώρακεν πώποτε [God no one has seen, ever]; the TR text has the same words. KJV Hosea 12:4: “Yea, he had power over the angel, and prevailed”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/32/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/33/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN32.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO33.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/43/1/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/43/1/.",
    "reasoning": "Jacob’s words are a plain claim to have seen God and survived. Exodus 33:20 says that cannot be, and John 1:18 says it has never happened. The KJV matches the Hebrew in Genesis and Exodus, and Brenton’s Greek has the same sense in both.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed. The statement in Genesis is Jacob’s own; the narrator calls his opponent “a man” (32:24), and Hosea 12:4 says “the angel”. The verses can be reconciled if what Jacob saw was a man or an angel in whom he recognised God, and not God as Exodus and John mean it; Genesis 32 does not say so. Exodus 33:11 and 24:10, which bear on the same question inside Exodus, are not treated in this record; it adds only Jacob’s statement in Genesis. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
