    "title": "Benjamin: born on the way to Ephrath, or in Padanaram?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Genesis 35 tells of Benjamin’s birth on the road from Bethel to Ephrath, after Jacob had come out of Padanaram. A few verses later it lists Jacob’s twelve sons, Benjamin among them, as those born to him in Padanaram.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 35:9: “And God appeared unto Jacob again, when he came out of Padanaram”. KJV 35:16: “And they journeyed from Bethel; and there was but a little way to come to Ephrath: and Rachel travailed”. KJV 35:18: “she called his name Benoni: but his father called him Benjamin”. Hebrew (WLC) 35:16: וַיִּסְעוּ֙ מִבֵּ֣ית אֵ֔ל וַֽיְהִי־עֹ֥וד כִּבְרַת־הָאָ֖רֶץ לָבֹ֣וא אֶפְרָ֑תָה וַתֵּ֥לֶד רָחֵ֖ל [and they journeyed from Bethel, and there was still a stretch of land to come to Ephrath, and Rachel gave birth]. KJV 35:24: “The sons of Rachel; Joseph, and Benjamin:” KJV 35:26: “And the sons of Zilpah, Leah's handmaid: Gad, and Asher: these are the sons of Jacob, which were born to him in Padanaram.” Hebrew (WLC) 35:24: בְּנֵ֣י רָחֵ֔ל יֹוסֵ֖ף וּבִנְיָמִֽן [the sons of Rachel: Joseph and Benjamin]; 35:26: אֵ֚לֶּה בְּנֵ֣י יַעֲקֹ֔ב אֲשֶׁ֥ר יֻלַּד־לֹ֖ו בְּפַדַּ֥ן אֲרָֽם [these are the sons of Jacob who were born to him in Paddan-aram]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 35:24: Υἱοὶ δὲ Ῥαχὴλ, Ἰωσὴφ, καὶ Βενιαμίν [and the sons of Rachel: Joseph and Benjamin]; 35:26: οὗτοι υἱοὶ Ἰακὼβ, οἳ ἐγένοντο αὐτῷ ἐν Μεσοποταμίᾳ τῆς Συρίας [these are the sons of Jacob, who were born to him in Mesopotamia of Syria]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/35/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN35.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The closing sentence of the list covers all the sons just named, and Benjamin is named in it. The birth story in the same chapter puts his birth in Canaan, between Bethel and Ephrath, after the return. The KJV, the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek agree at both places.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the three texts agree. The clash can be reconciled if the closing sentence speaks loosely of the sons as a group, eleven of whom were born in Padanaram; the sentence makes no exception for Benjamin. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
