    "title": "Israel bowed “upon the bed’s head” — or “upon the top of his staff”?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Genesis in the KJV says Israel bowed himself upon the bed’s head. Hebrews in the KJV says Jacob worshipped leaning upon the top of his staff. Brenton’s Greek of Genesis has the staff, as Hebrews does.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 47:31: “And he said, Swear unto me. And he sware unto him. And Israel bowed himself upon the bed's head.” Hebrew (WLC) 47:31: וַיִּשְׁתַּ֥חוּ יִשְׂרָאֵ֖ל עַל־רֹ֥אשׁ הַמִּטָּֽה [and Israel bowed down upon the head of the bed]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 47:31: καὶ προσεκύνησεν Ἰσραὴλ ἐπὶ τὸ ἄκρον τῆς ῥάβδου αὐτοῦ [and Israel bowed down upon the top of his staff]. KJV Hebrews 11:21: “By faith Jacob, when he was a dying, blessed both the sons of Joseph; and worshipped, leaning upon the top of his staff.” Greek New Testament (TISCH) Hebrews 11:21: καὶ προσεκύνησεν ἐπὶ τὸ ἄκρον τῆς ῥάβδου αὐτοῦ [and bowed down upon the top of his staff]; the TR text has the same words. KJV Genesis 48:2: “and Israel strengthened himself, and sat upon the bed”; Hebrew (WLC): וַיֵּ֖שֶׁב עַל־הַמִּטָּֽה [and sat upon the bed]; Brenton Greek: ἐκάθισεν ἐπὶ τὴν κλίνην [sat upon the bed]. KJV Exodus 4:2: “And he said, A rod”; Hebrew (WLC): וַיֹּ֖אמֶר מַטֶּֽה [and he said, A staff]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/47/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/48/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/4/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN47.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN48.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/11/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/11/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV of Genesis and the KJV of Hebrews describe the same man bowing, upon a bed’s head in one and upon a staff in the other. The KJV of Genesis follows the pointed Hebrew. Brenton’s Greek of Genesis has the words that Hebrews has. The Hebrew words for “bed” (48:2) and “staff” (Exodus 4:2) are written with the same consonants and differ only in the vowel points.",
    "scope": "G applies at Genesis 47:31. No H tag is proposed: the KJV matches the Hebrew as the edition points it. Hebrews 11:21 names the blessing of Joseph’s sons, which Genesis tells in chapter 48, and Genesis 47:31 is the oath before it; if Hebrews refers to a different moment, the two KJV verses describe two acts, though Genesis reports no bowing upon a staff. That the two Hebrew words share consonants is an observation about the letters quoted and does not show which word was first written. No claim is made about which reading is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
