    "id": "simeon-and-levi-digged-down-a-wall-or-houghed-an-ox",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Genesis 49:6",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Genesis 49:6"
    ],
    "title": "Simeon and Levi: “digged down a wall”, or hamstrung an ox?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says that in their self-will Simeon and Levi digged down a wall. The Hebrew as pointed says they hamstrung an ox, and Brenton’s Greek says they hamstrung a bull.",
    "text": "KJV Genesis 49:6: “for in their anger they slew a man, and in their selfwill they digged down a wall”. Hebrew (WLC) 49:6: וּבִרְצֹנָ֖ם עִקְּרוּ־שֹֽׁור [and in their self-will they hamstrung an ox]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 49:6: καὶ ἐν τῇ ἐπιθυμίᾳ αὐτῶν ἐνευροκόπησαν ταῦρον [and in their desire they hamstrung a bull]. The noun. KJV Exodus 21:28: “If an ox gore a man or a woman”; Hebrew (WLC): וְכִֽי־יִגַּ֨ח שֹׁ֥ור [and if an ox gore]. KJV Genesis 49:22: “whose branches run over the wall”; Hebrew (WLC): עֲלֵי־שֽׁוּר [over a wall]. The verb. KJV Joshua 11:6: “thou shalt hough their horses”; Hebrew (WLC): אֶת־סוּסֵיהֶ֣ם תְּעַקֵּ֔ר [their horses thou shalt hamstring]; Brenton Greek: τοὺς ἵππους αὐτῶν νευροκοπήσεις [their horses thou shalt hamstring]. KJV 2 Samuel 8:4: “and David houghed all the chariot horses”; Hebrew (WLC): וַיְעַקֵּ֤ר דָּוִד֙ אֶת־כָּל־הָרֶ֔כֶב [and David hamstrung all the chariot (horses)]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/49/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/21/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/6/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/10/8/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN49.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOS11.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The last two Hebrew words of the verse are a verb and a noun. The noun, as the edition points it, is the word the KJV renders “ox” at Exodus 21:28; the word the KJV renders “wall” sixteen verses later, at 49:22, has the same consonants and a different vowel. The verb is the one the KJV renders “hough” of horses at Joshua 11:6 and 2 Samuel 8:4. Brenton’s Greek has “hamstrung a bull”, using at Genesis 49:6 the verb it uses at Joshua 11:6. Destroying a wall and laming an animal are different acts.",
    "scope": "H and G both apply at Genesis 49:6: the Greek agrees with the pointed Hebrew against the KJV. The H rests on the vowel points of the edition quoted. Written without points, the nouns for “ox” and “wall” are the same letters, so the difference shown here is between the KJV and the Hebrew as the Leningrad text points it, not proof of what the consonants were first meant to say. Only the places quoted were compared for the verb; other uses of its root were not surveyed. The other verses are quoted to show how the KJV renders the two words elsewhere, not as part of the finding. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
