    "title": "Israel went up “harnessed” — or “in the fifth generation”?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says the children of Israel went up harnessed out of Egypt. Brenton’s Greek says they went up in the fifth generation. Genesis, in the KJV and in the same Greek edition, had said they would return in the fourth generation.",
    "text": "KJV Exodus 13:18: “and the children of Israel went up harnessed out of the land of Egypt”. Hebrew (WLC) 13:18: וַחֲמֻשִׁ֛ים עָל֥וּ בְנֵי־יִשְׂרָאֵ֖ל מֵאֶ֥רֶץ מִצְרָֽיִם [and in battle order, or armed, the children of Israel went up from the land of Egypt]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 13:18: πέμπτῃ δὲ γενεᾷ ἀνέβησαν οἱ υἱοὶ Ἰσραὴλ ἐκ γῆς Αἰγύπτου [and in the fifth generation the children of Israel went up out of the land of Egypt]. KJV Genesis 15:16: “But in the fourth generation they shall come hither again”. Hebrew (WLC) 15:16: וְדֹ֥ור רְבִיעִ֖י יָשׁ֣וּבוּ הֵ֑נָּה [and the fourth generation shall return here]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 15:16: Τετάρτῃ δὲ γενεᾷ ἀποστραφήσονται ὧδε [and in the fourth generation they shall return here]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/13/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/15/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO13.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN15.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV describes how the people went up: harnessed, that is, equipped. Brenton’s Greek says nothing of equipment and instead says when: in the fifth generation. The first Hebrew word quoted shares its consonants with the Hebrew word for “fifty”, which is formed from “five”; the KJV and the Greek take it in different ways. The Greek reading stands beside Genesis 15:16, where both the KJV and Brenton’s Greek have the fourth generation.",
    "scope": "G applies at Exodus 13:18. No H is proposed; the KJV’s “harnessed” is an accepted sense of the Hebrew word as pointed. “Fifth generation” at the going up and “fourth generation” at the return to Canaan need not conflict: they may be counted from different persons or to different events, and neither verse says from whom the generations are counted. No claim is made about which reading is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
