    "title": "Did they say “It is manna” — or ask “What is this?”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV the Israelites see the food and say “It is manna: for they wist not what it was”. In Brenton’s Greek they ask “What is this?”, for they did not know what it was.",
    "text": "KJV Exodus 16:15: “And when the children of Israel saw it, they said one to another, It is manna: for they wist not what it was.” KJV 16:31: “And the house of Israel called the name thereof Manna”. Hebrew (WLC) 16:15: מָ֣ן ה֔וּא כִּ֛י לֹ֥א יָדְע֖וּ מַה־ה֑וּא [“man hu”, for they did not know what it was]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 16:15: εἶπαν ἕτερος τῷ ἑτέρῳ, τί ἐστι τοῦτο; οὐ γὰρ ᾔδεισαν τί ἦν [they said one to another, What is this? for they did not know what it was]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/16/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO16.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the people make a statement and name the thing, and the reason given is that they did not know what it was. In Brenton’s Greek they ask a question, and the same reason follows. The Greek has no name for the food in this verse.",
    "scope": "G applies at Exodus 16:15. No H is proposed. The first two Hebrew words quoted can be taken as “it is man” or as “what is it?”. In the Hebrew quoted, the word the people use and the word for “what” in the clause that follows are two different words. This record does not decide between the readings. No claim is made about the origin of the name given at 16:31. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
