    "title": "Kill, adultery, steal — or adultery, steal, kill? The order of three commandments",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, with the Hebrew, three short commandments stand in the order kill, adultery, steal. Brenton’s Greek of Exodus has adultery, steal, kill. The Greek of Deuteronomy in the same edition keeps the KJV’s order, and Luke 18:20 puts adultery before killing.",
    "text": "KJV Exodus 20:13: “Thou shalt not kill.” KJV 20:14: “Thou shalt not commit adultery.” KJV 20:15: “Thou shalt not steal.” Hebrew (WLC) 20:13: לֹ֥֖א תִּֿרְצָֽ֖ח [you shall not kill]. 20:14: לֹ֣֖א תִּֿנְאָֽ֑ף [you shall not commit adultery]. 20:15: לֹ֣֖א תִּֿגְנֹֽ֔ב [you shall not steal]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 20:13: Οὐ μοιχεύσεις. [you shall not commit adultery]. 20:14: Οὐ κλέψεις. [you shall not steal]. 20:15: Οὐ φονεύσεις. [you shall not kill]. KJV Deuteronomy 5:17: “Thou shalt not kill.” KJV 5:18: “Neither shalt thou commit adultery.” KJV 5:19: “Neither shalt thou steal.” Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Deuteronomy 5:17: Οὐ φονεύσεις. 5:18: Οὐ μοιχεύσεις. 5:19: Οὐ κλέψεις. KJV Luke 18:20: “Do not commit adultery, Do not kill, Do not steal”. Greek New Testament (TISCH; TR has the same words) Luke 18:20: μὴ μοιχεύσῃς, μὴ φονεύσῃς, μὴ κλέψῃς [do not commit adultery, do not kill, do not steal]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/20/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO20.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU05.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/42/18/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/42/18/.",
    "reasoning": "The same three commandments are present in every text; their order is not the same. The KJV and the Hebrew quoted have kill, adultery, steal in Exodus and in Deuteronomy. Brenton’s Greek of Exodus has adultery, steal, kill, while its Deuteronomy keeps kill, adultery, steal. Luke 18:20, in the KJV and in the Greek, has a third order: adultery, kill, steal.",
    "scope": "G applies at Exodus 20:13, 20:14 and 20:15, where the Greek of each verse is a different commandment from the KJV’s. Deuteronomy 5:17–19 and Luke 18:20 are quoted for comparison and carry no tag. Nothing is added to or taken from the commandments, so this is a difference of order and not of content. No claim is made about which order is original or about how the commandments should be numbered. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
