    "title": "“They saw the God of Israel” — or “the place where the God of Israel stood”?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says Moses, Aaron, Nadab, Abihu and seventy elders saw the God of Israel, and again that they saw God. Brenton’s Greek says they saw the place where the God of Israel stood, and that they appeared in the place of God.",
    "text": "KJV Exodus 24:9: “Then went up Moses, and Aaron, Nadab, and Abihu, and seventy of the elders of Israel:” KJV 24:10: “And they saw the God of Israel: and there was under his feet as it were a paved work of a sapphire stone”. KJV 24:11: “also they saw God, and did eat and drink”. Hebrew (WLC) 24:10: וַיִּרְא֕וּ אֵ֖ת אֱלֹהֵ֣י יִשְׂרָאֵ֑ל [and they saw the God of Israel]. 24:11: וַֽיֶּחֱזוּ֙ אֶת־הָ֣אֱלֹהִ֔ים וַיֹּאכְל֖וּ וַיִּשְׁתּֽוּ [and they beheld God, and ate and drank]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 24:10: Καὶ εἶδον τὸν τόπον οὗ εἱστήκει ὁ Θεὸς τοῦ Ἰσραήλ [and they saw the place where the God of Israel stood]. 24:11: καὶ ὤφθησαν ἐν τῷ τόπῳ τοῦ Θεοῦ, καὶ ἔφαγον καὶ ἔπιον [and they appeared in the place of God, and ate and drank]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/24/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Twice the KJV and the Hebrew quoted say directly that the company saw God. Twice Brenton’s Greek avoids that statement: in 24:10 what is seen is a place, and in 24:11 the men are the ones seen, in the place of God. In the Greek of these two verses no one is said to see God.",
    "scope": "G applies at Exodus 24:10 and 24:11. This record is about the Greek wording only. Whether seeing God here conflicts with statements that God cannot be seen is a separate question and is not treated in this record. No H is proposed; the KJV follows the Hebrew quoted. No claim is made about which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
