    "title": "“He rested, and was refreshed” and “fainteth not, neither is weary”",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Exodus says that on the seventh day the Lord rested and was refreshed. Isaiah says the Lord, the Creator, does not faint and is not weary. Brenton’s Greek of Exodus has no word for “refreshed”: it says he ceased and rested.",
    "text": "KJV Exodus 31:17: “for in six days the Lord made heaven and earth, and on the seventh day he rested, and was refreshed”. Hebrew (WLC) 31:17: וּבַיֹּום֙ הַשְּׁבִיעִ֔י שָׁבַ֖ת וַיִּנָּפַֽשׁ [and on the seventh day he ceased and took breath]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 31:17: καὶ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ τῇ ἑβδόμῃ κατέπαυσε, καὶ ἐπαύσατο [and on the seventh day he ceased, and rested]. KJV Isaiah 40:28: “the everlasting God, the Lord, the Creator of the ends of the earth, fainteth not, neither is weary”. Hebrew (WLC) 40:28: לֹ֥א יִיעַ֖ף וְלֹ֣א יִיגָ֑ע [he does not faint and does not grow weary]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/31/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/23/40/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO31.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Being refreshed after resting can suggest recovery from tiredness, but does not necessarily assert prior fatigue. Exodus says this of the Lord after the six days of making heaven and earth. Isaiah, speaking of the same Creator, says he does not faint and is not weary. The KJV follows the Hebrew in Exodus. Brenton’s Greek of Exodus has two verbs of stopping and resting and nothing that answers to “was refreshed”.",
    "scope": "G applies at Exodus 31:17, for the Greek’s lack of “was refreshed”; Isaiah 40:28 is the other side and carries no tag. The Greek of Isaiah 40:28 was not compared. A tension arises if refreshment is taken to imply prior fatigue; the statements can be reconciled if “was refreshed” is a figure of speech for ceasing from work and not a report of recovered strength; Exodus does not say so. No H is proposed; “was refreshed” is a fair rendering of the Hebrew verb quoted. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
