    "title": "Moses “took the tabernacle” before it was made — or took his own tent?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV Moses takes “the tabernacle” and pitches it outside the camp, several chapters before the tabernacle is made and reared up. Brenton’s Greek says he took his own tent. The Hebrew says “the tent”.",
    "text": "KJV Exodus 33:7: “And Moses took the tabernacle, and pitched it without the camp, afar off from the camp, and called it the Tabernacle of the congregation.” KJV 40:17: “And it came to pass in the first month in the second year, on the first day of the month, that the tabernacle was reared up.” Hebrew (WLC) 33:7: וּמֹשֶׁה֩ יִקַּ֨ח אֶת־הָאֹ֜הֶל וְנָֽטָה־לֹ֣ו ׀ מִח֣וּץ לַֽמַּחֲנֶ֗ה [and Moses would take the tent and pitch it for himself outside the camp]; וְקָ֥רָא לֹ֖ו אֹ֣הֶל מֹועֵ֑ד [and he called it the tent of meeting]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 33:7: Καὶ λαβὼν Μωυσῆς τὴν σκηνὴν αὐτοῦ, ἔπηξεν ἔξω τῆς παρεμβολῆς [and Moses took his tent and pitched it outside the camp]; καὶ ἐκλήθν Σκηνὴ μαρτυρίου [and it was called the Tent of Witness; the verb is printed so on the page]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/2/33/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO33.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the thing Moses takes is “the tabernacle”, the word the KJV also uses for the sanctuary whose making begins in chapter 36 and which is reared up at 40:17. Brenton’s Greek has a word the KJV lacks: “his” tent, which makes it Moses’ own. The Hebrew quoted has “the tent”, with no “his”.",
    "scope": "G applies at Exodus 33:7 only. Exodus 40:17 is context. No H is proposed: the KJV uses “tabernacle” for more than one Hebrew word, and “the tent” is within what that English word can cover. The KJV’s wording can be read of an earlier tent that is not the sanctuary; the verse does not say which tent it is, and the Greek supplies an answer the KJV does not give. No claim is made about how the two tents are related or about which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
