    "title": "Sacrifices “unto devils” — the Greek says “to vain things”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says Israel shall no more offer sacrifices “unto devils”. Brenton’s Greek has “to the vain things”, a word for what is empty or worthless. The Hebrew word is the plural of the word used for the goats of the chapter before.",
    "text": "KJV Leviticus 17:7: “And they shall no more offer their sacrifices unto devils, after whom they have gone a whoring.” Hebrew (WLC) 17:7: וְלֹא־יִזְבְּח֥וּ עֹוד֙ אֶת־זִבְחֵיהֶ֔ם לַשְּׂעִירִ֕ם [and they shall no more sacrifice their sacrifices to the seirim]. Hebrew (WLC) 16:8, the same plural noun: עַל־שְׁנֵ֥י הַשְּׂעִירִ֖ם [upon the two goats]; KJV 16:8: “upon the two goats” Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 17:7: Καὶ οὐ θύσουσιν ἔτι τὰς θυσίας αὐτῶν τοῖς ματαίοις [and they shall no longer sacrifice their sacrifices to the vain things]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/3/17/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/3/16/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/LEV17.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV names the recipients of the sacrifices as “devils”, which are beings. Brenton’s Greek uses an adjective meaning vain or empty, which says the recipients are worthless and does not call them devils. The two wordings describe the objects of the forbidden worship differently.",
    "scope": "G applies at Leviticus 17:7. This compares two published wordings and is not a contradiction: both forbid the same sacrifices. No H is proposed. The Hebrew noun is the one the KJV renders “goats” at 16:8, but a shared word does not settle what it means in this verse, and it may here name beings thought of in goat form; this record does not decide. No claim is made about what was in fact worshipped. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
