    "title": "“She shall be scourged” — the Greek has no scourging and says “for them”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV the betrothed bondmaid “shall be scourged”. Brenton’s Greek has no word for scourging: it says there shall be a visitation, or looking into, “for them” — both the man and the woman.",
    "text": "KJV Leviticus 19:20: “And whosoever lieth carnally with a woman, that is a bondmaid, betrothed to an husband, and not at all redeemed, nor freedom given her; she shall be scourged; they shall not be put to death, because she was not free.” KJV 19:21: “And he shall bring his trespass offering unto the Lord” Hebrew (WLC) 19:20: בִּקֹּ֧רֶת תִּהְיֶ֛ה לֹ֥א יוּמְת֖וּ כִּי־לֹ֥א חֻפָּֽשָׁה [there shall be a biqqoreth; they shall not be put to death, because she was not freed]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 19:20: ἐπισκοπὴ ἔσται αὐτοῖς· οὐκ ἀποθανοῦνται, ὅτι οὐκ ἀπηλευθερώθη [there shall be a visitation for them; they shall not die, because she was not set free]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/3/19/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/LEV19.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV names a punishment, scourging, and lays it on the woman: “she shall be scourged”. Brenton’s Greek has a noun for a visitation or inspection, and a plural pronoun, “for them”. So the Greek neither names a beating nor singles out the woman. The Hebrew quoted has a noun and the verb “there shall be”, with no pronoun for “she”.",
    "scope": "G applies at Leviticus 19:20. This compares two published wordings. The Greek noun can be used of a visitation that punishes, so the two texts can both describe some penalty; what differs is that the KJV specifies scourging and the woman, and the Greek does not. No H is proposed: the Hebrew noun is left untranslated in the brackets because its meaning is not settled here, and the KJV’s sense is not shown to be excluded. No claim is made about how the law was applied. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
