    "scope": "G applies at Leviticus 19:26. This compares two published wordings of one clause. The two commands do not exclude each other, and the KJV forbids eating blood in other verses of Leviticus, so nothing is lost from the law as a whole in either text. The Hebrew words for “the blood” and “the mountains” are close in their letters, which may explain the difference; this record does not decide which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
