    "title": "Flying creeping things: “these may ye eat” — or “they shall not be eaten”?",
    "status": "Textual conflict",
    "summary": "Leviticus forbids flying creeping things but makes an exception: four kinds, the locust among them, may be eaten. Deuteronomy says every creeping thing that flies is unclean and shall not be eaten, and names no exception among them.",
    "text": "KJV Leviticus 11:20: “All fowls that creep, going upon all four, shall be an abomination unto you.” KJV 11:21: “Yet these may ye eat of every flying creeping thing that goeth upon all four, which have legs above their feet, to leap withal upon the earth” KJV 11:22: “Even these of them ye may eat; the locust after his kind, and the bald locust after his kind, and the beetle after his kind, and the grasshopper after his kind.” KJV 11:23: “But all other flying creeping things, which have four feet, shall be an abomination unto you.” KJV Deuteronomy 14:19: “And every creeping thing that flieth is unclean unto you: they shall not be eaten.” KJV Deuteronomy 14:20: “But of all clean fowls ye may eat.” Hebrew (WLC) Leviticus 11:21: אַ֤ךְ אֶת־זֶה֙ תֹּֽאכְל֔וּ מִכֹּל֙ שֶׁ֣רֶץ הָעֹ֔וף הַהֹלֵ֖ךְ עַל־אַרְבַּ֑ע [yet this you may eat of every swarming thing of the wing that goes on four]. Deuteronomy 14:19: וְכֹל֙ שֶׁ֣רֶץ הָעֹ֔וף טָמֵ֥א ה֖וּא לָכֶ֑ם לֹ֖א יֵאָכֵֽלוּ [and every swarming thing of the wing is unclean to you; they shall not be eaten]. Deuteronomy 14:20: כָּל־עֹ֥וף טָהֹ֖ור תֹּאכֵֽלוּ [every clean winged thing you may eat]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Leviticus 11:21: Ἀλλὰ ταῦτα φάγεσθε ἀπὸ τῶν ἑρπετῶν τῶν πετεινῶν [but these you shall eat of the creeping things among the winged]. Deuteronomy 14:19: Πάντα τὰ ἑρπετὰ τῶν πετεινῶν ἀκάθαρτά ἐστιν ὑμῖν [all the creeping things among the winged are unclean to you]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/3/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/14/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/LEV11.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU14.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted uses the same two-word phrase for the class in both books, and Brenton’s Greek does too. Leviticus takes four kinds out of that class and permits them. Deuteronomy says “every” member of the class is unclean and is not to be eaten. As worded, the locust is allowed by one law and falls under the ban of the other.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek agree with the KJV in both books. The two can be reconciled if the “clean fowls” of Deuteronomy 14:20 are meant to take in the leaping kinds that Leviticus allows; Deuteronomy does not name them or say so. They can also be reconciled if Deuteronomy gives a summary and leaves the exception to Leviticus; it does not refer to it. No claim is made about what these creatures are or about which law is older. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
