    "title": "Suburbs reaching “a thousand cubits” from the wall — the Greek of the same verse says two thousand",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, gives the suburbs of the Levite cities as a thousand cubits outward from the wall in one verse and two thousand cubits on each side in the next. Brenton’s Greek has two thousand in both verses.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 35:4: “And the suburbs of the cities, which ye shall give unto the Levites, shall reach from the wall of the city and outward a thousand cubits round about.” KJV 35:5: “And ye shall measure from without the city on the east side two thousand cubits” Hebrew (WLC) 35:4: מִקִּ֤יר הָעִיר֙ וָח֔וּצָה אֶ֥לֶף אַמָּ֖ה סָבִֽיב [from the wall of the city and outward a thousand cubits around]. 35:5: אֶת־פְּאַת־קֵ֣דְמָה אַלְפַּ֪יִם בָּֽאַמָּ֟ה [the east side two thousand by the cubit]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 35:4: ἀπὸ τείχους τῆς πόλεως καὶ ἔξω δισχιλίους πήχεις κύκλῳ [from the wall of the city and outward two thousand cubits around]. 35:5: τὸ κλίτος τὸ πρὸς ἀνατολὰς δισχιλίους πήχεις [the side toward the east two thousand cubits]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/35/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM35.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew quoted give one thousand cubits outward from the wall in verse 4 and two thousand along the named side in verse 5. Whether these describe the same dimension is not settled here. Brenton’s Greek gives two thousand in both, so the two verses carry one figure in that edition.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 35:4 only; at 35:5 the Greek agrees with the KJV. A published record already notes the two KJV figures; this record adds the Greek wording. It does not say which figure is original. The KJV’s two figures have been read as two different measurements, one outward from the wall and one along each side; the verses do not explain how they fit. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
