    "title": "Two alarms, for the east and south camps — the Greek adds a third and a fourth",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, gives a trumpet alarm for the camps on the east and a second for the camps on the south, and stops. Brenton’s Greek goes on to a third alarm for the camps by the sea and a fourth for the camps toward the north.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 10:5: “When ye blow an alarm, then the camps that lie on the east parts shall go forward.” KJV 10:6: “When ye blow an alarm the second time, then the camps that lie on the south side shall take their journey: they shall blow an alarm for their journeys.” KJV 10:22: “And the standard of the camp of the children of Ephraim set forward” KJV 10:25: “And the standard of the camp of the children of Dan set forward, which was the rereward of all the camps” Hebrew (WLC) 10:6: וּתְקַעְתֶּ֤ם תְּרוּעָה֙ שֵׁנִ֔ית וְנָֽסְעוּ֙ הַֽמַּחֲנֹ֔ות הַחֹנִ֖ים תֵּימָ֑נָה תְּרוּעָ֥ה יִתְקְע֖וּ לְמַסְעֵיהֶֽם [and you shall blow an alarm a second time, and the camps that encamp southward shall set out; an alarm they shall blow for their settings out]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 10:6: καὶ σαλπιεῖτε σημασίαν δευτέραν, καὶ ἐξαροῦσιν αἱ παρεμβολαὶ αἱ παρεμβάλλουσαι Λίβα· καὶ σαλπιεῖτε σημασίαν τρίτην, καὶ ἐξαροῦσιν αἱ παρεμβολαὶ αἱ παρεμβάλλουσαι παρὰ θάλασσαν· καὶ σαλπιεῖτε σημασίαν τετάρτην, καὶ ἐξαροῦσιν αἱ παρεμβολαὶ αἱ παρεμβάλλουσαι πρὸς Βοῤῥᾶν [and you shall sound a second signal, and the camps encamping to the south shall set out; and you shall sound a third signal, and the camps encamping by the sea shall set out; and you shall sound a fourth signal, and the camps encamping toward the north shall set out]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/10/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM10.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The trumpet law in the KJV and the Hebrew quoted names a signal for two of the four sides of the camp. Brenton’s Greek names a signal for all four. The third and fourth signals are in the Greek only.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 10:6. The KJV does say later in the chapter that two more standards, Ephraim’s and Dan’s, set forward (10:22, 10:25), but it gives no numbered trumpet signal for a third or a fourth group of camps; the Greek supplies one for each. The KJV’s closing words, “they shall blow an alarm for their journeys”, can be read as covering the remaining camps without numbering the signals. This record does not say whether the shorter or the longer text is original. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
