    "title": "“Thou mayest be to us instead of eyes” — in the Greek, “you shall be an elder among us”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, with the Hebrew, Moses asks Hobab to stay because he knows how Israel is to encamp in the wilderness and can be “instead of eyes”. In Brenton’s Greek the reason is that Hobab was with them in the wilderness, and he is to be an elder among them. The Greek has no “eyes”.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 10:29: “And Moses said unto Hobab, the son of Raguel the Midianite, Moses' father in law” KJV 10:31: “And he said, Leave us not, I pray thee; forasmuch as thou knowest how we are to encamp in the wilderness, and thou mayest be to us instead of eyes.” Hebrew (WLC) 10:31: כִּ֣י ׀ עַל־כֵּ֣ן יָדַ֗עְתָּ חֲנֹתֵ֙נוּ֙ בַּמִּדְבָּ֔ר וְהָיִ֥יתָ לָּ֖נוּ לְעֵינָֽיִם [for because you know our encamping in the wilderness, and you shall be to us for eyes]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 10:31: οὗ ἕνεκεν ἦσθα μεθʼ ἡμῶν ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ, καὶ ἔσῃ ἐν ἡμῖν πρεσβύτης [because you were with us in the wilderness, and you shall be an elder among us]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/10/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM10.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew quoted give Hobab the part of a guide: he knows the camping places and is to serve as eyes. Brenton’s Greek gives him a standing among the people and says nothing of knowing where to camp or of eyes.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 10:31. A published record sets Hobab as “eyes” beside the cloud that leads the camp; in Brenton’s Greek of this verse the guiding role is not stated. This record compares two published wordings of one verse and does not say which is original. An elder may also give guidance, so the two wordings are not shown to exclude each other. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
