    "id": "they-prophesied-and-did-not-cease-or-did-so-no-more-hebrew",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Numbers 11:25",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Numbers 11:25"
    ],
    "title": "The seventy elders “prophesied, and did not cease” — the Hebrew and the Greek say they did not do it again",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says the seventy elders prophesied “and did not cease”. The pointed Hebrew says they prophesied “and did not add”, and Brenton’s Greek says “and no longer added”: they did it then and not again. The KJV renders the same Hebrew verb at Deuteronomy 5:22 as “added no more”.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 11:25: “and it came to pass, that, when the spirit rested upon them, they prophesied, and did not cease.” Hebrew (WLC) 11:25: וַיִּֽתְנַבְּא֖וּ וְלֹ֥א יָסָֽפוּ [and they prophesied and did not add]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 11:25: καὶ ἐπροφήτευσαν, καὶ οὐκ ἔτι προσέθεντο [and they prophesied, and no longer added]. KJV Deuteronomy 5:22: “with a great voice: and he added no more” Hebrew (WLC) Deuteronomy 5:22: קֹ֥ול גָּדֹ֖ול וְלֹ֣א יָסָ֑ף [a great voice, and he did not add]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Deuteronomy 5:22: φωνὴ μεγάλη· καὶ οὐ προσέθηκε [a great voice; and he did not add]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/5/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM11.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU05.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The verb in the pointed Hebrew is the verb “to add” with a negative, the same verb, in a singular form, that the KJV renders “he added no more” at Deuteronomy 5:22, where it means the speaking stopped. Brenton’s Greek uses its own verb “to add” in both verses. On that wording the elders prophesied when the spirit rested on them and did not go on. The KJV says the opposite about how long it lasted: they “did not cease”.",
    "scope": "H and G both apply at Numbers 11:25: the Greek agrees with the pointed Hebrew against the KJV. Deuteronomy 5:22 is quoted only to show how the KJV renders the same verb form; no tag applies there. The Hebrew consonants of the verb, without the vowel points, have also been read as a different verb meaning “come to an end”, which with the negative gives the KJV’s sense; the pointed Leningrad text and Brenton’s Greek do not read it that way. This record does not say which reading is original. It concerns the seventy at the tabernacle only, not Eldad and Medad in the following verses. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
