    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, with the Hebrew, Moses says of the men opposing him that he has not taken “one ass” from them. In Brenton’s Greek he says he has not taken a desirable thing of any of them. The Greek names no animal.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 16:15: “And Moses was very wroth, and said unto the Lord, Respect not thou their offering: I have not taken one ass from them, neither have I hurt one of them.” Hebrew (WLC) 16:15: לֹ֠א חֲמֹ֨ור אֶחָ֤ד מֵהֶם֙ נָשָׂ֔אתִי [not one ass from them have I taken]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 16:15: οὐκ ἐπιθύμημα οὐδενὸς αὐτῶν εἴληφα [I have not taken a desirable thing of any one of them]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/16/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM16.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The thing Moses denies taking differs. The KJV and the Hebrew quoted name a single ass. Brenton’s Greek has a general word for something desired and no word for an ass.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 16:15. Both wordings deny that Moses took property from the men, so they agree in what Moses claims for himself; the difference is in what is named. This record compares two published wordings and does not say which is original or how the Greek wording arose. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
