    "title": "“What he did in the Red sea” — the Greek has no sea: “a war of the Lord set Zoob on fire”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV cites “the book of the wars of the Lord” for “What he did in the Red sea, and in the brooks of Arnon”. Brenton’s Greek cites “a book” without a title, and its quotation says that a war of the Lord set Zoob on fire. It has no sea.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 21:14: “Wherefore it is said in the book of the wars of the Lord, What he did in the Red sea, and in the brooks of Arnon,” KJV 21:15: “And at the stream of the brooks that goeth down to the dwelling of Ar, and lieth upon the border of Moab.” Hebrew (WLC) 21:14: עַל־כֵּן֙ יֵֽאָמַ֔ר בְּסֵ֖פֶר מִלְחֲמֹ֣ת יְהוָ֑ה אֶת־וָהֵ֣ב בְּסוּפָ֔ה וְאֶת־הַנְּחָלִ֖ים אַרְנֹֽון [therefore it is said in the book of the wars of the LORD: waheb in suphah, and the valleys, Arnon]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 21:14: Διὰ τοῦτο λέγεται ἐν βιβλίῳ, πόλεμος τοῦ Κυρίου τὴν Ζωὸβ ἐφλόγισε, καὶ τοὺς χιμάῤῥους Ἀρνῶν [Therefore it is said in a book: a war of the Lord set Zoob on fire, and the torrents of Arnon]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/21/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM21.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Three things differ. In the KJV the book has a title, “the book of the wars of the Lord”; in Brenton’s Greek the words “war of the Lord” begin the quotation and are its subject. The KJV’s quotation is about what was done “in the Red sea”; the Greek has a place or thing called Zoob and a verb, “set on fire”. And the KJV has a sea where the Greek has none.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 21:14. The Hebrew is quoted for comparison and no H is proposed. The Hebrew line as quoted has no verb for “did” and no word for “sea”; its two words waheb and suphah are left untranslated in the brackets because their meaning is not shown here, and an unresolved meaning is not a finding against the KJV. Elsewhere the KJV’s “Red sea” stands for a Hebrew phrase with the word for “sea” in it; that phrase is not in this verse. This record does not say which wording is original. The quotation is a fragment of a poem in both texts. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
