    "title": "“Let my last end be like his” — in the Greek, “let my seed be as their seed”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, with the Hebrew, Balaam wishes for the death of the righteous and that his own “last end” be “like his”. In Brenton’s Greek he wishes that his seed, his offspring, be as theirs.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 23:10: “Who can count the dust of Jacob, and the number of the fourth part of Israel? Let me die the death of the righteous, and let my last end be like his!” Hebrew (WLC) 23:10: תָּמֹ֤ת נַפְשִׁי֙ מֹ֣ות יְשָׁרִ֔ים וּתְהִ֥י אַחֲרִיתִ֖י כָּמֹֽהוּ [let my soul die the death of the upright, and let my end be like his]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 23:10: ἀποθάνοι ἡ ψυχή μου ἐν ψυχαῖς δικαίων, καὶ γένοιτο τὸ σπέρμα μου ὡς τὸ σπέρμα τούτων [may my soul die among the souls of the righteous, and may my seed be as the seed of these]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/23/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM23.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The last wish differs in its object. The KJV and the Hebrew quoted speak of Balaam’s own end. Brenton’s Greek speaks of his seed, set beside “the seed of these”. One is a wish about how he will finish; the other is a wish about those who come after him.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 23:10. This compares two published wordings of one line of a poem and does not say which is original. A wish about one’s end and a wish about one’s descendants can both be held; the record shows a difference of wording, not an incompatibility. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
