    "title": "“A Sceptre shall rise out of Israel” — in the Greek, “a man”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, pairs “a Star out of Jacob” with “a Sceptre” rising out of Israel. Brenton’s Greek has the star and then “a man” rising out of Israel.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 24:17: “there shall come a Star out of Jacob, and a Sceptre shall rise out of Israel, and shall smite the corners of Moab” Hebrew (WLC) 24:17: דָּרַ֨ךְ כֹּוכָ֜ב מִֽיַּעֲקֹ֗ב וְקָ֥ם שֵׁ֙בֶט֙ מִיִּשְׂרָאֵ֔ל [a star steps forth from Jacob, and a rod rises from Israel]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 24:17: ἀνατελεῖ ἄστρον ἐξ Ἰακὼβ, ἀναστήσεται ἄνθρωπος ἐξ Ἰσραήλ [a star shall rise out of Jacob, a man shall stand up out of Israel]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/24/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV and the Hebrew quoted the second figure is an object, a sceptre or rod. In Brenton’s Greek it is a person, “a man”. The Greek says outright that someone arises; the KJV leaves that to be drawn from the figure.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 24:17. A sceptre can stand for the one who holds it, so the two wordings can be read of the same thing; the difference shown is between a figure and a plain word. This record does not say which wording is original and makes no claim about who is meant. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
