    "title": "Balaam’s last saying has no one in view in the KJV — the Greek adds “having seen Og”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Two of Balaam’s short sayings begin with whom he looked on: Amalek, then the Kenites. The last begins in the KJV, with the Hebrew, only “And he took up his parable”. Brenton’s Greek begins it “And having seen Og”.",
    "text": "KJV Numbers 24:20: “And when he looked on Amalek, he took up his parable” KJV 24:21: “And he looked on the Kenites, and took up his parable” KJV 24:23: “And he took up his parable, and said, Alas, who shall live when God doeth this!” Hebrew (WLC) 24:23: וַיִּשָּׂ֥א מְשָׁלֹ֖ו וַיֹּאמַ֑ר [and he took up his parable and said]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 24:23: Καὶ ἰδὼν τὸν Ὢγ, καὶ ἀναλαβὼν τὴν παραβολὴν αὐτοῦ, εἶπεν [And having seen Og, and having taken up his parable, he said]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/4/24/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/NUM24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew quoted do not say whom Balaam looked on before his last saying. Brenton’s Greek names Og. Nothing in the KJV of verses 20 to 24 names Og.",
    "scope": "G applies at Numbers 24:23. The Greek states something the KJV does not state in this passage. The record does not say whether the shorter or the longer text is original, or which Og the Greek means. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
