    "title": "The Greek of Deuteronomy 6:13 adds “and thou shalt cleave to him”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Brenton’s Greek of Deuteronomy 6:13 adds a clause about cleaving to God. The KJV states that clause at 10:20, in a different passage. Its adjacent 6:14 already excludes other gods, so the Greek word “only” is context rather than a qualifying addition here.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 6:13: “Thou shalt fear the Lord thy God, and serve him, and shalt swear by his name.” KJV 6:14: “Ye shall not go after other gods” KJV 10:20: “him shalt thou serve, and to him shalt thou cleave, and swear by his name” KJV Matthew 4:10: “for it is written, Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve.” Hebrew (WLC) Deuteronomy 6:13: אֶת־יְהוָ֧ה אֱלֹהֶ֛יךָ תִּירָ֖א וְאֹתֹ֣ו תַעֲבֹ֑ד וּבִשְׁמֹ֖ו תִּשָּׁבֵֽעַ [the LORD thy God thou shalt fear, and him thou shalt serve, and by his name thou shalt swear]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Deuteronomy 6:13: Κύριον τὸν Θεόν σου φοβήθήσῃ, καὶ αὐτῷ μόνῳ λατρεύσεις, καὶ πρὸς αὐτὸν κολληθήσῃ, καὶ ἐπὶ τῷ ὀνόματι αὐτοῦ ὀμῇ [the Lord thy God shalt thou fear, and him only shalt thou serve, and to him shalt thou cleave, and by his name shalt thou swear]. Greek New Testament (TISCH) Matthew 4:10: κύριον τὸν θεόν σου προσκυνήσεις καὶ αὐτῷ μόνῳ λατρεύσεις [the Lord thy God shalt thou worship, and him only shalt thou serve]. The Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/6/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU06.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/4/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/4/.",
    "reasoning": "The words αὐτῷ μόνῳ λατρεύσεις [him only shalt thou serve] stand in Brenton’s Greek of Deuteronomy 6:13 and in Matthew 4:10. The KJV of Deuteronomy 6:13 and the Hebrew quoted have no word for “only”. The Greek of 6:13 also has a clause about cleaving to him, which the KJV has at 10:20 and not at 6:13.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 6:13 to the added clause about cleaving, which the KJV states at 10:20 in another passage. Under the direct owner ruling, an addition already stated in the same KJV passage does not qualify. Accordingly “only” is retained as verified wording context, not as the qualifying G finding. The KJV’s next verse forbids going after other gods, so the KJV passage excludes other gods without the word “only”; what the Greek adds is the word itself, in the form the Gospel quotes as written. Matthew has “worship” where both the KJV and Brenton’s Greek of Deuteronomy have “fear”; that difference is noted and not examined. The record does not say which text Matthew’s quotation was drawn from. Luke 4:8 has the same quotation and was not set out. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
