    "title": "“A Syrian ready to perish was my father” — the Greek says “my father abandoned Syria”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the words to be said with the firstfruits, the KJV, with the Hebrew, calls the forefather a Syrian who was ready to perish. Brenton’s Greek does not call him a Syrian: it says that he left Syria.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 26:5: “A Syrian ready to perish was my father, and he went down into Egypt, and sojourned there with a few” Hebrew (WLC) 26:5: אֲרַמִּי֙ אֹבֵ֣ד אָבִ֔י וַיֵּ֣רֶד מִצְרַ֔יְמָה [an Aramean perishing (or wandering) was my father, and he went down to Egypt]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 26:5: Συρίαν ἀπέβαλεν ὁ πατήρ μου, καὶ κατέβη εἰς Αἴγυπτον [Syria my father cast off, and he went down into Egypt]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/26/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU26.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV and the Hebrew quoted, “Syrian” describes the father. In Brenton’s Greek, Syria is a country and the object of a verb: the father put it away from him. The Greek has no statement that he was a Syrian and none that he was near to perishing.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 26:5. The record compares wording. It does not say which sense is original, or who the father is: the verse does not name him. Both forms agree that he went down into Egypt. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew as pointed, whether the Hebrew word is taken as perishing or as wandering. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
