    "title": "“And Moses went and spake these words” — the Greek says Moses finished speaking all these words",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says Moses “went and spake”. Brenton’s Greek has no going: it says Moses “finished speaking all these words”, wording close to the KJV’s own at 32:45.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 31:1: “And Moses went and spake these words unto all Israel.” KJV 32:45: “And Moses made an end of speaking all these words to all Israel:” Hebrew (WLC) 31:1: וַיֵּ֖לֶךְ מֹשֶׁ֑ה וַיְדַבֵּ֛ר אֶת־הַדְּבָרִ֥ים הָאֵ֖לֶּה אֶל־כָּל־יִשְׂרָאֵֽל [and Moses went and spoke these words to all Israel]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 31:1: Καὶ συνετέλεσε Μωυσῆς λαλῶν πάντας τοὺς λόγους τούτους πρὸς πάντας υἱοὺς Ἰσραὴλ [and Moses finished speaking all these words to all the sons of Israel]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/31/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU31.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew quoted open the chapter with a movement and a speech, so that “these words” can be the words that follow. Brenton’s Greek opens it with an ending, so that “all these words” can be read as the words already spoken; the wording alone does not settle the referent.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 31:1. 32:45 is context only. The record compares wording. It does not say which reading is original, or where Moses went in the KJV’s form: the verse does not say. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
