    "title": "“To me belongeth vengeance, and recompence” — the Greek says “In the day of vengeance I will recompense”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, has God say that vengeance and recompence are his. Brenton’s Greek has no “to me”: it says “In the day of vengeance I will recompense”. Romans and Hebrews quote a form with both “mine” and “I will repay”.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 32:35: “To me belongeth vengeance and recompence; their foot shall slide in due time” KJV Romans 12:19: “for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord.” KJV Hebrews 10:30: “Vengeance belongeth unto me, I will recompense” Hebrew (WLC) Deuteronomy 32:35: לִ֤י נָקָם֙ וְשִׁלֵּ֔ם לְעֵ֖ת תָּמ֣וּט רַגְלָ֑ם [mine is vengeance and recompence, at the time their foot slips]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Deuteronomy 32:35: Ἐν ἡμέρᾳ ἐκδικήσεως ἀνταποδώσω, ὅταν σφαλῇ ὁ ποὺς αὐτῶν [in the day of vengeance I will recompense, whenever their foot slips]. Greek New Testament (TISCH) Romans 12:19: ἐμοὶ ἐκδίκησις, ἐγὼ ἀνταποδώσω [mine is vengeance, I will recompense]. Hebrews 10:30: ἐμοὶ ἐκδίκησις, ἐγὼ ἀνταποδώσω. The Textus Receptus text served has the same words in both verses. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/32/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU32.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/12/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/12/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/10/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/10/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV and the Hebrew quoted state whose vengeance is. Brenton’s Greek states when recompense will be made and has a verb, “I will recompense”, where the KJV has a noun. The form quoted in Romans and Hebrews has “mine”, as in the KJV and the Hebrew, and the verb “I will recompense”, as in Brenton’s Greek.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 32:35. Romans 12:19 and Hebrews 10:30 are context: the KJV follows the Greek New Testament there. The record compares wording. In all the forms quoted the one who repays is God, so the forms do not contradict one another. It does not say which text the New Testament writers quoted from. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
