    "title": "“Rejoice, O ye nations, with his people” — the Greek of the verse adds “let all the angels of God worship him”, the words quoted in Hebrews",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Hebrews 1:6 quotes “And let all the angels of God worship him”. Those words are not in the KJV or the Hebrew of Deuteronomy 32:43. They stand word for word in Brenton’s Greek of that verse, which has four calls to rejoice or worship where the KJV has one.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 32:43: “Rejoice, O ye nations, with his people: for he will avenge the blood of his servants, and will render vengeance to his adversaries, and will be merciful unto his land, and to his people.” KJV Hebrews 1:6: “he saith, And let all the angels of God worship him.” KJV Psalm 97:7: “worship him, all ye gods” KJV Romans 15:10: “Rejoice, ye Gentiles, with his people.” Hebrew (WLC) Deuteronomy 32:43: הַרְנִ֤ינוּ גֹויִם֙ עַמֹּ֔ו כִּ֥י דַם־עֲבָדָ֖יו יִקֹּ֑ום [rejoice, nations, his people, for the blood of his servants he will avenge]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Deuteronomy 32:43: Εὐφράνθητε οὐρανοὶ ἅμα αὐτῷ, καὶ προσκυνησάτωσαν αὐτῷ πάντες ἄγγελοι Θεοῦ· εὐφράνθητε ἔθνη μετὰ τοῦ λαοῦ αὐτοῦ, καὶ ἐνισχυσάτωσαν αὐτῷ πάντες υἱοὶ Θεοῦ, ὅτι τὸ αἷμα τῶν υἱῶν αὐτοῦ ἐκδικᾶται [rejoice, heavens, with him, and let all the angels of God worship him; rejoice, nations, with his people, and let all the sons of God be strong in him, for the blood of his sons is avenged]. Greek New Testament (TISCH) Hebrews 1:6: καὶ προσκυνησάτωσαν αὐτῷ πάντες ἄγγελοι θεοῦ [and let all the angels of God worship him]. The Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/32/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU32.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/1/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/1/.",
    "reasoning": "Brenton’s Greek of the verse has a call to the heavens, a call that all the angels of God worship him, the call to the nations that the KJV has, and a call to the sons of God. The KJV and the Hebrew quoted have the call to the nations only. The second Greek call is the sentence quoted in Hebrews 1:6, with the same six Greek words in the same order.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 32:43. The other verses are context. The KJV does not state the added calls anywhere in this verse or the verses beside it. Psalm 97:7 in the KJV has “worship him, all ye gods”, which is a different wording; whether Hebrews quotes Deuteronomy in its Greek form, the Psalm, or something else is not stated in Hebrews and is not decided here. Romans 15:10 matches the one call that the KJV and the Greek share. The record does not say which form of the verse is original. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
