    "title": "“From his right hand went a fiery law for them” — the Greek says “on his right hand were angels with him”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says the Lord came “with ten thousands of saints” and that a fiery law went from his right hand. Brenton’s Greek has “with ten thousands of Kades” and, in place of the fiery law, angels at his right hand.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 33:2: “he came with ten thousands of saints: from his right hand went a fiery law for them” Hebrew (WLC) 33:2: וְאָתָ֖ה מֵרִבְבֹ֣ת קֹ֑דֶשׁ מִֽימִינֹ֕ו [אֵשְׁדָּת כ] (אֵ֥שׁ ק) (דָּ֖ת ק) לָֽמֹו [and he came from ten thousands of holiness; from his right hand — written as one word, read as two words, fire and law — for them]. The bracketed notes are the edition’s own marks for the written and the read form. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 33:2: σὺν μυρίασι Κάδης, ἐκ δεξιῶν αὐτοῦ ἄγγελοι μετʼ αὐτοῦ [with ten thousands of Kades, at his right hand angels with him]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/33/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU33.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s last clause is about a law. Brenton’s Greek has no law and no fire in the clause: it has angels. Where the KJV has “saints”, the Greek has a name, Kades.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 33:2. No H is proposed: the KJV’s “fiery law” answers to the two words the Hebrew edition gives as the read form. The written form is quoted for completeness and is not the finding. The record does not say which wording is original or what the written Hebrew form means. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
