    "title": "Moses: “he buried him” — the Greek says “they buried him”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says of Moses “he buried him”, with one burier who is not named in the verse. Brenton’s Greek has a plural: “they buried him”.",
    "text": "KJV Deuteronomy 34:5: “So Moses the servant of the Lord died there in the land of Moab, according to the word of the Lord.” KJV 34:6: “And he buried him in a valley in the land of Moab, over against Bethpeor: but no man knoweth of his sepulchre unto this day.” Hebrew (WLC) 34:6: וַיִּקְבֹּ֨ר אֹתֹ֤ו בַגַּיְ֙ בְּאֶ֣רֶץ מֹואָ֔ב מ֖וּל בֵּ֣ית פְּעֹ֑ור [and he buried him in the valley in the land of Moab over against Beth-peor]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 34:6: Καὶ ἔθαψαν αὐτὸν ἐν Γαὶ ἐγγὺς οἴκου Φογώρ [and they buried him in Gai near the house of Phogor]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/5/34/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU34.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew verb quoted is singular and the KJV renders it “he buried”. The Greek verb is plural. In the KJV the last one named before the verb is the Lord, at the end of verse 5; a plural verb cannot be read of him alone.",
    "scope": "G applies at Deuteronomy 34:6. 34:5 is context only. The record compares wording. It does not say who buried Moses in either form: neither text names the burier, and a separate record asks that question. A singular verb with no subject can also be read as “one buried him”. The Greek clause quoted has no words for “in the land of Moab”; that difference is noted and not examined. No H is proposed: the KJV follows the Hebrew. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
