    "title": "Camels 435 and asses 6,720 — the Greek of Nehemiah has no camels and 2,700 asses",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV and the Hebrew of Nehemiah count 435 camels and 6,720 asses, as Ezra 2:67 does. Brenton’s Greek of Nehemiah prints only “asses, two thousand seven hundred”.",
    "text": "KJV Nehemiah 7:69: “Their camels, four hundred thirty and five: six thousand seven hundred and twenty asses.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 7:68: גְּמַלִּ֕ים אַרְבַּ֥ע מֵאֹ֖ות שְׁלֹשִׁ֣ים וַחֲמִשָּׁ֑ה ס חֲמֹרִ֕ים שֵׁ֣שֶׁת אֲלָפִ֔ים שְׁבַ֥ע מֵאֹ֖ות וְעֶשְׂרִֽים [camels, four hundred and thirty-five; asses, six thousand seven hundred and twenty]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 7:69, the whole verse: Ὄνοι δισχίλιοι ἑπτακόσιοι. [asses, two thousand seven hundred]. KJV Ezra 2:67: “Their camels, four hundred thirty and five; their asses, six thousand seven hundred and twenty.” Septuagint (Brenton Greek) Ezra 2:67: Κάμηλοι αὐτῶν, τετρακόσιοι τριακονταπέντε· ὄνοι αὐτῶν, ἑξακισχίλιοι ἑπτακόσιοι εἴκοσι. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/16/7/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EZR17.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EZR02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Greek verse has one animal and one figure where the KJV has two animals and two figures, and its figure for the asses is 4,020 lower.",
    "scope": "G applies at Nehemiah 7:69 only. The KJV follows the Hebrew quoted, so no H is proposed. Brenton’s Greek of Ezra 2:67 has the KJV’s figures; the difference is inside the Greek of Nehemiah. The record does not say which figure is original. Brenton’s Greek prints Nehemiah as the later chapters of its Ezra pages; the address given is the page that prints this chapter. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
