    "title": "“In their rebellion appointed a captain to return to their bondage” — the Greek has “to their bondage in Egypt” and no “in their rebellion”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the prayer of Nehemiah 9 the KJV says the fathers “in their rebellion appointed a captain to return to their bondage”. Brenton’s Greek has no words for “in their rebellion”; where they would stand it has “in Egypt”.",
    "text": "KJV Nehemiah 9:17: “but hardened their necks, and in their rebellion appointed a captain to return to their bondage” Hebrew (WLC): וַיַּקְשׁוּ֙ אֶת־עָרְפָּ֔ם וַיִּתְּנוּ־רֹ֛אשׁ לָשׁ֥וּב לְעַבְדֻתָ֖ם בְּמִרְיָ֑ם [and they stiffened their neck and set a head to return to their bondage, in their rebellion]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek): καὶ ἐσκλήρυναν τὸν τράχηλον αὐτῶν, καὶ ἔδωκαν ἀρχὴν ἐπιστρέψαι εἰς δουλείαν αὐτῶν ἐν Αἰγύπτῳ [and they hardened their neck and gave a leader to return to their bondage in Egypt]. KJV Numbers 14:4: “And they said one to another, Let us make a captain, and let us return into Egypt.” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/16/9/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EZR19.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV, with the Hebrew quoted, describes the act as done “in their rebellion” and does not name the place of the bondage in this verse. The Greek names the place and does not call the act rebellion in this clause.",
    "scope": "G applies at 9:17. The two wordings can both be true of one event: the bondage meant in the KJV is, in the passage, the bondage of Egypt, and the Greek verse still speaks of hardened necks. The difference is in what this clause states. Numbers 14:4 is quoted for the proposal it reports; that verse reports what the people said, and the record does not set it against Nehemiah. Brenton’s Greek prints Nehemiah as the later chapters of its Ezra pages; the address given is the page that prints this chapter. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
