    "title": "The scribes called “in the third month, that is, the month Sivan” — the Greek has the first month, Nisan",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, dates the letters written for the Jews to the twenty-third day of the third month, Sivan. Brenton’s Greek dates them to the twenty-third day of the first month, Nisan. Haman’s letters are dated in all three texts to the thirteenth day of the first month.",
    "text": "KJV Esther 8:9: “Then were the king's scribes called at that time in the third month, that is, the month Sivan, on the three and twentieth day thereof” Hebrew (WLC) 8:9: בַּחֹ֨דֶשׁ הַשְּׁלִישִׁ֜י הוּא־חֹ֣דֶשׁ סִיוָ֗ן בִּשְׁלֹושָׁ֣ה וְעֶשְׂרִים֮ בֹּו֒ [in the third month, that is the month Sivan, on the twenty-third of it]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 8:9: Ἐκλήθησαν δὲ οἱ γραμματεῖς ἐν τῷ πρώτῳ μηνὶ, ὅς ἐστι Νισὰν, τρίτῃ καὶ εἰκάδι τοῦ αὐτοῦ ἔτους [and the scribes were called in the first month, which is Nisan, on the twenty-third, of the same year]. KJV Esther 3:12: “Then were the king's scribes called on the thirteenth day of the first month” Greek 3:12: μηνὶ πρώτῳ τῇ τρισκαιδεκάτῃ [in the first month, on the thirteenth]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/17/8/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ESG08.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ESG03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Counted from the date of Haman’s letters, the second letters follow after two months and ten days in the Hebrew and the KJV, and after ten days in the Greek. On a chronological reading of the narrative, the events told between the two dates, from Mordecai’s mourning to Haman’s death, fall in that interval.",
    "scope": "G applies at 8:9. At 3:12 the three texts agree. The intervals are counted from the dates as printed and assume both dates fall in one year, as the Greek says and the KJV does not say. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which figure or wording is original. Brenton’s Greek Esther is printed on the pages addressed; it carries passages under lettered verse numbers that have no counterpart in the Hebrew edition or in the KJV’s Esther, and quotations from them are marked by their printed labels. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
