    "id": "haman-the-agagite-or-the-bugaean-and-the-macedonian-greek",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Esther 3:1",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Esther 3:1",
      "Esther 9:24"
    ],
    "title": "“Haman the son of Hammedatha the Agagite” — the Greek has “the Bugaean” and “the Macedonian”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, calls Haman “the Agagite”. In the quoted verses Brenton’s Greek has other wording: it calls him “Bugaean” at 3:1 and 9:10 and “the Macedonian” at 9:24, and has no such word at 3:10 or 8:3.",
    "text": "KJV Esther 3:1: “Haman the son of Hammedatha the Agagite” KJV Esther 9:24: “Because Haman the son of Hammedatha, the Agagite, the enemy of all the Jews” KJV Esther 3:10: “gave it unto Haman the son of Hammedatha the Agagite, the Jews' enemy” KJV Esther 8:3: “the mischief of Haman the Agagite” KJV Esther 9:10: “The ten sons of Haman the son of Hammedatha, the enemy of the Jews” Hebrew (WLC) 3:1: אֶת־הָמָ֧ן בֶּֽן־הַמְּדָ֛תָא הָאֲגָגִ֖י; 9:24: הָמָ֨ן בֶּֽן־הַמְּדָ֜תָא הָֽאֲגָגִ֗י [Haman son of Hammedatha the Agagite, in both]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 3:1: Ἀμὰν Ἀμαδαθοῦ Βουγαῖον [Aman of Amadathes, a Bugaean]; 9:10: τοὺς δέκα υἱοὺς Ἀμὰν Ἀμαδάθοῦ Βουγαίου τοῦ ἐχθροῦ τῶν Ἰουδαίων [the ten sons of Aman of Amadathes the Bugaean, the enemy of the Jews]; 9:24: Ἀμὰν Ἀμαδαθοῦ ὁ Μακεδὼν [Aman of Amadathes the Macedonian]; 3:10: ἔδωκεν εἰς χεῖρας τῷ Ἀμὰν [gave it into the hands of Aman]; 8:3: ἀφελεῖν τὴν Ἀμὰν κακίαν [to take away the evil of Aman]. KJV 1 Samuel 15:8: “And he took Agag the king of the Amalekites alive” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/17/3/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/17/9/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ESG03.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ESG09.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ESG08.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The word that describes Haman is not the same word in Greek dress: the Greek has two other descriptions, and one of them, “the Macedonian”, names a different people. A reader of the KJV may connect “Agagite” with Agag, the king of the Amalekites in 1 Samuel; if that connection is intended, the Greek gives its reader no such pointer.",
    "scope": "G applies at 3:1 and 9:24. The verses quoted do not say what “Agagite” or “Bugaean” means, and the link between “Agagite” and the Agag of 1 Samuel 15:8 is a reading, not a statement of the text. The same man is meant in the three texts. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which figure or wording is original. Brenton’s Greek Esther is printed on the pages addressed; it carries passages under lettered verse numbers that have no counterpart in the Hebrew edition or in the KJV’s Esther, and quotations from them are marked by their printed labels. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
