    "title": "In Shushan “on the spoil laid they not their hand” — the Greek has “and they plundered”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says three times that the Jews did not take spoil. Brenton’s Greek agrees the second and third time, but at 9:10, of the first day in Shushan, it says without a negative that they plundered.",
    "text": "KJV Esther 9:10: “slew they; but on the spoil laid they not their hand” Hebrew (WLC) 9:10: וּבַ֨בִּזָּ֔ה לֹ֥א שָׁלְח֖וּ אֶת־יָדָֽם [and on the spoil they did not put forth their hand]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek), the end of 9:10 and the start of 9:11: τοῦ ἐχθροῦ τῶν Ἰουδαίων, καὶ διήρπασαν ἐν αὐτῇ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ· [the enemy of the Jews, and they plundered on that same day]. KJV Esther 9:15: “but on the prey they laid not their hand” Greek 9:15: καὶ οὐδὲν διήρπασαν [and they plundered nothing]. KJV Esther 9:16: “but they laid not their hands on the prey” Greek 9:16: καὶ οὐδὲν διήρπασαν. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/17/9/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ESG09.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 9:10 the Greek uses the same verb as at 9:15 and 9:16 but without “nothing”, so that it affirms what the Hebrew and the KJV deny. On the first day in Shushan the Jews take no spoil in the KJV and take spoil in the Greek.",
    "scope": "G applies at 9:10. At 9:15 and 9:16 the three texts agree. The Greek of 9:10 prints no object for the verb, so it does not say what was plundered or from whom. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which figure or wording is original. Brenton’s Greek Esther is printed on the pages addressed; it carries passages under lettered verse numbers that have no counterpart in the Hebrew edition or in the KJV’s Esther, and quotations from them are marked by their printed labels. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
