    "title": "“The sons of God came to present themselves before the Lord” — the Greek has “the angels of God”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says that the sons of God came to present themselves before the Lord. Brenton’s Greek says in both verses that the angels of God came.",
    "text": "KJV Job 1:6: “Now there was a day when the sons of God came to present themselves before the Lord” Hebrew (WLC) 1:6: וַיָּבֹ֙אוּ֙ בְּנֵ֣י הָאֱלֹהִ֔ים לְהִתְיַצֵּ֖ב עַל־יְהוָ֑ה [and the sons of God came to station themselves by the LORD]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 1:6: καὶ ἰδοὺ ἦλθον οἱ ἄγγελοι τοῦ Θεοῦ παραστῆναι ἐνώπιον τοῦ Κυρίου [and behold, the angels of God came to stand before the Lord]. KJV Job 2:1: “Again there was a day when the sons of God came to present themselves before the Lord” Hebrew (WLC) 2:1: וַיָּבֹ֙אוּ֙ בְּנֵ֣י הָֽאֱלֹהִ֔ים. Brenton Greek 2:1: καὶ ἦλθον οἱ ἄγγελοι τοῦ Θεοῦ παραστῆναι ἔναντι Κυρίου. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/1/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/2/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB01.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew and the KJV call those who come “the sons of God” and do not say in these two verses what kind of beings they are. The Greek calls them angels. The Greek wording names a kind of being where the KJV gives only the phrase.",
    "scope": "G applies at 1:6 and 2:1. The two wordings can describe the same company; the difference is that the Greek says what they are and the KJV in these verses does not. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The phrase also stands at Job 38:7, which was not compared here. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
