    "title": "“He will curse thee to thy face” — the Greek has “he will bless thee to thy face”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV Satan says twice that Job “will curse thee to thy face”. Brenton’s Greek prints in both places the verb of blessing, the verb it uses for “blessed be the name of the Lord” in 1:21.",
    "text": "KJV Job 1:11: “and he will curse thee to thy face” Hebrew (WLC) 1:11: אִם־לֹ֥א עַל־פָּנֶ֖יךָ יְבָרֲכֶֽךָּ. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 1:11: ἦ μὴν εἰς πρόσωπόν σε εὐλογήσει. [verily he will bless thee to thy face]. KJV Job 2:5: “and he will curse thee to thy face” Hebrew (WLC) 2:5: אִם־לֹ֥א אֶל־פָּנֶ֖יךָ יְבָרֲכֶֽךָּ. Brenton Greek 2:5: ἦ μὴν εἰς πρόσωπόν σε εὐλογήσει. For comparison, KJV Job 1:21: “blessed be the name of the Lord”; Hebrew (WLC) 1:21: יְהִ֛י שֵׁ֥ם יְהוָ֖ה מְבֹרָֽךְ; Brenton Greek 1:21: εἴη τὸ ὄνομα Κυρίου εὐλογημένον. At the two other places where the KJV of these chapters has “curse” of God, the Greek is worded otherwise. KJV 1:5: “and cursed God in their hearts”; Hebrew (WLC): וּבֵרֲכ֥וּ אֱלֹהִ֖ים בִּלְבָבָ֑ם; Brenton Greek: ἐν τῇ διανοίᾳ αὐτῶν κακὰ ἐνενόησαν πρὸς Θεόν [have thought evil things in their mind toward God]. KJV 2:9: “curse God, and die”; Hebrew (WLC): בָּרֵ֥ךְ אֱלֹהִ֖ים וָמֻֽת; Brenton Greek, printed under the label 9e: ἀλλὰ εἶπόν τι ῥῆμα εἰς Κύριον, καὶ τελεύτα. [but say some word unto the Lord, and die]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/1/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/2/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB01.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 1:11 and 2:5 the KJV has Satan foretell a curse. The Greek edition prints at both places a form of the verb it prints at 1:21 for blessing the name of the Lord. In the Hebrew edition the verb at 1:11, 2:5, 1:5 and 2:9 is written with the same three root letters (ברך) as the word the KJV renders “blessed” at 1:21.",
    "scope": "G applies at 1:11 and 2:5, where the Greek word is one of blessing and the KJV word is one of cursing. No H is tagged: a shared root does not settle what a word means in its context, and this record does not establish that the Hebrew verb cannot be used here as a manner of speaking for its opposite. The same caution applies to the Greek: the verb printed is the verb of blessing, and whether it is meant plainly or as a manner of speaking is not stated on the page. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
