    "title": "Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar: named by their places in the KJV, called kings in the Greek",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, names Job’s three friends as the Temanite, the Shuhite and the Naamathite. Brenton’s Greek calls Eliphaz and Zophar kings and Bildad a sovereign, each of a people.",
    "text": "KJV Job 2:11: “Eliphaz the Temanite, and Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite” Hebrew (WLC) 2:11: אֱלִיפַ֤ז הַתֵּימָנִי֙ וּבִלְדַּ֣ד הַשּׁוּחִ֔י וְצֹופַ֖ר הַנַּֽעֲמָתִ֑י. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2:11: Ἐλιφὰζ ὁ Θαιμανῶν βασιλεὺς, Βαλδὰδ ὁ Σαυχέων τύραννος, Σωφὰρ Μιναίων βασιλεύς· [Eliphaz the king of the Thaemans, Baldad the sovereign of the Sauchaeans, Sophar king of the Minaeans]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/2/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV verse the three are friends who come each from his own place, and nothing is said of their rank. In the Greek verse each is the ruler of a people. The Greek gives the three men a standing that the KJV verse does not give them.",
