    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says the Sabeans took Job’s oxen and asses and the Chaldeans took his camels. Brenton’s Greek names neither people: it has “the spoilers” and “the horsemen”.",
    "text": "KJV Job 1:15: “And the Sabeans fell upon them, and took them away” Hebrew (WLC) 1:15: וַתִּפֹּ֤ל שְׁבָא֙ וַתִּקָּחֵ֔ם [and Sheba fell and took them]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 1:15: καὶ ἐλθόντες οἱ αἰχμαλωτεύοντες ᾐχμαλώτευσαν αὐτὰς [and the takers of spoil came and carried them off]. KJV Job 1:17: “The Chaldeans made out three bands, and fell upon the camels, and have carried them away” Hebrew (WLC) 1:17: כַּשְׂדִּ֞ים שָׂ֣מוּ ׀ שְׁלֹשָׁ֣ה רָאשִׁ֗ים [Chaldeans set three heads]. Brenton Greek 1:17: οἱ ἱππεῖς ἐποίησαν ἡμῖν κεφαλᾶς τρεῖς, καὶ ἐκύκλωσαν τὰς καμήλους, καὶ ᾐχμαλώτευσαν αὐτὰς [the horsemen made three heads against us, and surrounded the camels, and carried them off]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew and the KJV attribute the two raids to two named peoples. The Greek attributes them to raiders it does not name. A reader of the Greek is not told who took Job’s herds.",
    "scope": "G applies at 1:15 and 1:17. The Greek does not contradict the KJV: unnamed spoilers and horsemen could be Sabeans and Chaldeans. The difference is that the two names are in the Hebrew and the KJV and not in the Greek of these verses. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
