    "id": "thou-movedst-me-against-him-or-thou-hast-said-to-destroy-his-substance-greek",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Job 2:3",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Job 2:3"
    ],
    "title": "“Thou movedst me against him, to destroy him without cause” — the Greek has “thou hast said to destroy his substance without cause”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, with the Hebrew, the Lord says to Satan that Satan moved him against Job, to destroy him. In Brenton’s Greek the Lord says that Satan spoke of destroying Job’s goods; the Greek has no clause in which the Lord is moved against Job.",
    "text": "KJV Job 2:3: “and still he holdeth fast his integrity, although thou movedst me against him, to destroy him without cause.” Hebrew (WLC) 2:3: וְעֹדֶ֙נּוּ֙ מַחֲזִ֣יק בְּתֻמָּתֹ֔ו וַתְּסִיתֵ֥נִי בֹ֖ו לְבַלְּעֹ֥ו חִנָּֽם. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 2:3: ἔτι δὲ ἔχεται ἀκακίας· σὺ δὲ εἶπας ὑπάρχοντα αὐτοῦ διακενῆς ἀπολέσαι. [and he still holds to innocence; but thou didst say to destroy his possessions for nothing]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/2/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Two things differ. In the KJV the one acted upon is the Lord (“thou movedst me”), and in the Greek Satan only speaks (“thou didst say”). In the KJV the thing to be destroyed is Job (“him”), and in the Greek it is what Job has. The KJV’s wording makes the Lord say that he was moved against a man without cause; the Greek wording does not say that.",
    "scope": "G applies at 2:3. The Hebrew quoted has the suffixes of “me” and “him” as the KJV has; its verbs are quoted and not further glossed here. The comparison is of the three wordings of this one clause; it does not settle how either wording is to be understood of God. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
