    "title": "“Ready to raise up their mourning” — the Greek has “the great sea monster”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV ends Job 3:8 with “who are ready to raise up their mourning”. Brenton’s Greek ends the verse with one who is about to master “the great sea monster”. The last Hebrew word of the verse has the same letters as the word the KJV prints as “leviathan” at Job 41:1.",
    "text": "KJV Job 3:8: “Let them curse it that curse the day, who are ready to raise up their mourning.” Hebrew (WLC) 3:8: יִקְּבֻ֥הוּ אֹרְרֵי־יֹ֑ום הָ֝עֲתִידִ֗ים עֹרֵ֥ר לִוְיָתָֽן. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 3:8: Ἀλλὰ καταράσαιτο αὐτὴν ὁ καταρώμενος τὴν ἡμέραν ἐκείνην, ὁ μέλλων τὸ μέγα κῆτος χειρώσασθαι. [But let him curse it who curses that day, he who is about to master the great sea monster]. KJV Job 41:1: “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?” Hebrew (WLC), where this verse is numbered 40:25: תִּמְשֹׁ֣ךְ לִוְיָתָ֣ן בְּחַכָּ֑ה. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/3/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/40/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV those who curse the day are ready to raise “their mourning”. In the Greek the one who curses is about to master a great creature, and no mourning is mentioned. The Hebrew word at the end of 3:8 is written with the same letters as the word at 41:1 (Hebrew 40:25), where the KJV prints the name of a creature.",
    "scope": "G applies at 3:8. No H is tagged: that the KJV prints a creature’s name for these letters in another chapter shows that the word is shared, not what it means in 3:8, and this record supplies no dictionary source. Which creature the Greek words mean is not identified here. The verse stands in a speech in verse; the comparison is of what the three texts print, not of what the speaker meant beyond the words quoted. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
