    "title": "“Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him” — the Greek has no word of trust",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV Job says that though God slay him, he will trust in him. Brenton’s Greek of the verse says that though the Mighty One lay hand on him, he will speak and plead before him; it has no statement of trust.",
    "text": "KJV Job 13:15: “Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him: but I will maintain mine own ways before him.” Hebrew (WLC) 13:15: הֵ֣ן יִ֭קְטְלֵנִי [לֹא כ] (לֹ֣ו ק) אֲיַחֵ֑ל אַךְ־דְּ֝רָכַ֗י אֶל־פָּנָ֥יו אֹוכִֽיחַ. The edition prints two forms for one word here, one marked as written (כ) and one marked as read (ק). Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 13:15: Ἐάν με χειρώσηται ὁ δυνάστης, ἐπεὶ καὶ ἦρκται, ἦ μὴν λαλήσω καὶ ἐλέγξω ἐναντίον αὐτοῦ· [Though the Mighty One lay hand on me, since he has indeed begun, verily I will speak and plead before him]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/13/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB13.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s first clause ends in a declaration of trust. The Greek verse has two verbs of speaking where the KJV has “trust” and “maintain”, and no word for trusting. A reader of the Greek does not find the declaration the KJV has.",
    "scope": "G applies at 13:15. The written and read forms in the Hebrew edition are given as background; they are not the finding, and no H is tagged. The verse stands in a speech in verse; the comparison is of what the three texts print, not of what the speaker meant beyond the words quoted. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
