    "title": "“The children’s sake of mine own body” — the Greek has “the sons of my concubines”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV Job says he intreated “for the children’s sake of mine own body”. Brenton’s Greek says he called upon “the sons of my concubines”. Job’s seven sons and three daughters are reported dead in chapter 1.",
    "text": "KJV Job 19:17: “My breath is strange to my wife, though I intreated for the children's sake of mine own body.” Hebrew (WLC) 19:17: ר֭וּחִֽי זָ֣רָה לְאִשְׁתִּ֑י וְ֝חַנֹּתִ֗י לִבְנֵ֥י בִטְנִֽי. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 19:17: Καὶ ἱκέτευον τὴν γυναῖκά μου, προσεκαλούμην δὲ … υἱοὺς παλλακίδων μου· [And I besought my wife, and called upon … the sons of my concubines]. KJV Job 1:2: “And there were born unto him seven sons and three daughters.” KJV 1:18: “Thy sons and thy daughters were eating and drinking wine in their eldest brother's house” KJV 1:19: “and it fell upon the young men, and they are dead” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/19/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB19.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s Job speaks of children of his own body in a chapter that comes after the report that his sons and daughters are dead. The Greek specifically names sons of concubines, of whom the KJV’s Job says nothing: the word “concubine” does not occur in the KJV of Job (a word search of the packaged text).",
    "scope": "G applies at 19:17. The KJV wording is not shown to conflict with chapter 1: “for the children’s sake” can be an appeal made in the name of children who have died, and the verse does not say that they are alive. The Greek specifies sons of concubines where the KJV speaks of children of his own body; these descriptions need not identify different children. The verse stands in a speech in verse; the comparison is of what the three texts print, not of what the speaker meant beyond the words quoted. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
