    "title": "“Behold now behemoth, which I made with thee” — the Greek has “beasts”, and a creature “made to be played with by his angels”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV keeps the Hebrew word as a name, “behemoth”, for one creature that God made, and says its maker can bring his sword near it. Brenton’s Greek has the plural “beasts” at 40:15 with no word of making, and at 40:19 says the creature was made to be played with by his angels.",
    "text": "KJV Job 40:15: “Behold now behemoth, which I made with thee; he eateth grass as an ox.” Hebrew (WLC) 40:15: הִנֵּה־נָ֣א בְ֭הֵמֹות אֲשֶׁר־עָשִׂ֣יתִי עִמָּ֑ךְ. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 40:15: Ἀλλὰ δὴ ἰδοὺ θηρία παρὰ σοὶ, χόρτον ἶσα βουσὶν ἐσθίουσιν. [But now behold beasts beside thee; they eat grass like oxen.] KJV Job 40:19: “He is the chief of the ways of God: he that made him can make his sword to approach unto him.” Hebrew (WLC) 40:19: ה֖וּא רֵאשִׁ֣ית דַּרְכֵי־אֵ֑ל הָ֝עֹשֹׂו יַגֵּ֥שׁ חַרְבֹּֽו. Brenton Greek 40:19: Τουτέστιν ἀρχὴ πλάσματος Κυρίου· πεποιημένον ἐγκαταπαίζεσθαι ὑπὸ τῶν ἀγγέλων αὐτοῦ. [This is the beginning of the Lord’s forming; made to be played with by his angels.] Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/40/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB40.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 40:15 the KJV names one creature and has God say “which I made with thee”; the Greek verb “eat” is plural, its noun is the plural “beasts”, and the clause about making is not in the Greek verse. At 40:19 the KJV speaks of the maker’s sword; the Greek has no sword and speaks of angels playing with the creature.",
    "scope": "G applies at 40:15 and 40:19. The Greek of 40:16–18 and 40:20–24 was not compared with the KJV here. The verse stands in a speech in verse; the comparison is of what the three texts print, not of what the speaker meant beyond the words quoted. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. A question record asks what creatures behemoth and leviathan are. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
