    "title": "“Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?” — the Greek has “a dragon”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV prints “leviathan”, preserving the Hebrew term. Brenton’s Greek uses its word for a dragon or serpent, and a halter about the nose where the KJV has a cord for the tongue.",
    "text": "KJV Job 41:1: “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook? or his tongue with a cord which thou lettest down?” The Hebrew and the Greek editions number this verse 40:25. Hebrew (WLC) 40:25: תִּמְשֹׁ֣ךְ לִוְיָתָ֣ן בְּחַכָּ֑ה וּ֝בְחֶ֗בֶל תַּשְׁקִ֥יעַ לְשֹׁנֹֽו. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 40:25: Ἄξεις δὲ δράκοντα ἐν ἀγκίστρῳ, περιθήσεις δὲ φορβαίεαν περὶ ῥῖνα αὐτοῦ; [And wilt thou bring a dragon with a hook, and put a halter about his nose?] Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/40/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB40.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV preserves the Hebrew creature term. The Greek uses a different creature term, the same noun it prints at Job 26:13 for the creature slain there (quoted in a separate record). The second half of the verse also differs: tongue and cord in the KJV, nose and halter in the Greek.",
    "scope": "G applies at 41:1 (40:25 in the Hebrew and Greek editions). The KJV’s name and the Greek’s noun may point to the same creature; the comparison does not establish that one must be a proper name and the other cannot be. The tongue/cord versus nose/halter contrast independently qualifies G. Brenton’s own English of the verse has “the serpent” for the Greek noun (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JOB40.htm). The rest of the description (KJV 41:2–34) was not compared with the Greek. A separate record compares Job 3:8, where the same Hebrew letters stand and the KJV has “their mourning”. The verse stands in a speech in verse; the comparison is of what the three texts print, not of what the speaker meant beyond the words quoted. This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
