    "title": "“Every man also gave him a piece of money, and every one an earring of gold” — the Greek has a lamb and a weight of gold",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV each visitor gives Job a piece of money and an earring of gold. In Brenton’s Greek each gives him one lamb and a four-drachm weight of gold.",
    "text": "KJV Job 42:11: “every man also gave him a piece of money, and every one an earring of gold” Hebrew (WLC) 42:11: וַיִּתְּנוּ־לֹ֗ו אִ֚ישׁ קְשִׂיטָ֣ה אֶחָ֔ת וְאִ֕ישׁ נֶ֥זֶם זָהָ֖ב אֶחָֽד. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 42:11: ἔδωκε δὲ αὐτῷ ἕκαστος ἀμνάδα μίαν, καὶ τετράδραχμον χρυσοῦ καὶ ἀσήμου. [and each gave him one lamb, and a four-drachm weight of gold, unstamped.] Earlier in the verse, KJV: “and they bemoaned him, and comforted him over all the evil that the Lord had brought upon him”; Brenton Greek: παρεκάλεσαν αὐτὸν, καὶ ἐθαύμασαν ἐπὶ πᾶσιν οἷς ἐπήγαγεν ἐπʼ αὐτῷ ὁ Κύριος· [they comforted him, and wondered at all that the Lord had brought upon him;] Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/42/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB42.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The first gift is money in the KJV and an animal in the Greek. The second is an ornament in the KJV and a weight of metal in the Greek. In the earlier clause the KJV’s visitors bemoan Job; the Greek’s visitors wonder.",
    "scope": "G applies at 42:11. No H is tagged: this record does not establish what the Hebrew word for the first gift means. The rendering “unstamped” follows Brenton’s own English of the verse (“even of unstamped gold”, https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JOB42.htm). This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
