    "title": "Job’s daughters: “Jemima … Kezia … Kerenhappuch” — the Greek has “Day”, “Casia” and “Amalthaea’s horn”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV gives the three daughters the names Jemima, Kezia and Kerenhappuch, following the sounds of the Hebrew. Brenton’s Greek gives the first the Greek word for “day” and the third a Greek phrase, “horn of Amalthaea”.",
    "text": "KJV Job 42:14: “And he called the name of the first, Jemima; and the name of the second, Kezia; and the name of the third, Kerenhappuch.” Hebrew (WLC) 42:14: וַיִּקְרָ֤א שֵׁם־הָֽאַחַת֙ יְמִימָ֔ה וְשֵׁ֥ם הַשֵּׁנִ֖ית קְצִיעָ֑ה וְשֵׁ֥ם הַשְּׁלִישִׁ֖ית קֶ֥רֶן הַפּֽוּךְ. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 42:14: Καὶ ἐκάλεσε τὴν μὲν πρώτην, Ἡμέραν· τὴν δὲ δευτέραν, Κασίαν· τὴν δὲ τρίτην, Ἀμαλθαίας κέρας. [And he called the first, Day; and the second, Casia; and the third, Amalthaea’s horn.] Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/42/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB42.htm.",
    "reasoning": "For the second daughter the Greek name has the sounds of the KJV’s. For the first and third it does not: the Greek prints ordinary Greek words, a noun and a two-word phrase, where the KJV prints names that carry the Hebrew sounds.",
    "scope": "G applies at 42:14. The same three daughters are meant in each text; the difference is in what two of them are called. This record does not say what the Hebrew names mean, and it does not say where the Greek phrase comes from; the glosses follow Brenton’s own English of the verse (“he called the first, Day, and the second, Casia, and the third, Amalthæa's horn”, https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JOB42.htm). This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
