    "title": "“After this lived Job an hundred and forty years” — the Greek has a hundred and seventy, and a total of two hundred and forty",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV, with the Hebrew, says Job lived a hundred and forty years after his trial and gives no total. Brenton’s Greek says a hundred and seventy years after it, and adds that all the years he lived were two hundred and forty.",
    "text": "KJV Job 42:16: “After this lived Job an hundred and forty years, and saw his sons, and his sons' sons, even four generations.” Hebrew (WLC) 42:16: וַיְחִ֤י אִיֹּוב֙ אַֽחֲרֵי־זֹ֔את מֵאָ֥ה וְאַרְבָּעִ֖ים שָׁנָ֑ה [and Job lived after this a hundred and forty years]. Septuagint (Brenton Greek) 42:16: Ἔζησε δὲ Ἰὼβ μετὰ τὴν πληγὴν ἔτη ἑκατὸν ἑβδομήκοντα· τὰ δὲ πάντα ἔτη ἔζησε, διακόσια τεσσράκοντα· [And Job lived after the affliction a hundred and seventy years; and all the years he lived were two hundred and forty;] Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/42/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB42.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The figure for the years after the trial is 140 in the KJV and 170 in the Greek. The Greek also states a whole lifetime, 240 years; the KJV verse states none.",
    "scope": "G applies at 42:16. The Greek’s two figures leave seventy years before the affliction, if both are counted to the same point; the Greek does not state that figure and the KJV gives no age for Job before his trial in the verses compared (1:1–5, 42:10–17). This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
