    "title": "“Kiss the Son, lest he be angry” — the Greek has no son here: “Lay hold of instruction, lest the Lord be angry”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV tells the kings of the earth to “Kiss the Son, lest he be angry”. Brenton’s Greek has no word for a son or for kissing in the verse: it says to lay hold of instruction, lest the Lord be angry, and it speaks of “the righteous way”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 2:12: “Kiss the Son, lest he be angry, and ye perish from the way, when his wrath is kindled but a little. Blessed are all they that put their trust in him.” Hebrew (WLC) 2:12: נַשְּׁקוּ־בַ֡ר פֶּן־יֶאֱנַ֤ף. Brenton’s Greek 2:12: Δράξασθε παιδείας, μή ποτε ὀργισθῇ Κύριος, καὶ ἀπολεῖσθε ἐξ ὁδοῦ δικαίας [Lay hold of instruction, lest the Lord be angry and you perish from the righteous way]. KJV Psalms 2:7: “Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee” Brenton’s Greek 2:7: υἱός μου εἶ σὺ, ἐγὼ σήμερον γεγέννηκά σε [Thou art my son, today I have begotten thee]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/2/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA002.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV commands “Kiss the Son” and then says “lest he be angry”; taking “he” as the Son is a contextual reading, not an explicitly named subject. The Greek commands acceptance of instruction and explicitly names the Lord as the one who may be angry. The command differs, and the Greek explicitly names the subject where the KJV uses a pronoun.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Both texts have “Thou art my Son” at verse 7, so the Greek psalm does speak of a son; it does not do so in verse 12. The Hebrew is quoted as printed; this record does not say what its second word means and is not tagged for the Hebrew. The KJV has “from the way” where the Greek has “from the righteous way”. No numbering difference in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
