    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm says “Stand in awe, and sin not”. Brenton’s Greek has a command to be angry in that place, in the same Greek words that stand at Ephesians 4:26, where the KJV has “Be ye angry, and sin not”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 4:4: “Stand in awe, and sin not: commune with your own heart upon your bed, and be still. Selah.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 4:5 because the title is counted as verse 1: רִגְז֗וּ וְֽאַל־תֶּ֫חֱטָ֥אוּ. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 4:5: Ὀργίζεσθε καὶ μὴ ἁμαρτάνετε· ἃ λέγετε ἐν ταῖς καρδίαις ὑμῶν, ἐπὶ ταῖς κοίταις ὑμῶν κατανύγητε [Be angry and do not sin; for what you say in your hearts, feel remorse on your beds]. KJV Ephesians 4:26: “Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath:” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Ephesians 4:26: ὀργίζεσθε καὶ μὴ ἁμαρτάνετε; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/4/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA004.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/49/4/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/49/4/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV psalm commands awe; the Greek psalm commands, or allows, anger. The four Greek words are the same in the Greek psalm and in Ephesians, and the KJV renders them “Be ye angry, and sin not” there. A reader of the KJV alone does not find the Ephesians sentence in the psalm.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Ephesians does not name the psalm, and identical words do not prove a quotation. The Hebrew is quoted as printed; this record does not say which emotion its verb names and is not tagged for the Hebrew. The second half of the verse also differs (“commune with your own heart upon your bed, and be still” in the KJV). The Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
