    "title": "“God is angry with the wicked every day” — the Greek says he is patient, “not bringing on wrath every day”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says God “is angry with the wicked every day”. Brenton’s Greek describes the daily expression of wrath differently: God is a righteous judge, strong and patient, “not” bringing wrath every day.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 7:11: “God judgeth the righteous, and God is angry with the wicked every day.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 7:12: אֱ֭לֹהִים שֹׁופֵ֣ט צַדִּ֑יק וְ֝אֵ֗ל זֹעֵ֥ם בְּכָל־יֹֽום. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 7:12: Ὁ Θεὸς κριτὴς δίκαιος, καὶ ἰσχυρὸς, καὶ μακρόθυμος, μὴ ὀργὴν ἐπάγων καθʼ ἑκάστην ἡμέραν [God is a righteous judge, and strong, and patient, not bringing on wrath each day]. KJV Psalms 7:12: “If he turn not, he will whet his sword; he hath bent his bow, and made it ready.” Brenton’s Greek, numbered 7:13: τὴν ῥομφαίαν αὐτοῦ στιλβώσει, τὸ τόξον αὐτοῦ ἐνέτεινε [he will polish his sword; he has bent his bow]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/7/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA007.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV affirms anger every day. The Greek has a negative (μὴ) before the bringing of wrath and has two words the KJV verse does not have, “strong” and “patient”. Being angry and bringing wrath are not necessarily the same act; the negative and the added description of patience make the Greek wording distinct without establishing incompatible claims.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The KJV’s words “with the wicked” have no separate word in the Hebrew quoted; that is stated for completeness and is not the finding, and the record is not tagged for the Hebrew. The Hebrew consonants אל can be pointed as the word for God or as a negative; the Leningrad text points them as the word for God, and this record does not say how the Greek wording arose. The next verse in both texts speaks of a sword and a bow, so the Greek psalm does not say that God never acts in wrath. The Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
