    "title": "“Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou ordained strength” — the Greek psalm and Matthew 21:16 have “perfected praise”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm says God has “ordained strength” out of the mouth of babes. Brenton’s Greek has “prepared praise”, and Matthew 21:16 gives the saying in those Greek words, which the KJV renders “thou hast perfected praise”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 8:2: “Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou ordained strength because of thine enemies, that thou mightest still the enemy and the avenger.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 8:3: מִפִּ֤י עֹֽולְלִ֨ים ׀ וְֽיֹנְקִים֮ יִסַּ֪דְתָּ֫ עֹ֥ז. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 8:3: Ἐκ στόματος νηπίων καὶ θηλαζόντων κατηρτίσω αἶνον [Out of the mouth of infants and sucklings thou hast prepared praise]. KJV Matthew 21:16: “have ye never read, Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings thou hast perfected praise?” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Matthew 21:16: ἐκ στόματος νηπίων καὶ θηλαζόντων κατηρτίσω αἶνον; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/8/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA008.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/21/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/21/.",
    "reasoning": "Matthew presents the sentence as something read (“have ye never read”). The KJV’s own psalm does not have the sentence as Matthew gives it: it has “ordained strength” where Matthew has “perfected praise”. The Greek psalm has the words Matthew has.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Strength and praise are different things said to come from the infants’ mouths; this record does not say that either wording is wrong, and a reader may hold that praise is a kind of strength. Matthew does not name the psalm. The KJV’s “strength” follows the Hebrew noun quoted; the record is not tagged for the Hebrew. The Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
