    "title": "“Put them in fear, O Lord” — the Greek asks for a lawgiver: “Appoint, O Lord, a lawgiver over them”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV prays that the Lord would put the nations in fear. Brenton’s Greek prays that the Lord would set a lawgiver over them.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 9:20: “Put them in fear, O Lord: that the nations may know themselves to be but men. Selah.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 9:21: שִׁ֘יתָ֤ה יְהוָ֨ה ׀ מֹורָ֗ה לָ֫הֶ֥ם. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 9:21: Κατάστησον, Κύριε, νομοθέτην ἐπʼ αὐτοὺς, γνώτωσαν ἔθνη ὅτι ἀνθρωποί εἰσι [Appoint, O Lord, a lawgiver over them; let the nations know that they are men]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/9/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA009.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV asks for a feeling to be put into the nations. The Greek asks for a person to be set over them, one who gives law. The requests are different in kind: fear in the one, a lawgiver in the other.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Hebrew is quoted as printed; this record does not say what its third word means and is not tagged for the Hebrew. The two requests are not exclusive. The Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm; Brenton’s Greek prints KJV Psalms 9 and 10 together as its Psalm 9. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
