    "title": "“Thou shalt keep them, O Lord, thou shalt preserve them” — the Greek has “keep us” and “preserve us”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV says the Lord will keep “them” and preserve “them”. Brenton’s Greek has “us” both times, so the ones kept are the speakers.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 12:5: “For the oppression of the poor, for the sighing of the needy, now will I arise, saith the Lord” KJV 12:6: “The words of the Lord are pure words: as silver tried in a furnace of earth, purified seven times.” KJV 12:7: “Thou shalt keep them, O Lord, thou shalt preserve them from this generation for ever.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 12:8: אַתָּֽה־יְהוָ֥ה תִּשְׁמְרֵ֑ם תִּצְּרֶ֓נּוּ ׀ מִן־הַדֹּ֖ור ז֣וּ לְעֹולָֽם. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 11:8: Σὺ Κύριε φυλάξεις ἡμᾶς· καὶ διατηρήσεις ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ τῆς γενεᾶς ταύτης, καὶ εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα [Thou, O Lord, wilt keep us, and wilt preserve us from this generation and for ever]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/12/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA011.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the ones kept are spoken of in the third person, after a verse about “the words of the Lord” and a verse about the poor and needy. In the Greek the ones kept are “us”. The Greek wording cannot refer to the words of verse 6; the KJV wording leaves a reader to decide what “them” refers to.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. In the Hebrew quoted the two verbs carry two different endings (the first ends in ם, the second in נו); this record does not say how the second ending is to be read and is not tagged for the Hebrew. A separate question record asks what “them” refers to in the KJV. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 11 (KJV Psalm 12). The Hebrew and the Greek also run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
