    "title": "“There is none that doeth good, no, not one” — the Greek verse goes on with six more lines, the ones that follow in Romans 3:13–18",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, with the Hebrew, Psalm 14:3 ends at “no, not one”. In Brenton’s Greek the same verse continues with the lines about the open sepulchre, the poison of asps, the feet swift to shed blood and no fear of God. Romans 3:12–18 has the whole run in that order under “As it is written”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 14:3: “They are all gone aside, they are all together become filthy: there is none that doeth good, no, not one.” Hebrew (WLC) 14:3, the end of the verse: אֵ֤ין עֹֽשֵׂה־טֹ֑וב אֵ֝֗ין גַּם־אֶחָֽד. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 13:3: οὐκ ἔστι ποιῶν χρηστότητα, οὐκ ἔστιν ἕως ἑνός· τάφος ἀνεῳγμένος ὁ λάρυγξ αὐτῶν, ταῖς γλώσσαις αὐτῶν ἐδολιοῦσαν, ἰὸς ἀσπίδων ὑπὸ τὰ χείλη αὐτῶν· ὧν τὸ στόμα ἀρᾶς καὶ πικρίας γέμει, ὀξεῖς οἱ πόδες αὐτῶν ἐκχέαι αἷμα· σύντριμμα καὶ ταλαιπωρία ἐν ταῖς ὁδοῖς αὐτῶν, καὶ ὁδὸν εἰρήνης οὐκ ἔγνωσαν· οὐκ ἔστι φόβος Θεοῦ ἀπέναντι τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν αὐτῶν [there is none doing good, there is not even one. Their throat is an opened grave, with their tongues they have deceived, the poison of asps is under their lips, whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness; their feet are swift to shed blood; ruin and misery are in their ways, and the way of peace they have not known; there is no fear of God before their eyes]. KJV Romans 3:10: “As it is written” KJV 3:12: “there is none that doeth good, no, not one.” KJV 3:13: “Their throat is an open sepulchre; with their tongues they have used deceit; the poison of asps is under their lips:” KJV 3:14: “Whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness:” KJV 3:15: “Their feet are swift to shed blood:” KJV 3:16: “Destruction and misery are in their ways:” KJV 3:17: “And the way of peace have they not known:” KJV 3:18: “There is no fear of God before their eyes.” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Romans 3:13: τάφος ἀνεῳγμένος ὁ λάρυγξ αὐτῶν; Romans 3:18: οὐκ ἔστιν φόβος θεοῦ ἀπέναντι τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν αὐτῶν; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words in both places. Where the KJV has like lines in other places. Psalms 5:9: “their throat is an open sepulchre; they flatter with their tongue” Psalms 140:3: “adders' poison is under their lips” Psalms 10:7: “His mouth is full of cursing and deceit and fraud” Isaiah 59:7: “Their feet run to evil, and they make haste to shed innocent blood” Isaiah 59:8: “The way of peace they know not” Psalms 36:1: “there is no fear of God before his eyes” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/14/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA013.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/3/.",
    "reasoning": "The Greek psalm has, inside one verse, six lines that the KJV psalm and the Hebrew verse quoted do not have. Romans 3:12–18 has the end of the psalm verse and then those six lines, in the Greek psalm’s order. The like lines quoted here from the KJV Old Testament stand in six separate places, none of them in Psalm 14.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The record does not say whether the Greek psalm took the lines from Romans, Romans from a Greek psalm, or both from the scattered passages; the texts quoted do not settle it. “As it is written” in Romans does not name a book. The six other places are quoted in the KJV only; their Hebrew and Greek were not compared, and their wording is like, not identical to, the Romans lines. KJV Psalm 53:3 repeats Psalm 14:3; it was not compared here. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 13 (KJV Psalm 14). No difference of verse number at 14:3. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
