    "title": "“Their line is gone out through all the earth” — the Greek psalm and Romans 10:18 have “their sound”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm says the heavens’ “line” has gone out through all the earth. Brenton’s Greek has “their sound”, and Romans 10:18 gives the sentence in the Greek psalm’s words, which the KJV renders “their sound went into all the earth”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 19:3: “There is no speech nor language, where their voice is not heard.” KJV 19:4: “Their line is gone out through all the earth, and their words to the end of the world.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 19:5: בְּכָל־הָאָ֨רֶץ ׀ יָ֘צָ֤א קַוָּ֗ם וּבִקְצֵ֣ה תֵ֭בֵל מִלֵּיהֶ֑ם. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 18:5: Εἰς πᾶσαν τὴν γῆν ἐξῆλθεν ὁ φθόγγος αὐτῶν, καὶ εἰς τὰ πέρατα τῆς οἰκουμένης τὰ ῥήματα αὐτῶν [Into all the earth their sound went out, and to the ends of the inhabited world their words]. KJV Romans 10:18: “But I say, Have they not heard? Yes verily, their sound went into all the earth, and their words unto the ends of the world.” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Romans 10:18: εἰς πᾶσαν τὴν γῆν ἐξῆλθεν ὁ φθόγγος αὐτῶν, καὶ εἰς τὰ πέρατα τῆς οἰκουμένης τὰ ῥήματα αὐτῶν; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/19/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA018.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/10/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/10/.",
    "reasoning": "Romans has the sentence word for word as the Greek psalm prints it. The KJV’s Romans says “their sound”; the KJV’s psalm says “Their line”. A line and a sound are different things, and the KJV reader finds one in the psalm and the other in the letter.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Romans does not name the psalm. The KJV’s “line” follows the Hebrew noun quoted; the record is not tagged for the Hebrew, and it does not say what the line is. KJV 19:3 already speaks of a “voice”, so the idea of sound is in the KJV passage; what differs is the word in verse 4. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 18 (KJV Psalm 19). The Hebrew and the Greek also run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
