    "summary": "The KJV calls on “ye mighty” to give glory to the Lord. Brenton’s Greek calls on “sons of God”, and it has a line the KJV lacks, telling them to bring “sons of rams”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 29:1: “Give unto the Lord, O ye mighty, give unto the Lord glory and strength.” Hebrew (WLC) 29:1, after the title: הָב֣וּ לַֽ֭יהוָה בְּנֵ֣י אֵלִ֑ים הָב֥וּ לַ֝יהוָ֗ה כָּבֹ֥וד וָעֹֽז. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 28:1, after the title: Ἐνέγκατε τῷ Κυρίῳ υἱοὶ Θεοῦ [Bring to the Lord, sons of God] … υἱοὺς κριῶν [sons of rams] … ἐνέγκατε τῷ Κυρίῳ δόξαν καὶ τιμὴν [bring to the Lord glory and honour]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/29/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA028.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV those addressed are “ye mighty”, and they are asked for two things, glory and strength. In the Greek those addressed are called sons of God, and they are asked for three things, the first of them animals for offering. The Greek verse has three “bring” clauses where the KJV has two “give” clauses.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Hebrew quoted has two clauses, as the KJV has, and the phrase בני אלים where the KJV has “O ye mighty”; this record does not say what that phrase means and is not tagged for the Hebrew. It does not say who the “sons of God” are. The page prints the word before “sons of rams” as “Κρίῳ”; that word is left out of the quotation. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 28 (KJV Psalm 29). No difference of verse number at 29:1; the Hebrew and the Greek print a title inside verse 1. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
