    "title": "“Mine ears hast thou opened” — the Greek psalm and Hebrews 10:5 have “a body hast thou prepared me”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm says “mine ears hast thou opened”. Brenton’s Greek has “a body thou hast prepared for me”, and Hebrews 10:5 gives the sentence in the Greek psalm’s words, which the KJV renders “a body hast thou prepared me”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 40:6: “Sacrifice and offering thou didst not desire; mine ears hast thou opened: burnt offering and sin offering hast thou not required.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 40:7: זֶ֤בַח וּמִנְחָ֨ה ׀ לֹֽא־חָפַ֗צְתָּ אָ֭זְנַיִם כָּרִ֣יתָ לִּ֑י. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 39:7: Θυσίαν καὶ προσφορὰν οὐκ ἠθέλησας, σῶμα δὲ κατηρτίσω μοι [Sacrifice and offering thou didst not wish, but a body thou didst prepare for me]. KJV Hebrews 10:5: “Wherefore when he cometh into the world, he saith, Sacrifice and offering thou wouldest not, but a body hast thou prepared me:” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Hebrews 10:5: θυσίαν καὶ προσφορὰν οὐκ ἠθέλησας, σῶμα δὲ κατηρτίσω μοι; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. KJV Hebrews 10:7 continues with the next verse of the psalm: “Then said I, Lo, I come (in the volume of the book it is written of me,)” KJV Psalms 40:7: “Then said I, Lo, I come: in the volume of the book it is written of me,” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/40/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA039.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/10/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/10/.",
    "reasoning": "Hebrews gives the sentence as spoken words and builds on “body”. The KJV’s own psalm does not have a body in the verse: it has opened ears. The Greek psalm has the words Hebrews has. A reader who turns from the KJV’s Hebrews to the KJV’s psalm finds a different clause.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The KJV’s “ears” follows the Hebrew noun quoted; the record is not tagged for the Hebrew. This record does not say that the two clauses cannot express one thought, and it does not say how the Greek wording arose. Hebrews does not name the psalm. The rest of Hebrews 10:5–9 was not compared with the psalm word by word. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 39 (KJV Psalm 40). The Hebrew and the Greek also run one verse ahead of the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
