    "title": "“In the volume of the book it is written of me” — which book, and what is written there?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The speaker of Psalm 40 says that something is written of him “in the volume of the book”. The verses quoted do not name the book or say what it has written. Hebrews 10:7 repeats the sentence and does not name the book either.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 40:6: “Sacrifice and offering thou didst not desire; mine ears hast thou opened: burnt offering and sin offering hast thou not required.” KJV 40:7: “Then said I, Lo, I come: in the volume of the book it is written of me,” KJV 40:8: “I delight to do thy will, O my God: yea, thy law is within my heart.” KJV Hebrews 10:5: “Wherefore when he cometh into the world, he saith” KJV Hebrews 10:7: “Then said I, Lo, I come (in the volume of the book it is written of me,) to do thy will, O God.” Hebrew (WLC), numbered 40:8: בִּמְגִלַּת־סֵ֝֗פֶר כָּת֥וּב עָלָֽי. Brenton’s Greek, numbered 39:8: ἐν κεφαλίδι βιβλίου γέγραπται περὶ ἐμοῦ. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/40/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA039.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The speaker appeals to a writing as the ground of his coming (“Lo, I come”), set against sacrifice and offering. Which writing it is decides what he claims: that a law binds him as it binds others, or that a book speaks of him in particular. The phrase “the volume of the book” is printed only twice in the packaged KJV, here and where Hebrews repeats it.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Possible readings: that the book is the law, in which a duty is written for the speaker (verse 8 goes on, “thy law is within my heart”); that it is a writing that foretells the speaker; and that it is a record kept by God. Each is an interpretation; the verses quoted state none of them, and this record asserts none. Hebrews 10:5 brings the words in with “when he cometh into the world, he saith”, so Hebrews gives the words to the one it is speaking of there; the verses of Hebrews quoted repeat “the volume of the book” and do not say which book it is. The Hebrew and the Greek quoted have the same clause and name no book. A separate record compares the Greek of verse 6. The psalm is verse. Check made: Psalms 40:6–8 and Hebrews 10:5–7 were read in the packaged KJV, and the packaged KJV was searched for the phrase “volume of the book” (these two verses only). That is a word search, not a reading of the whole Bible. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 39, and the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
