    "title": "“The help of his countenance” — the Greek has “the salvation of my countenance”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "At Psalm 42:5 the KJV and the pointed Hebrew have “his countenance”. Brenton’s Greek of the verse has “my countenance”, as the KJV, the Hebrew and the Greek all have at 42:11.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 42:5: “Why art thou cast down, O my soul? and why art thou disquieted in me? hope thou in God: for I shall yet praise him for the help of his countenance.” KJV 42:11: “who is the health of my countenance, and my God” Hebrew (WLC) 42:6: יְשׁוּעֹ֥ות פָּנָֽיו. Hebrew (WLC) 42:12: יְשׁוּעֹ֥ת פָּ֝נַ֗י וֵֽאלֹהָֽי. Brenton’s Greek 41:6: σωτήριον τοῦ προσώπου μου [the salvation of my face]. Brenton’s Greek 41:12: ἡ σωτηρία τοῦ προσώπου μου, καὶ ὁ Θεός μου [the salvation of my face, and my God]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/42/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA041.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The refrain of Psalms 42 and 43 is worded twice here. In the KJV and the pointed Hebrew the first has “his” and the second “my”; in the Greek both have “my”. The two refrains are therefore alike in the Greek and unlike in the KJV.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Greek of 41:6 ends the verse at “my face” and puts “my God” at the head of the next verse, while at the second quoted refrain the KJV puts “and my God” at the end of 42:11. The record compares these printed words and does not say what letters stood in any source of the Greek. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 41 (KJV Psalm 42); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
