    "title": "“Even the people of the God of Abraham” — the Greek has the rulers assembled “with the God of Abraam”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV the princes of the people are gathered together, “even the people of the God of Abraham”, and “the shields of the earth” belong to God. In Brenton’s Greek the rulers are assembled “with the God of Abraam”, and “the mighty ones of the earth” are exalted.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 47:9: “The princes of the people are gathered together, even the people of the God of Abraham: for the shields of the earth belong unto God: he is greatly exalted.” Hebrew (WLC) 47:10: נְדִ֘יבֵ֤י עַמִּ֨ים ׀ נֶאֱסָ֗פוּ עַם֮ אֱלֹהֵ֪י אַבְרָ֫הָ֥ם כִּ֣י לֵֽ֭אלֹהִים מָֽגִנֵּי־אֶ֗רֶץ מְאֹ֣ד נַעֲלָֽה. Brenton’s Greek 46:10: Ἄρχοντες λαῶν συνήχθησαν μετὰ τοῦ Θεοῦ Ἀβραὰμ [Rulers of peoples were gathered with the God of Abraham]; ὅτι τοῦ Θεοῦ οἱ κραταιοὶ τῆς γῆς σφόδρα ἐπῄρθησαν [for the mighty ones of the earth belong to God; they are greatly exalted]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/47/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA046.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the gathered princes are joined to a people: “even the people of the God of Abraham”. In the Greek the rulers are gathered “with” the God of Abraham, with no word for a people after them. The KJV’s second clause names “shields”; the Greek names “the mighty ones”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Hebrew words quoted run from “princes of peoples” to “the God of Abraham” with one word between them, which the KJV renders “even the people of”. This record compares the English printed at each place; it does not decide the Hebrew sense. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 46 (KJV Psalm 47); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
