    "title": "“The cattle upon a thousand hills” — the Greek has “the cattle on the mountains, and oxen”, and “the beauty of the field”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV has “a thousand hills” at 50:10 and “the wild beasts of the field” at 50:11. In Brenton’s Greek the corresponding 49:10 has no thousand and ends with “and oxen”, and 49:11 has “the beauty of the field” and the birds “of the sky”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 50:10: “For every beast of the forest is mine, and the cattle upon a thousand hills.” KJV 50:11: “I know all the fowls of the mountains: and the wild beasts of the field are mine.” Hebrew (WLC) 50:10: בְּ֝הֵמֹ֗ות בְּהַרְרֵי־אָֽלֶף. Hebrew (WLC) 50:11: יָ֭דַעְתִּי כָּל־עֹ֣וף הָרִ֑ים. Brenton’s Greek 49:10: κτήνη ἐν τοῖς ὄρεσι, καὶ βόες [cattle on the mountains, and oxen]. Brenton’s Greek 49:11: Ἔγνωκα πάντα τὰ πετεινὰ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ, καὶ ὡραιότης ἀγροῦ μετʼ ἐμοῦ ἐστιν [I have known all the birds of the sky, and the beauty of the field is with me]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/50/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA049.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 50:10 the KJV states a number (“a thousand”) that the Greek does not print. At 50:11 the KJV has the birds of “the mountains” and “the wild beasts of the field”; the Greek has the birds of “the sky” and “the beauty of the field”, which names no beast.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. A thousand hills is a figure of many, not a count, and this record does not treat it as one; it records that the Greek prints no number. The Hebrew words at the end of 50:11 are not quoted, because the digital text served prints them in a form the quotation helper could not match safely; the KJV and the Greek are quoted in full for that clause. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 49 (KJV Psalm 50); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
