    "title": "“Be clear when thou judgest” — the Greek psalm and Romans 3:4 have “overcome when thou art judged”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm says God is “justified when thou speakest, and be clear when thou judgest”. Brenton’s Greek of the psalm, and Romans 3:4 in Greek, have “in thy sayings” and “overcome when thou art judged”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 51:4: “that thou mightest be justified when thou speakest, and be clear when thou judgest” Hebrew (WLC) 51:6: לְ֭מַעַן תִּצְדַּ֥ק בְּדָבְרֶ֗ךָ תִּזְכֶּ֥ה בְשָׁפְטֶֽךָ. Brenton’s Greek 50:6: ὅπως ἂν δικαιωθῇς ἐν τοῖς λόγοις σου, καὶ νικήσῃς ἐν τῷ κρίνεσθαί σε [that thou mightest be justified in thy sayings, and mightest prevail when thou art judged]. KJV Romans 3:4: “That thou mightest be justified in thy sayings, and mightest overcome when thou art judged” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Romans 3:4: ὅπως ἂν δικαιωθῇς ἐν τοῖς λόγοις σου … νικήσεις ἐν τῷ κρίνεσθαί σε; the Textus Receptus text served has the same first words and prints the verb as νικησης εν τω κρινεσθαι σε. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/51/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA050.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/3/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV psalm has God as the one who speaks and who judges. The Greek psalm and the Greek of Romans 3:4 have God as the one who is judged (“when thou art judged”). The KJV’s own English of Romans 3:4 has the passive form that the KJV psalm does not.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The last Hebrew word of the verse carries the ending for “thy”; this record does not say how the Hebrew letters are to be taken beyond quoting them. The Greek verb of Romans 3:4 differs between the two New Testament editions served (one prints a form ending in -εις, the other -ῃς), and the Greek psalm has the form that the Textus Receptus text has. The record does not say what David or Paul meant. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 50 (KJV Psalm 51); the Hebrew and the Greek run two verses ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
