    "summary": "The KJV has “we took sweet counsel together”, with the man of verse 13 and the speaker as “we”. Brenton’s Greek has a second-person verb and a word for foods: “who didst sweeten foods together”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 55:13: “But it was thou, a man mine equal, my guide, and mine acquaintance.” KJV 55:14: “We took sweet counsel together, and walked unto the house of God in company.” Hebrew (WLC) 55:15: אֲשֶׁ֣ר יַ֭חְדָּו נַמְתִּ֣יק סֹ֑וד. Brenton’s Greek 54:15: ὃς ἐπιτοαυτὸ ἐγλύκανας ἐδέσματα [who together didst sweeten foods]; ἐν τῷ οἴκῳ τοῦ Θεοῦ ἐπορεύθημεν ἐν ὁμονοίᾳ [in the house of God we walked in concord]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/55/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA054.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV has the two men taking counsel, and the Hebrew verb is first-person plural, “we”. The Greek verb is second-person singular, a statement to the man addressed in verse 13, and its object is a plural word for foods, not for counsel. The second half of the verse, with “we walked”, is alike in both.",
