    "title": "“Put thou my tears into thy bottle: are they not in thy book?” — the Greek has no bottle and no book",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV verse has God telling the wanderings, tears put into a bottle and a question about a book. Brenton’s Greek has the speaker declaring his life to God, the tears set before God “according to thy promise”, and no bottle.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 56:8: “Thou tellest my wanderings: put thou my tears into thy bottle: are they not in thy book?” Hebrew (WLC) 56:9: נֹדִי֮ סָפַ֪רְתָּ֫ה אָ֥תָּה שִׂ֣ימָה דִמְעָתִ֣י בְנֹאדֶ֑ךָ הֲ֝לֹ֗א בְּסִפְרָתֶֽךָ. Brenton’s Greek 55:9: τὴν ζωήν μου ἐξήγγειλά σοι, ἔθου τὰ δάκρυά μου ἐνώπιόν σου, ὡς καὶ ἐν τῇ ἐπαγγελίᾳ σου [my life I told to thee; thou hast set my tears before thee, as also in thy promise]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/56/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA055.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Three things differ. In the KJV God “tellest” (counts) the wanderings; in the Greek the speaker has told his life to God. The KJV’s tears go “into thy bottle”; the Greek’s are set “before thee”. The KJV ends with a question about “thy book”; the Greek ends with a clause about “thy promise”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Brenton’s own English of the verse has “according to thy promise” (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA055.htm). The record does not say why the Greek differs. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 55 (KJV Psalm 56); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
