    "text": "KJV Psalms 60:7: “Gilead is mine, and Manasseh is mine; Ephraim also is the strength of mine head; Judah is my lawgiver;” KJV 60:8: “Moab is my washpot; over Edom will I cast out my shoe: Philistia, triumph thou because of me.” Hebrew (WLC) 60:9: יְ֝הוּדָ֗ה מְחֹֽקְקִי. Hebrew (WLC) 60:10: מֹואָ֤ב ׀ סִ֬יר רַחְצִ֗י. Brenton’s Greek 59:9: Ἰούδας βασιλεύς μου [Judas is my king]. Brenton’s Greek 59:10: Μωὰβ λέβης τῆς ἐλπίδος μου [Moab is the caldron of my hope]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/60/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA059.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both lines are members of a list of tribes and nations claimed by the speaker. In the first the Greek has a king where the KJV has a lawgiver; in the second the Greek has a vessel of hope where the KJV has a vessel for washing.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Greek puts the sentence about Judas at the end of 59:9, which is the end of the KJV’s 60:7; Brenton’s English prints it at the start of 59:10. The words are compared as printed, and the record does not say that “king” and “lawgiver” name one office. Psalm 108 repeats this passage and was not compared. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 59 (KJV Psalm 60); the Hebrew and the Greek run two verses ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
