    "text": "KJV Psalms 64:7: “But God shall shoot at them with an arrow; suddenly shall they be wounded.” KJV 64:8: “So they shall make their own tongue to fall upon themselves: all that see them shall flee away.” Hebrew (WLC) 64:8: וַיֹּרֵ֗ם אֱלֹ֫הִ֥ים חֵ֥ץ פִּתְאֹ֑ום הָ֝י֗וּ מַכֹּותָֽם. Hebrew (WLC) 64:9: וַיַּכְשִׁיל֣וּהוּ עָלֵ֣ימֹו לְשֹׁונָ֑ם. Brenton’s Greek 63:8: καὶ ὑψωθήσεται ὁ Θεός· βέλος νηπίων ἐγενήθησαν αἱ πληγαὶ αὐτῶν [and God shall be exalted; a missile of infants their wounds have become]. Brenton’s Greek 63:9: καὶ ἐξουθένησαν αὐτὸν αἱ γλῶσσαι αὐτῶν· ἐταράχθησαν πάντες οἱ θεωροῦντες αὐτοὺς [and their tongues set him at nought; all who looked on them were troubled]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/64/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA063.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV God is the archer (“shall shoot at them”) and the wicked’s own tongue is turned on them. In the Greek God is the one exalted; the weapon belongs to “infants”; the tongues treat “him” as nothing. In the KJV those who see them “shall flee away”; in the Greek they “were troubled”.",
