    "summary": "The KJV has a count, “twenty thousand”, for the chariots of God. Brenton’s Greek uses a singular chariot noun, “ten-thousand-fold”, and “thousands” of a different kind from the KJV’s “angels”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 68:17: “The chariots of God are twenty thousand, even thousands of angels: the Lord is among them, as in Sinai, in the holy place.” Hebrew (WLC) 68:18: רֶ֤כֶב אֱלֹהִ֗ים רִבֹּתַ֣יִם אַלְפֵ֣י שִׁנְאָ֑ן. Brenton’s Greek 67:18: Τὸ ἅρμα τοῦ Θεοῦ μυριοπλάσιον, χιλιάδες εὐθηνούντων. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/68/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA067.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV states a figure (twenty thousand) and the Greek states a multiple (ten-thousand-fold). The KJV’s chariots are plural and the Greek’s chariot is singular. The KJV ends the thought with “angels”; the Greek ends it with a participle that Brenton’s English prints as “rejoicing ones”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Brenton’s own English has “ten thousand fold” and “rejoicing ones” (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA067.htm). A singular Greek chariot noun can be collective and does not establish a count of one. The Hebrew has a dual form that the KJV prints as “twenty thousand”; this record does not say that the KJV’s figure is the only one the Hebrew allows. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 67 (KJV Psalm 68); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
