    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm has the Lord receive gifts “for men”. Brenton’s Greek has him receive gifts “in man”. Ephesians 4:8, which quotes the passage, has him give gifts “unto men”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 68:18: “thou hast received gifts for men” Hebrew (WLC) 68:19: לָקַ֣חְתָּ מַ֭תָּנֹות בָּאָדָ֑ם. Brenton’s Greek 67:19: ἔλαβες δόματα ἐν ἀνθρώπῳ [thou didst receive gifts in man]. KJV Ephesians 4:8: “and gave gifts unto men” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Ephesians 4:8: ἔδωκεν δόματα τοῖς ἀνθρώποις [gave gifts to the men]; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/68/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA067.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/49/4/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/49/4/.",
    "reasoning": "The psalm’s verb in all three texts quoted is “received” (KJV, Hebrew, Greek). The New Testament’s verb is “gave”. The KJV psalm’s preposition is “for”; the Greek psalm’s is “in”; the Greek of Ephesians has a dative of the plural “men”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record compares wording. It does not say that Paul used another text of the psalm, a free quotation or an interpretation, and it does not decide whether “received” and “gave” describe one act from two sides. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 67 (KJV Psalm 68); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
