    "title": "“Let their table become a snare” — the Greek psalm and Romans 11:9 add “a recompence” and “a stumblingblock”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV psalm has the table a “snare” and “a trap”. Brenton’s Greek has it “a snare … a recompence … a stumbling-block”, three of the four nouns of Romans 11:9.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 69:22: “Let their table become a snare before them: and that which should have been for their welfare, let it become a trap.” KJV 69:23: “Let their eyes be darkened, that they see not; and make their loins continually to shake.” Hebrew (WLC) 69:23: יְהִֽי־שֻׁלְחָנָ֣ם לִפְנֵיהֶ֣ם לְפָ֑ח וְלִשְׁלֹומִ֥ים לְמֹוקֵֽשׁ. Hebrew (WLC) 69:24: תֶּחְשַׁ֣כְנָה עֵ֭ינֵיהֶם מֵרְאֹ֑ות וּ֝מָתְנֵ֗יהֶם תָּמִ֥יד הַמְעַֽד. Brenton’s Greek 68:23: Γενηθήτω ἡ τράπεζα αὐτῶν ἐνώπιον αὐτῶν εἰς παγίδα, καὶ εἰς ἀνταπόδοσιν, καὶ εἰς σκάνδαλον [Let their table before them become a snare, and a recompense, and a stumbling-block]. Brenton’s Greek 68:24: τὸν νῶτον αὐτῶν διαπαντὸς σύγκαμψον [bow down their back continually]. KJV Romans 11:9: “Let their table be made a snare, and a trap, and a stumblingblock, and a recompence unto them” KJV Romans 11:10: “bow down their back alway” Greek New Testament (Tischendorf) Romans 11:9: εἰς παγίδα καὶ εἰς θήραν καὶ εἰς σκάνδαλον καὶ εἰς ἀνταπόδομα; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Romans 11:10: τὸν νῶτον αὐτῶν διαπαντὸς; the Textus Receptus text served has the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/69/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA068.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/11/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/11/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV psalm has “snare”, then “trap” for the line about welfare, and says nothing of a recompence or a stumbling block. The Greek psalm has a snare, a recompense and a stumbling-block. Romans 11:9 in the KJV has four nouns: a snare, a trap, a stumblingblock and a recompence. At 69:23 the KJV psalm has “make their loins continually to shake”, where the Greek psalm and Romans 11:10 have “bow down their back”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Greek psalm has no noun for “trap”, and Romans 11:9 has one more noun than the Greek psalm. The nouns are quoted as printed; the record does not say that Paul quoted this Greek text, and does not say what the Hebrew word printed as “welfare” means. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 68 (KJV Psalm 69); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
