    "summary": "The KJV names Sheba and Seba at 72:10 and Sheba at 72:15. Brenton’s Greek has “Arabians” and Saba at 72:10 and “Arabia” at 72:15.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 72:10: “The kings of Tarshish and of the isles shall bring presents: the kings of Sheba and Seba shall offer gifts.” KJV 72:15: “to him shall be given of the gold of Sheba” Hebrew (WLC) 72:10: מַלְכֵ֥י שְׁבָ֥א וּ֝סְבָ֗א אֶשְׁכָּ֥ר יַקְרִֽיבוּ. Hebrew (WLC) 72:15: וְיִתֶּן־לֹו֮ מִזְּהַ֪ב שְׁ֫בָ֥א. Brenton’s Greek 71:10: βασιλεῖς Ἀράβων καὶ Σαβὰ δῶρα προσάξουσι [kings of Arabians and Saba shall bring gifts]. Brenton’s Greek 71:15: ἐκ τοῦ χρυσίου τῆς Ἀραβίας [of the gold of Arabia]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/72/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA071.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the Hebrew and the KJV the two names at 72:10 are Sheba and Seba, and the name at 72:15 is the first of them. In the Greek the first name at 72:10 is a people (“of Arabians”), the second is Saba, and the name at 72:15 is “Arabia”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. Arabia may be the land of the Arabians and Sheba may lie in it; the record compares the printed names and does not identify them. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 71 (KJV Psalm 72); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
