    "title": "“It was thou, a man mine equal” — who is the friend who turned against the speaker in Psalm 55?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The speaker of Psalm 55 says the one who did him harm was not an enemy but “a man mine equal, my guide, and mine acquaintance”, with whom he “took sweet counsel together”. The verses quoted do not name the man or identify the particular episode; they describe reproach, broken covenant and hostile words.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 55:12: “For it was not an enemy that reproached me; then I could have borne it: neither was it he that hated me that did magnify himself against me; then I would have hid myself from him:” KJV 55:13: “But it was thou, a man mine equal, my guide, and mine acquaintance.” KJV 55:14: “We took sweet counsel together, and walked unto the house of God in company.” KJV 55:20: “He hath put forth his hands against such as be at peace with him: he hath broken his covenant.” KJV 55:21: “The words of his mouth were smoother than butter, but war was in his heart: his words were softer than oil, yet were they drawn swords.” Hebrew (WLC) 55:14: וְאַתָּ֣ה אֱנֹ֣ושׁ כְּעֶרְכִּ֑י אַ֝לּוּפִ֗י וּמְיֻדָּֽעִי. Brenton’s Greek 54:14: Σὺ δὲ ἄνθρωπε ἰσόψυχε, ἡγεμών μου καὶ γνωστέ μου. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/55/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA054.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The man is described by three relations (equal, guide, acquaintance) and two shared acts (counsel, going to the house of God), and verses 20–21 describe someone as having “put forth his hands against such as be at peace with him” and broken “his covenant”. The verses give no name, no office and no event, so it is not stated who the man is or what the breach was.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Possible readings: that the man is one named person known to the speaker, whom the verses leave unnamed; that “thou” is a figure for a class of friends who betray; and that verses 20–21 speak of a different person from verses 12–14. Each is an interpretation; the verses quoted state none of them, and this record asserts none. The speaker is not named in the verses quoted, and the psalm’s title is not in the packaged KJV and was not examined for this record. The psalm is verse. Check made: Psalms 55:12–14 and 55:20–21 were read in the packaged KJV; no other psalm or book was searched for a statement about this man. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 54 (KJV Psalm 55); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
