    "title": "“Let them be blotted out of the book of the living” — what book is this, and what is lost when a name is blotted out?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Psalm 69:28 asks that “them” be blotted out of “the book of the living” and “not be written with the righteous”. The verses quoted do not say what the book is or what follows from a name being removed.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 69:27: “Add iniquity unto their iniquity: and let them not come into thy righteousness.” KJV 69:28: “Let them be blotted out of the book of the living, and not be written with the righteous.” Hebrew (WLC) 69:29: יִ֭מָּחֽוּ מִסֵּ֣פֶר חַיִּ֑ים וְעִ֥ם צַ֝דִּיקִ֗ים אַל־יִכָּתֵֽבוּ. Brenton’s Greek 68:29: Ἐξαλειφθήτωσαν ἐκ βίβλου ζώντων, καὶ μετὰ δικαίων μὴ γραφήτωσαν. KJV Exodus 32:32: “blot me, I pray thee, out of thy book which thou hast written” KJV Exodus 32:33: “Whosoever hath sinned against me, him will I blot out of my book” KJV Philippians 4:3: “whose names are in the book of life” KJV Revelation 20:12: “another book was opened, which is the book of life” Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/69/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA068.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The request pairs two clauses: blotted out of “the book of the living”, and “not … written with the righteous”. What is lost depends on what the book is: a record of who is alive, a list of the righteous, or a record of those whom God counts for life. The verses of the psalm name neither the keeper nor the book’s other contents, and do not say whether blotting out concerns this life or another.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Possible readings: that the book is a register of the living, so that being blotted out is death; that it is a list of those counted among the righteous, as the second clause may suggest; and that it is the book of Exodus 32:32–33, from which a name is blotted for sin. Each is an interpretation; the verses quoted state none of them, and this record asserts none. Exodus 32:32–33, Philippians 4:3 and Revelation 20:12 are quoted as context: they name a book that God has written, a book of life, and the book of life, and they do not say that any of these is the book of Psalm 69:28. Check made: Psalm 69:27–28 was read, and the packaged KJV was searched for the phrases “book of the living” (this verse only) and “book of life” (eight verses, in Philippians and Revelation). That is a word search, not a reading of the whole Bible. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 68 (KJV Psalm 69); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
