    "title": "“Thou turnest man to destruction” — the Greek has “Turn not man back to humiliation”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 90:3 states that God turns man to destruction and says “Return”. Brenton’s Greek of the first clause is a prohibition with a negative, and the second clause is a question.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 90:3: “Thou turnest man to destruction; and sayest, Return, ye children of men.” Hebrew (WLC) 90:3: תָּשֵׁ֣ב אֱ֭נֹושׁ עַד־דַּכָּ֑א וַ֝תֹּ֗אמֶר. Brenton’s Greek 89:3: Μὴ ἀποστρέψῃς ἄνθρωπον εἰς ταπείνωσιν, καὶ εἶπας, ἐπιστρέψατε υἱοὶ ἀνθρώπων; Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA089.htm) prints: “Turn not man back to his low place, whereas thou saidst, Return, ye sons of men?”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/90/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA089.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV clause is a statement addressed to God that he turns man to destruction. The Greek begins with μή and a verb in the form of a request, so it asks that man not be turned back; and it ends with a question mark where the KJV has a full stop. The KJV states something God does; the Greek, as Brenton’s English prints it, asks him not to do it.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record does not say what the quoted Hebrew wording means, and does not say whether the Greek question mark is the editor’s punctuation or part of an older text. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 89 (KJV Psalm 90); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
