    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 90:12 asks God to teach us to number our days. In Brenton’s Greek the verb of numbering stands at the end of the verse before, and the object of “make known” in verse 12 is “thy right hand”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 90:12: “So teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts unto wisdom.” Hebrew (WLC) 90:12: לִמְנֹ֣ות יָ֭מֵינוּ כֵּ֣ן הֹודַ֑ע. Brenton’s Greek 89:11: καὶ ἀπὸ τοῦ φόβου τοῦ θυμοῦ σου ἐξαριθμήσασθαι. Brenton’s Greek 89:12: τὴν δεξιάν σου οὕτως γνώρισον, καὶ τοὺς πεπαιδευμένους τῇ καρδίᾳ ἐν σοφίᾳ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA089.htm) prints, at the start of its verse 12: “and who knows how to number his days because of the fear of thy wrath?” and then “So manifest thy right hand”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/90/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA089.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Two things differ. The verb of numbering (ἐξαριθμήσασθαι) is printed at the end of Greek verse 11 and is joined there to “the fear of thy wrath”, where the KJV has numbering our days as the matter of the request in verse 12. And what God is asked to make known in the Greek verse 12 is “thy right hand” (τὴν δεξιάν σου), where the KJV has the numbering of days.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record does not say what the quoted Hebrew wording means; it states only that the Greek verse 12 prints a noun for God’s hand where the KJV has “our days”. Brenton’s English page places the numbering clause at the start of its own verse 12 and the Greek page places it at the end of verse 11; the record quotes each where it is printed. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 89 (KJV Psalm 90); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
