    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Brenton’s Greek page prints at the head of Psalm 95:4 a sentence, “for the Lord will not cast off his people”, that the quoted KJV verses 95:3–4 do not have, and it has “the ends of the earth” where the KJV has “the deep places of the earth”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 95:3: “For the Lord is a great God, and a great King above all gods.” KJV 95:4: “In his hand are the deep places of the earth: the strength of the hills is his also.” Hebrew (WLC) 95:4: אֲשֶׁ֣ר בְּ֭יָדֹו מֶחְקְרֵי־אָ֑רֶץ. Brenton’s Greek 94:4: ὅτι οὐκ ἀπώσεται Κύριος τὸν λαὸν αὐτοῦ, ὅτι ἐν τῇ χειρὶ αὐτοῦ τὰ πέρατα τῆς γῆς. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA094.htm) prints the first sentence at the end of its verse 3: “for the Lord will not cast off his people”, and at verse 4: “For the ends of the earth are in his hands”. KJV Psalms 94:14: “For the Lord will not cast off his people”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/95/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA094.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Two things differ. The Greek has a sentence, “the Lord will not cast off his people”, that the quoted KJV verses 95:3–4 do not have; the KJV states it in another psalm (94:14), so the Greek adds it to this one. And the Greek names “the ends of the earth” (τὰ πέρατα τῆς γῆς) where the KJV names “the deep places of the earth”.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Hebrew of 95:3 and 95:4 as printed in the WLC was read for a clause of this kind and the verses quoted do not have one. The record does not say what the quoted Hebrew wording means beyond the KJV’s own wording. Brenton’s English page and the Greek page divide the added sentence between verses 3 and 4 differently; each is quoted where it prints it. The tag applies at 95:4. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 94 (KJV Psalm 95); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
