    "title": "“As in the day of temptation in the wilderness” — the Greek psalm has “the day of irritation”, where Hebrews 3:8 has “temptation”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 95:8 and of Hebrews 3:8 both have “the day of temptation in the wilderness”. In Brenton’s Greek of the psalm the noun is πικρασμοῦ, which Brenton’s English prints “irritation”; the Greek of Hebrews 3:8 has πειρασμοῦ.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 95:8: “Harden not your heart, as in the provocation, and as in the day of temptation in the wilderness:” KJV Hebrews 3:8: “in the day of temptation in the wilderness”. Hebrew (WLC) 95:8: כְּיֹ֥ום מַ֝סָּ֗ה בַּמִּדְבָּֽר. Brenton’s Greek 94:8: ὡς ἐν τῷ παραπικρασμῷ, κατὰ τὴν ἡμέραν τοῦ πικρασμοῦ ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA094.htm) prints: “according to the day of irritation in the wilderness”. Greek New Testament, Hebrews 3:8: κατὰ τὴν ἡμέραν τοῦ πειρασμοῦ ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/95/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA094.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/3/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV gives the same noun, “temptation”, in the psalm and in Hebrews. The Greek of the psalm in Brenton’s edition prints a different noun (τοῦ πικρασμοῦ) in the place where the Greek of Hebrews prints τοῦ πειρασμοῦ; Brenton’s English renders the first “irritation”. The first noun of the pair, “the provocation”, is the same word in both Greek texts (παραπικρασμῷ).",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record compares printed nouns. It does not say what the Hebrew word printed at the end of the quoted clause means, does not say whether it is here a place name or a common noun, and does not say that the two Greek nouns differ in sense for the speaker. The tag applies at Psalm 95:8; the Hebrews verse is a comparison text. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 94 (KJV Psalm 95); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
