    "text": "KJV Psalms 102:23: “He weakened my strength in the way; he shortened my days.” Hebrew (WLC) 102:24: עִנָּ֖ה בַדֶּ֥רֶךְ. Brenton’s Greek 101:24: Ἀπεκρίθη αὐτῷ ἐν ὁδῷ ἰσχύος αὐτοῦ, τὴν ὀλιγότητα τῶν ἡμερῶν μου ἀνάγγειλόν μοι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA101.htm) prints: “He answered him in the way of his strength: tell me the fewness of my days”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/102/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA101.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV verse reports two things done to the speaker: his strength weakened and his days shortened. The Greek verse reports an answer given “him” and then asks that the speaker be told the fewness of his days (ἀνάγγειλόν μοι is a request). The KJV states an act; the Greek, in its second clause, a petition.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The Hebrew text at this verse carries a written form and a read form (printed in the WLC as bracketed and parenthesised words); they are background only and the record rests on neither. The record does not say what the Hebrew verb printed first in the quotation means, and does not say who “him” is in the Greek clause. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 101 (KJV Psalm 102); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
