    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 102:26 has God change the heavens “as a vesture”. Brenton’s Greek of the psalm has the verb ἑλίξεις, which Brenton’s English prints “fold”. Hebrews 1:12 in the KJV has “fold them up”; the Textus Receptus there has ἑλίξεις and the Tischendorf text has ἀλλάξεις.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 102:26: “They shall perish, but thou shalt endure: yea, all of them shall wax old like a garment; as a vesture shalt thou change them, and they shall be changed:” KJV Hebrews 1:12: “And as a vesture shalt thou fold them up, and they shall be changed”. Hebrew (WLC) 102:27: כַּלְּב֖וּשׁ תַּחֲלִיפֵ֣ם וְֽיַחֲלֹֽפוּ. Brenton’s Greek 101:27: καὶ ὡσεὶ περιβόλαιον ἑλίξεις αὐτοὺς, καὶ ἀλλαγήσονται. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA101.htm) prints: “as a vesture shalt thou fold them, and they shall be changed”. Greek New Testament, Hebrews 1:12: Textus Receptus ελιξεις αυτους (unaccented as served); Tischendorf ἀλλάξεις αὐτούς. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/102/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA101.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/1/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/1/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV psalm has a verb of changing (“shalt thou change them”) and then “they shall be changed”. Brenton’s Greek has two different verbs: ἑλίξεις, “fold”, and then ἀλλαγήσονται, “be changed”. The KJV of Hebrews 1:12 follows the same pattern as the Greek psalm (“fold them up … be changed”), and the Textus Receptus of Hebrews has the same first verb as Brenton’s Greek psalm, while the Tischendorf text has ἀλλάξεις.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record does not say what the Hebrew verb printed in the quotation means beyond the KJV’s own word, which the KJV repeats in the next clause. The tag applies at Psalm 102:26; Hebrews 1:12 is a comparison text, and the difference between the two Greek New Testament editions is stated and not settled. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 101 (KJV Psalm 102); the Hebrew and the Greek run one verse ahead of the KJV. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
