    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 104:26 has “that leviathan”. Brenton’s Greek prints δράκων οὗτος ὃν ἔπλασας ἐμπαίζειν αὐτῷ, which Brenton’s English prints “this dragon whom thou hast made to play in it”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 104:26: “There go the ships: there is that leviathan, whom thou hast made to play therein.” Hebrew (WLC) 104:26: לִ֝וְיָתָ֗ן זֶֽה־יָצַ֥רְתָּ לְשַֽׂחֶק־בֹּֽו. Brenton’s Greek 103:26: δράκων οὗτος ὃν ἔπλασας ἐμπαίζειν αὐτῷ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA103.htm) prints: “this dragon whom thou hast made to play in it”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/104/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA103.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The creature is named “leviathan” in the KJV and δράκων in the Greek, which Brenton’s English prints “dragon”. The final clause has “therein” in the KJV and the pronoun αὐτῷ in Greek, rendered “in it” in Brenton’s English; this comparison does not establish a different referent or event.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record states the printed nouns. It does not say what creature either noun names, does not say whether the Hebrew word is here a name or a common noun, and does not answer the question of what creature is meant, which a published record on behemoth and leviathan already asks. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 103 (KJV Psalm 104); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
