    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV of Psalm 105:18 it is said of the one whose feet were hurt with fetters that “he was laid in iron”. In Brenton’s Greek the subject of the verb is “his soul”: σίδηρον διῆλθεν ἡ ψυχὴ αὐτοῦ.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 105:18: “Whose feet they hurt with fetters: he was laid in iron:” Hebrew (WLC) 105:18: בַּ֝רְזֶ֗ל בָּ֣אָה נַפְשֹֽׁו. Brenton’s Greek 104:18: σίδηρον διῆλθεν ἡ ψυχὴ αὐτοῦ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA104.htm) prints: “his soul passed into iron”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/105/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA104.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV clause has a person laid in iron. The Greek clause has the noun ἡ ψυχὴ αὐτοῦ, “his soul”, as the subject of a verb of passing and σίδηρον, “iron”, as what is passed. The Hebrew words quoted end with a noun that the KJV does not print in this clause.",
    "scope": "This compares the wording of three texts. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The record does not say that “his soul” stands for the person in either text, and does not say what the verb at the head of the Hebrew quotation means. The written form and read form at “his feet” in the earlier clause are background only. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 104 (KJV Psalm 105); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
