    "title": "“They rebelled not against his word” — who are “they” in Psalm 105:28?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Psalm 105:26–28 names Moses and Aaron as sent, says “they shewed his signs among them … in the land of Ham”, and then, after the darkness, says “they rebelled not against his word”. The verse does not name who “they” are.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 105:26: “He sent Moses his servant; and Aaron whom he had chosen.” KJV 105:27: “They shewed his signs among them, and wonders in the land of Ham.” KJV 105:28: “He sent darkness, and made it dark; and they rebelled not against his word.” Hebrew (WLC) 105:27: שָֽׂמוּ־בָ֭ם. Hebrew (WLC) 105:28: וְלֹֽא־מָ֝ר֗וּ אֶת. Brenton’s Greek 104:28: καὶ παρεπίκραναν τοὺς λόγους αὐτοῦ. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/105/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA104.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In 105:27 “they” shew the signs “among them” and the land is Ham’s, where “they” and “them” are different pronouns; in 105:28 the clause “they rebelled not” has no noun. Whoever “they” are decides whether the verse says that those sent obeyed or that those who saw the signs did not rebel. The Greek clause has no negative at all, so in that text the same question is asked of a clause that says they did rebel.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Possible readings: that “they” are Moses and Aaron, named in 105:26, who did not rebel against the word; that “they” are the people among whom the signs were shown; and that the clause is not tied to either. Each is an interpretation; the verses quoted state none of them. The record does not say what the Hebrew word at 105:28 (written and read forms are background only) means, and a separate G record compares the Greek wording of the verse. Check made: Psalms 105:26–28 were read in the packaged KJV, and the Hebrew and Greek of 105:27–28 in the editions named; no other book was searched or compared. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 104 (KJV Psalm 105); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
