    "title": "“They provoked his spirit” — whose spirit does Psalm 106:33 mean?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Psalm 106:32–33 says that “they angered him … so that it went ill with Moses”, and then that “they provoked his spirit, so that he spake unadvisedly with his lips”. The verse does not say whose spirit it is.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 106:32: “They angered him also at the waters of strife, so that it went ill with Moses for their sakes:” KJV 106:33: “Because they provoked his spirit, so that he spake unadvisedly with his lips.” KJV Numbers 20:10: “Hear now, ye rebels; must we fetch you water out of this rock?” KJV Numbers 20:12: “Because ye believed me not, to sanctify me in the eyes of the children of Israel”. KJV Isaiah 63:10: “But they rebelled, and vexed his holy Spirit”. Hebrew (WLC) 106:33: כִּֽי־הִמְר֥וּ אֶת־רוּחֹ֑ו וַ֝יְבַטֵּ֗א בִּשְׂפָתָֽיו. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/106/.",
    "reasoning": "In 106:32 the verse does not name the one the people angered (“him”), and “Moses” is named afterwards; in 106:33 “his spirit” and “he spake” follow without a noun. The pronouns could point to God or to Moses. The answer bears on how the psalm says Moses came to speak as he did: whether the people’s provocation is said to have moved his own spirit or to have grieved God’s.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. Possible readings: that “his spirit” is Moses’s spirit, so that the people’s provocation led him to speak unadvisedly; that it is God’s spirit, the people’s provocation being of him as in Isaiah 63:10; and that the clause is not tied to one person. Each is an interpretation; the verses quoted state none of them. The Numbers verses are quoted as part of the account of the waters of strife; this record does not say that the psalm follows or corrects them, and does not ask what Moses said, which Numbers 20:10 reports. Check made: Psalms 106:32–33, Numbers 20:10 and 20:12, and Isaiah 63:10 were read in the packaged KJV; no other book was searched. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 105 (KJV Psalm 106); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
