    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Job 3:8 has those who curse the day “ready to raise up their mourning”. The Hebrew’s last word is the word the KJV prints “leviathan” at Job 41:1, and Brenton’s Greek prints “the great sea monster”.",
    "text": "KJV Job 3:8: “Let them curse it that curse the day, who are ready to raise up their mourning.” Hebrew (WLC) 3:8: הָ֝עֲתִידִ֗ים עֹרֵ֥ר לִוְיָתָֽן [the ready ones / to rouse / leviathan]. KJV Job 41:1: “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?” Hebrew (WLC) Job 40:25, which is KJV 41:1: תִּמְשֹׁ֣ךְ לִוְיָתָ֣ן בְּחַכָּ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 3:8: ὁ μέλλων τὸ μέγα κῆτος χειρώσασθαι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JOB03.htm) prints: “ready to attack the great whale”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/3/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/18/40/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOB03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV has “their mourning” as the thing raised up. The Hebrew prints, at the end of the verse, the same letters that the KJV renders as the name “leviathan” at Job 41:1 (Hebrew 40:25). The Greek prints τὸ μέγα κῆτος, a great creature, and no word of mourning. Both original-language texts therefore differ from the KJV’s “mourning”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Job 3:8; the Hebrew and the Greek both differ from the KJV. The record does not say which creature is meant, does not say whether the Hebrew word is a name or a common noun in this verse, and does not say that the KJV’s “mourning” is excluded as a reading of those letters; no lexicon is supplied. The verse stands in a speech in verse. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew of Job 41:1 is numbered 40:25; the KJV verse numbers of Job 3:8 match the Hebrew and the Greek. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
