    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 107:17 opens with the noun “Fools” and says they are afflicted. Brenton’s Greek opens with a verb that Brenton’s English renders “He helped them”, and has no noun for fools.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 107:17: “Fools because of their transgression, and because of their iniquities, are afflicted.” Hebrew (WLC) 107:17: אֱ֭וִלִים מִדֶּ֣רֶךְ פִּשְׁעָ֑ם. Brenton’s Greek 106:17: Ἀντελάβετο αὐτῶν ἐξ ὁδοῦ ἀνομίας αὐτῶν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA106.htm) prints: “He helped them out of the way of their iniquity”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/107/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA106.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV clause names a group, “Fools”, and says they are afflicted because of their transgression and iniquities. The Greek clause opens with a verb followed by αὐτῶν, “them”, and Brenton’s English renders it as a help given to them out of the way of their iniquity; the Greek quoted has no word for fools. The second clause of both texts mentions iniquities and being afflicted or brought low, so the two texts share that statement and differ in the first clause.",
