    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 115:17 says the dead do not praise the Lord, “neither any that go down into silence”. Brenton’s Greek prints the place name Hades where the KJV has “silence”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 115:17: “The dead praise not the Lord, neither any that go down into silence.” Hebrew (WLC) 115:17: וְ֝לֹ֗א כָּל־יֹרְדֵ֥י דוּמָֽה. Brenton’s Greek 113:25 (KJV 115:17; Brenton’s Greek prints KJV Psalms 114 and 115 together as its Psalm 113): οὐδὲ πάντες οἱ καταβαίνοντες εἰς ᾅδου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA113.htm) prints: “nor any that go down to Hades”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/115/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA113.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both texts say that the dead do not praise and that those who go down somewhere do not praise. The KJV names where they go down with a common noun, “silence”. The Greek names it ᾅδου, the genitive of the word Brenton’s English prints as “Hades”. The same pair of renderings is already in the record on Psalm 94:17.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 115:17. The record compares what the texts print; it does not say what the Hebrew word at the end of the quotation means in a lexicon, does not say that “silence” cannot name the place of the dead, and does not say which wording is original. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 113 and prints KJV 114 and 115 together as one psalm of 26 verses; this is KJV verse 17 of Psalm 115 and Brenton’s verse 25. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
