    "text": "KJV Psalms 126:1–2: “When the Lord turned again the captivity of Zion, we were like them that dream.” “Then was our mouth filled with laughter, and our tongue with singing: then said they among the heathen, The Lord hath done great things for them.” KJV Psalms 126:3–4: “The Lord hath done great things for us; whereof we are glad.” “Turn again our captivity, O Lord, as the streams in the south.”",
    "reasoning": "The first three verses recall completed acts and express present gladness: the Lord “turned again the captivity of Zion”, “we were like them that dream”, “The Lord hath done great things for us”. The fourth verse is a request: “Turn again our captivity, O Lord”. The verses quoted do not name the captivity, the time, or the people who were turned, and do not say whether the request of 126:4 is made after the event of 126:1 or in the same breath as a memory of it. Possible readings: the first verses recall a completed return and the request asks that its fulfilment be completed; the request is made by people still in captivity who recall an earlier deliverance; the first verses are a hopeful anticipation put in the past form. The record does not decide between them.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. The question is limited to the four verses quoted. The record does not examine the psalm’s title or any other passage on a return from captivity, and does not say that any reading is the right one.",
