    "title": "“They that wasted us required of us mirth” — the Greek has “they that had carried us away asked a hymn”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 137:3 names a second group as “they that wasted us” and says they required “mirth”. Brenton’s Greek names the second group with a participle that Brenton’s English renders “carried us away”, and the thing asked for is a word that Brenton’s English renders “a hymn”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 137:3: “For there they that carried us away captive required of us a song; and they that wasted us required of us mirth, saying, Sing us one of the songs of Zion.” Hebrew (WLC) 137:3: וְתֹולָלֵ֣ינוּ שִׂמְחָ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 136:3: οἱ ἀπαγαγόντες ἡμᾶς, ὕμνον. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA136.htm) prints: “they that had carried us away asked a hymn”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/137/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA136.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both texts have two groups who ask the exiles for a song of Zion. The KJV describes the second group as those who “wasted” the speakers and says they required “mirth”. The Greek describes the second group with a participle of the verb for leading away, which Brenton’s English renders “carried us away”, and says they asked for ὕμνον, a hymn. So the KJV describes the second group by what it did (wasting) and names what it wanted as mirth; the Greek describes it as carrying away and names a hymn.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 137:3. Both texts report that the exiles were asked for one of the songs of Zion; the difference is in how the second group is described and what it is said to have asked for. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 136 (KJV Psalm 137); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
