    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 138:2 says that the Lord has magnified “thy word” above “all thy name”. Brenton’s Greek says that the Lord has magnified “the name, the holy one of thee” over everything, and prints no word for “word” in that clause.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 138:2: “I will worship toward thy holy temple, and praise thy name for thy lovingkindness and for thy truth: for thou hast magnified thy word above all thy name.” Hebrew (WLC) 138:2: הִגְדַּ֥לְתָּ עַל־כָּלשִׁ֝־מְךָ֗ אִמְרָתֶֽךָ. Brenton’s Greek 137:2: ἐμεγάλυνας ἐπὶ πᾶν τὸ ὄνομα τὸ ἅγιόν σου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA137.htm) prints: “thou hast magnified thy holy name above every thing”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/138/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA137.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV clause two things are set against each other: “thy word” is magnified, and it is magnified “above all thy name”. In the Greek clause one thing is magnified, the name, and it is called holy (τὸ ἅγιόν σου); the words that follow ἐμεγάλυνας are ἐπὶ πᾶν, “over all”, and then the name. The Greek quoted has no word for “word” in this clause, and the KJV has no word for “holy” in it.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 138:2. The record states what each text prints. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. It does not say how the Hebrew clause is to be construed. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 137 (KJV Psalm 138); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
