    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 139:4 says there is not a word in the speaker’s tongue that the Lord does not know. Brenton’s Greek has a qualifying adjective, which Brenton’s English renders “unrighteous”, so that the clause says there is no unrighteous word in the tongue.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 139:4: “For there is not a word in my tongue, but, lo, O Lord, thou knowest it altogether.” Hebrew (WLC) 139:4: כִּ֤י אֵ֣ין מִ֭לָּה בִּלְשֹׁונִ֑י. Brenton’s Greek 138:4: οὐκ ἔστι λόγος ἄδικος ἐν γλώσσῃ μου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA138.htm) prints: “there is no unrighteous word in my tongue”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/139/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA138.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the clause is about every word in the tongue: there is not one that the Lord does not know altogether. In the Greek quoted the noun λόγος is joined to the adjective ἄδικος, which Brenton’s English renders “unrighteous”, so the clause is about a kind of word. The rest of the verse in both texts says that the Lord knows: the KJV “thou knowest it altogether”, the Greek “thou hast known all things” as Brenton’s English renders it.",
