    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 139:16 says that “all my members were written” in the Lord’s book. Brenton’s Greek has a plural “all” with a verb in the future, which Brenton’s English renders “all men shall be written in thy book”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 139:16: “Thine eyes did see my substance, yet being unperfect; and in thy book all my members were written, which in continuance were fashioned, when as yet there was none of them.” Hebrew (WLC) 139:16: וְעַֽל־סִפְרְךָ֮ כֻּלָּ֪ם יִכָּ֫תֵ֥בוּ. Brenton’s Greek 138:16: ἐπὶ τὸ βιβλίον σου πάντες γραφήσονται. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA138.htm) prints: “all men shall be written in thy book”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/139/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA138.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the things written in the book are “my members”, and the verb is past. In the Greek quoted the subject is πάντες, a plural “all”, with no word for members or for the speaker, and the verb γραφήσονται is future; Brenton’s English supplies “men”. So the KJV states what was written in the book as the speaker’s members; the Greek states that all, in a form Brenton’s English renders “all men”, shall be written.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 139:16. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. The record does not say what the Hebrew word printed before the verb refers to, and does not say which wording is original. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 138 (KJV Psalm 139); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
