    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 139:17 praises God’s “thoughts” and the “sum of them”. Brenton’s Greek names “thy friends”, who “have been greatly honoured”, and says that “their rule has been greatly strengthened”, as Brenton’s English renders it.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 139:17: “How precious also are thy thoughts unto me, O God! how great is the sum of them!” Hebrew (WLC) 139:17: יָּקְר֣וּ רֵעֶ֣יךָ … מֶ֥ה עָ֝צְמוּ רָאשֵׁיהֶֽם. Brenton’s Greek 138:17: ἐτιμήθησαν οἱ φίλοι σου ὁ Θεὸς … ἐκραταιώθησαν αἱ ἀρχαὶ αὐτῶν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA138.htm) prints: “thy friends, O God, have been greatly honoured by me” and “their rule has been greatly strengthened”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/139/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA138.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s first clause is about the thoughts of God and its second about their sum. The Greek’s first clause is about persons, οἱ φίλοι σου, which Brenton’s English renders “thy friends”, and says they have been honoured; its second clause says that αἱ ἀρχαὶ αὐτῶν, which Brenton’s English renders “their rule”, has been strengthened. So the two texts name different subjects in both clauses and use different verbs.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 139:17. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. The record does not say whether the KJV’s “thoughts” is within the range of the Hebrew word printed, and does not say which wording is original. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 138 (KJV Psalm 139); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
