    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 141:4 ends with the speaker asking not to “eat of their dainties”. Brenton’s Greek has a different verb, which Brenton’s English renders “unite with”, and a plural “choice ones” where the KJV has “dainties”.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 141:4: “Incline not my heart to any evil thing, to practise wicked works with men that work iniquity: and let me not eat of their dainties.” Hebrew (WLC) 141:4: וּבַל־אֶ֝לְחַ֗ם בְּמַנְעַמֵּיהֶֽם. Brenton’s Greek 140:4: οὐ μὴ συνδοιάσω μετὰ τῶν ἐκλεκτῶν αὐτῶν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA140.htm) prints: “let me not unite with their choice ones”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/141/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA140.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV clause has a verb of eating and a thing eaten, “their dainties”. The Greek clause has a verb that Brenton’s English renders “unite with” and the preposition μετά with τῶν ἐκλεκτῶν αὐτῶν, “their choice ones” as Brenton’s English renders it. So the KJV clause is about eating a food and the Greek clause is about joining with the choice ones of the men named earlier in the verse.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 141:4. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. The record does not say who the “choice ones” are in the Greek, and does not say which wording is original. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 140 (KJV Psalm 141); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
