    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 141:7 compares the scattering of bones to a man cutting and cleaving wood. Brenton’s Greek compares it to a πάχος γῆς, which Brenton’s English renders “a lump of earth”, that was broken upon the earth; it has no word for wood and no person cutting.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 141:7: “Our bones are scattered at the grave's mouth, as when one cutteth and cleaveth wood upon the earth.” Hebrew (WLC) 141:7: כְּמֹ֤ו פֹלֵ֣חַ וּבֹקֵ֣עַ בָּאָ֑רֶץ. Brenton’s Greek 140:7: Ὡσεὶ πάχος γῆς διεῤῥάγη ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA140.htm) prints: “As a lump of earth is crushed upon the ground”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/141/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA140.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV simile has a person (“one”), two verbs (cutteth and cleaveth) and a material, “wood”. The Greek simile has a noun phrase, πάχος γῆς, a verb in the passive that Brenton’s English renders “is crushed”, and ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς, “upon the earth”. The Greek has no word for wood. The Hebrew words quoted are four words, and the Hebrew verse as printed has no word spelled עץ. The bones scattered at the mouth of the grave are named in both texts.",
