    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 145:9. The record does not say that the Greek excludes anyone from the Lord’s goodness; the second clause of the Greek has “all his works”. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. The English words “on him” are Brenton’s English; the Greek words quoted have no pronoun. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 144 (KJV Psalm 145); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
