    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "After the verse that the KJV numbers 145:13, Brenton’s Greek prints a further verse, labelled 13a, with two clauses: that the Lord is faithful in his words and holy in all his works. The KJV has no verse between 145:13 and 145:14.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 145:13: “Thy kingdom is an everlasting kingdom, and thy dominion endureth throughout all generations.” KJV Psalms 145:14: “The Lord upholdeth all that fall, and raiseth up all those that be bowed down.” KJV Psalms 145:17: “The Lord is righteous in all his ways, and holy in all his works.” Hebrew (WLC) 145:13: וּ֝מֶֽמְשֶׁלְתְּךָ֗ בְּכָל־דֹּ֥ור וָדֹֽור; 145:14 opens: סֹומֵ֣ךְ יְ֭הוָה. Brenton’s Greek 144:13a (printed between 144:13 and 144:14): πιστὸς Κύριος ἐν τοῖς λόγοις αὐτοῦ, καὶ ὅσιος ἐν πᾶσι τοῖς ἔργοις αὐτοῦ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA144.htm) prints: “The Lord is faithful in his words, and holy in all his works”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/145/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA144.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Greek verse 13a has two clauses. The second, “holy in all his works”, is stated in the KJV at 145:17 together with “righteous in all his ways”. The first, that the Lord is faithful in his words, is not in the KJV at 145:13, 145:14 or 145:17. The Hebrew edition quoted prints no verse between 145:13 and 145:14: the word printed after the end of 145:13 opens 145:14. So the Greek has a clause about the Lord’s faithfulness in his words at a place where the KJV and the Hebrew edition have none.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 145:13, where the Greek verse 13a is printed. The record states what the three texts print and checks only the verses named: 145:13, 145:14 and 145:17. It does not say that the Hebrew has no such line in any manuscript; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. It does not say which wording is original. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 144 (KJV Psalm 145); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. Brenton’s Greek prints this verse with the label 13a. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
