    "title": "“The Lord openeth the eyes of the blind” — the Greek has “the Lord gives wisdom to the blind”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 146:8 says the Lord “openeth the eyes of the blind”. Brenton’s Greek has a verb that Brenton’s English renders “gives wisdom”, with “the blind” as its object, and prints no word for eyes in that clause.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 146:8: “The Lord openeth the eyes of the blind: the Lord raiseth them that are bowed down: the Lord loveth the righteous:” Hebrew (WLC) 146:8: יְהוָ֤ה ׀ פֹּ֘קֵ֤חַ עִוְרִ֗ים. Brenton’s Greek 145:8: Κύριος σοφοῖ τυφλοὺς. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA145.htm) prints: “the Lord gives wisdom to the blind”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/146/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA145.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV clause names an organ, “the eyes”, and a verb of opening. The Greek clause has the verb σοφοῖ, which Brenton’s English renders “gives wisdom”, and the object τυφλούς, “the blind”; it has no noun for eyes. The two texts therefore state different acts of the Lord toward the blind: the opening of their eyes, and the giving of wisdom. The remaining clauses of the verse, that he raises the bowed down and loves the righteous, are stated in both.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 146:8. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. The record does not say whether either wording is figurative, and does not say which wording is original. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The psalm is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the singer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers this psalm 145 (KJV Psalm 146); the verse numbers of the Hebrew and the Greek match the KJV in this psalm. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
