    "title": "“Even as a father the son in whom he delighteth” — the Greek has “scourges every son whom he receives”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 3:12 compares the Lord’s correction to a father correcting “the son in whom he delighteth”. Brenton’s Greek says he “scourges every son whom he receives”, wording that the Greek New Testament prints at Hebrews 12:6.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 3:12: “For whom the Lord loveth he correcteth; even as a father the son in whom he delighteth.” Hebrew (WLC) 3:12: וּ֝כְאָ֗ב אֶת־בֵּ֥ן יִרְצֶֽה. Brenton’s Greek 3:12: μαστιγοῖ δὲ πάντα υἱὸν ὃν παραδέχεται. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO03.htm) prints: “for whom the Lord loves, he rebukes, and scourges every son whom he receives”. KJV Hebrews 12:6: “For whom the Lord loveth he chasteneth, and scourgeth every son whom he receiveth.” Greek New Testament Hebrews 12:6: μαστιγοῖ δὲ πάντα υἱὸν ὃν παραδέχεται. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/3/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO03.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/12/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/12/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s second clause is a comparison: the Lord corrects as a father corrects the son in whom he “delighteth”. The Greek’s second clause has no father and no delight; it names a second act, scourging, and says it falls on “every son whom he receives”. The KJV’s Hebrews 12:6 says “scourgeth every son whom he receiveth”, and the Greek New Testament wording at Hebrews 12:6 prints the same words as the Greek of Proverbs 3:12 for that clause. The KJV’s Proverbs 3:12 and its Hebrews 12:6 therefore word the second clause differently.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 3:12. The record does not say which text the writer of Hebrews followed and does not say what the Hebrew verb of the first clause or of the last word of the verse means. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
