    "title": "“He scorneth the scorners” — the Greek has “the Lord resists the proud”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 3:34 says “he scorneth the scorners”. Brenton’s Greek prints a verb that Brenton’s English renders “resists” and a noun it renders “the proud”, the wording the Greek New Testament prints at James 4:6.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 3:34: “Surely he scorneth the scorners: but he giveth grace unto the lowly.” Hebrew (WLC) 3:34: אִם־לַלֵּצִ֥ים הֽוּא־יָלִ֑יץ. Brenton’s Greek 3:34: Κύριος ὑπερηφάνοις ἀντιτάσσεται, ταπεινοῖς δὲ δίδωσι χάριν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO03.htm) prints: “The Lord resists the proud; but he gives grace to the humble”. KJV James 4:6: “But he giveth more grace. Wherefore he saith, God resisteth the proud, but giveth grace unto the humble.” Greek New Testament James 4:6: ὑπερηφάνοις ἀντιτάσσεται, ταπεινοῖς δὲ δίδωσιν χάριν. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/3/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO03.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/59/4/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/59/4/.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the first clause names a kind of person as “scorners” and says the Lord scorns them. In the Greek the first clause names the proud (ὑπερηφάνοις) and says the Lord resists them (ἀντιτάσσεται). The second clause of both texts has the same sense, grace to the lowly or humble. The KJV’s James 4:6 says “God resisteth the proud”, and the Greek New Testament wording there matches the Greek of Proverbs 3:34 in the verb and in the noun. So the KJV of Proverbs 3:34 and the KJV of James 4:6 word the first clause with different verbs and different persons.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 3:34. The record does not say that the Hebrew word rendered “scorners” means “the proud”, and does not say which text the writer of James followed. The Hebrew edition prints written and read forms in the last clause; they are not quoted. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
