    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 8:22 says “The Lord possessed me in the beginning of his way, before his works of old”. Brenton’s Greek prints a verb that Brenton’s English renders “made”, and ends “for his works”.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 8:22: “The Lord possessed me in the beginning of his way, before his works of old.” Hebrew (WLC) 8:22: יְֽהוָ֗ה קָ֭נָנִי רֵאשִׁ֣ית דַּרְכֹּ֑ו. Brenton’s Greek 8:22: Κύριος ἔκτισέ με ἀρχὴν ὁδῶν αὐτοῦ εἰς ἔργα αὐτοῦ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO08.htm) prints: “The Lord made me the beginning of his ways for his works”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/8/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO08.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The speaker in 8:22 is the one who has been crying out as wisdom since 8:1. The KJV says the Lord “possessed” her in the beginning of his way, and places that “before his works of old”. The Greek verb is ἔκτισε, which Brenton’s English renders “made”, and the Greek says the beginning of his ways was “for his works”, where the KJV has “before his works of old”. So the verb and the closing phrase differ between the KJV and the Greek.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 8:22. The record does not say what the Hebrew verb means, does not say that “possessed” and “made” cannot describe one act, and does not decide what the speaker is; the question of who or what the speaker is stands in its own question record. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
