    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 11:30 ends “he that winneth souls is wise”. Brenton’s Greek ends with a clause that Brenton’s English renders “the souls of transgressors are cut off before their time”.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 11:30: “The fruit of the righteous is a tree of life; and he that winneth souls is wise.” Hebrew (WLC) 11:30: וְלֹקֵ֖חַ נְפָשֹׂ֣ות חָכָֽם. Brenton’s Greek 11:30: ἀφαιροῦνται δὲ ἄωροι ψυχαὶ παρανόμων. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO11.htm) prints: “but the souls of transgressors are cut off before their time”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/11/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO11.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both texts end the verse with a clause about “souls”. In the KJV the person is one who “winneth” souls and is called wise. In the Greek the souls belong to transgressors (παρανόμων) and are taken away before their time (ἄωροι). So the clause is a statement about a wise person in the KJV and a statement about what happens to the souls of transgressors in the Greek.",
    "scope": "T applies at the last clause of Proverbs 11:30. The first clause (the fruit of the righteous as a tree of life) is worded much alike in the two texts and is not the finding. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
