    "title": "“The righteous shall be recompensed in the earth” — the Greek has “If the righteous scarcely be saved”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 11:31 says the righteous “shall be recompensed in the earth”, and the wicked much more. Brenton’s Greek asks “where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear?” after “If the righteous scarcely be saved”, wording the Greek New Testament prints at 1 Peter 4:18.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 11:31: “Behold, the righteous shall be recompensed in the earth: much more the wicked and the sinner.” Hebrew (WLC) 11:31: הֵ֣ן צַ֭דִּיק בָּאָ֣רֶץ יְשֻׁלָּ֑ם אַ֝֗ף כִּֽי־רָשָׁ֥ע וְחֹוטֵֽא. Brenton’s Greek 11:31: Εἰ ὁ μὲν δίκαιος μόλις σώζεται, ὁ ἀσεβὴς καὶ ἁμαρτωλὸς ποῦ φανεῖται;. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO11.htm) prints: “If the righteous scarcely be saved, where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear?”. KJV 1 Peter 4:18: “And if the righteous scarcely be saved, where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear?” Greek New Testament 1 Peter 4:18: ὁ δίκαιος μόλις σῴζεται, ὁ ἀσεβὴς καὶ ἁμαρτωλὸς ποῦ φανεῖται. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/11/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO11.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/60/4/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/60/4/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV makes a statement: the righteous will be recompensed on earth, and “much more” the wicked and the sinner. The Greek makes a conditional question: if the righteous are saved with difficulty, where will the ungodly and the sinner appear? The KJV’s 1 Peter 4:18 words the question in the same way as the Greek (“the righteous scarcely be saved, where shall the ungodly and the sinner appear”). The KJV of Proverbs 11:31 and of 1 Peter 4:18 therefore word the matter differently.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 11:31. The Greek New Testament words are quoted from Tischendorf’s edition; the Textus Receptus text served there prints the same words, compared without accents. The record does not say which text the writer of 1 Peter followed. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
