    "title": "“Go to the ant” — the Greek adds “go to the bee”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 6:6–8 sends the sluggard to the ant and ends with her food in summer and harvest. Brenton’s Greek prints three further verses after 6:8 that send the sluggard “to the bee”; no verse of the KJV book of Proverbs names the bee.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 6:6–8: “Go to the ant, thou sluggard; consider her ways, and be wise:” “Which having no guide, overseer, or ruler,” “Provideth her meat in the summer, and gathereth her food in the harvest.” Brenton’s Greek 6:8a: ἢ πορεύθητι πρὸς τὴν μέλισσαν, καὶ μάθε ὡς ἐργάτις ἐστὶ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO06.htm) prints: “Or go to the bee, and learn how diligent she is”. Brenton’s Greek 6:8c: τὴν σοφίαν τιμήσασα προήχθη. Brenton’s own English prints: “she is advanced by honouring wisdom”. A search of every verse of the packaged KJV book of Proverbs for the word “bee” finds nothing. Fetched from https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO06.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s three verses give one example for the sluggard, the ant. Brenton’s Greek prints the ant verses and then three lettered verses, 6:8a, 6:8b and 6:8c, that set a second example, the bee, beside the first. The KJV book of Proverbs does not name the bee at all, so the second example is not stated by the KJV verses or elsewhere in its Proverbs.",
    "scope": "T applies at the verses printed after 6:8 in Brenton’s Greek; the KJV number nearest to them is 6:8. The statement about the KJV is limited to the book of Proverbs and to a word search. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s Greek numbers the three added verses 6:8a, 6:8b and 6:8c; the Hebrew has no verses of these numbers. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
