    "title": "Proverbs 22:8 — the Greek adds “God loves a cheerful and liberal man”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 22:8 has two clauses about iniquity and the rod of anger, and 22:9 says the one with a bountiful eye shall be blessed. Brenton’s Greek prints, after 22:8, a verse that says God blesses a cheerful and liberal man. The Greek New Testament prints a sentence about God and a cheerful giver at 2 Corinthians 9:7.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 22:8–9: “He that soweth iniquity shall reap vanity: and the rod of his anger shall fail.” “He that hath a bountiful eye shall be blessed; for he giveth of his bread to the poor.” Brenton’s Greek 22:8a: ἄνδρα ἱλαρὸν καὶ δότην εὐλογεῖ ὁ Θεὸς. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO22.htm) prints: “God loves a cheerful and liberal man”. KJV 2 Corinthians 9:7: “Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver.” Greek New Testament 2 Corinthians 9:7: ἱλαρὸν γὰρ δότην ἀγαπᾷ ὁ θεός. Fetched from https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO22.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/47/9/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/47/9/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s 22:8 speaks of the one who sows iniquity and the failure of his rod, and 22:9 says the one with a bountiful eye shall be blessed because he gives of his bread. Neither verse says that God loves or blesses a person for being cheerful in giving. Brenton’s Greek prints a verse between them that names the object of God’s regard as a cheerful and liberal man. The KJV states that God loves a cheerful giver at 2 Corinthians 9:7, in another book.",
    "scope": "T applies at the verse Brenton’s Greek prints after Proverbs 22:8 (numbered 22:8a; the Hebrew has no verse of that number). Proverbs 22:9 in the KJV does speak of blessing and of giving; the record limits the difference to what 22:8 and 22:9 do not say, that God loves or blesses a person who is cheerful in giving. The record does not say which text the writer of 2 Corinthians followed. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
