    "title": "“Put a knife to thy throat” — the Greek has “apply thine hand, knowing that it behoves thee to prepare such meats”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 23:2 tells the one at a ruler’s table to “put a knife to thy throat” if he is given to appetite. Brenton’s Greek tells him to apply his hand, knowing that he must prepare such meats, and puts the appetite clause after it.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 23:1–2: “When thou sittest to eat with a ruler, consider diligently what is before thee:” “And put a knife to thy throat, if thou be a man given to appetite.” Hebrew (WLC) 23:2: וְשַׂמְתָּ֣ שַׂכִּ֣ין בְּלֹעֶ֑ךָ. Brenton’s Greek 23:2: Καὶ ἐπίβαλλε τὴν χεῖρά σου, εἰδὼς ὅτι τοιαῦτά σε δεῖ παρασκευάσαι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO23.htm) prints: “and apply thine hand, knowing that it behoves thee to prepare such meats”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/23/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO23.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both texts give a command in 23:2. The KJV follows it with a condition about appetite; the quoted Greek gives a reason about preparing such meats. The KJV’s command is to put a knife to one’s throat. The Greek’s command is to put one’s hand to something, with the reason that it is fitting to prepare such things. So the command is different in what it asks the reader to do.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 23:2. The record does not say what the Hebrew phrase means or whether the “knife” is a figure of speech. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
