    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 29:18 says “Where there is no vision, the people perish”. Brenton’s Greek prints a noun that Brenton’s English renders “interpreter” and says there shall be none “to a sinful nation”.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 29:18: “Where there is no vision, the people perish: but he that keepeth the law, happy is he.” Hebrew (WLC) 29:18: בְּאֵ֣ין חָ֭זֹון יִפָּ֣רַֽע עָ֑ם. Brenton’s Greek 29:18: Οὐ μὴ ὑπάρξῃ ἐξηγητὴς ἔθνει παρανόμῳ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO29.htm) prints: “There shall be no interpreter to a sinful nation”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/29/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO29.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s first clause names a missing thing, “vision”, and says what follows: the people “perish”. The Greek’s first clause names a missing person, an interpreter (ἐξηγητής), says he is missing for a sinful nation, and has no verb for perishing. The second clause is worded alike in both texts (the one who keeps the law is happy or blessed).",
    "scope": "T applies at the first clause of Proverbs 29:18. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
