    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 31:21 says the woman is not afraid of the snow for her household, because all of them are clothed with scarlet. Brenton’s Greek says her husband is not anxious about those at home when he is away, because all of her household are clothed.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 31:21: “She is not afraid of the snow for her household: for all her household are clothed with scarlet.” Hebrew (WLC) 31:21: לֹא־תִירָ֣א לְבֵיתָ֣הּ מִשָּׁ֑לֶג כִּ֥י כָל־בֵּ֝יתָ֗הּ לָבֻ֥שׁ שָׁנִֽים. Brenton’s Greek 31:21: Οὐ φροντίζει τῶν ἐν οἴκῳ ὁ ἀνὴρ αὐτῆς ὅταν που χρονίζῃ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO31.htm) prints: “Her husband is not anxious about those at home when he tarries anywhere abroad: for all her household are clothed”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/31/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO31.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the woman is the one who is not afraid, the thing not feared is the snow, and the reason is that her household are clothed with scarlet. In the Greek the one who is not anxious is her husband, the occasion is that he tarries away, and the reason is only that her household are clothed. The words for snow and scarlet do not occur in the Greek of 31:21, and Brenton’s Greek prints a different subject for the verb.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 31:21. The record does not say what the Hebrew word printed last in the verse means, and does not compare 31:22, where Brenton’s English has “double” and “scarlet”. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
