    "title": "“The words of Agur the son of Jakeh … unto Ithiel and Ucal” — the Greek has “These things says the man to them that trust in God; and I cease”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 30:1 names Agur the son of Jakeh as the speaker and Ithiel and Ucal as those addressed. Brenton’s Greek prints none of the four names; it says “the man” speaks “to them that trust in God” and ends “I cease”.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 30:1: “The words of Agur the son of Jakeh, even the prophecy: the man spake unto Ithiel, even unto Ithiel and Ucal,” Hebrew (WLC) 30:1: דִּבְרֵ֤י ׀ אָג֥וּר בִּן־יָקֶ֗ה … הַ֭גֶּבֶר לְאִֽיתִיאֵ֑ל לְאִ֖יתִיאֵ֣ל וְאֻכָֽל. Brenton’s Greek 24:22f: Τάδε λέγει ὁ ἀνὴρ τοῖς πιστεύουσι Θεῷ, καὶ παύομαι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO24.htm) prints: “These things says the man to them that trust in God; and I cease”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/30/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s verse has four proper names: a speaker, his father, and two addressees. The Greek sentence that stands where the Hebrew and the KJV have this verse has the speaker as “the man” and the addressees as those who trust in God (τοῖς πιστεύουσι Θεῷ), and has a verb, παύομαι, which Brenton’s English renders “I cease”, where the KJV has the names Ithiel and Ucal. So the KJV prints names where the Greek prints a description of the speaker and of the hearers.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 30:1. Brenton’s Greek prints the Hebrew chapter 30 and 31:1–9 inside its own chapter 24, under the lettered verses 24:22f–24:53 and the verses 24:54–24:62; the Hebrew and the KJV number these verses 30:1–33 and 31:1–9. The sentence quoted follows a bracketed passage that Brenton’s edition prints before it. The record does not say what the Hebrew words rendered “Ithiel” and “Ucal” mean, or whether the Hebrew words are names; it states what the three texts print. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
