    "title": "“I neither learned wisdom” — the Greek has “God has taught me wisdom”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 30:3 says “I neither learned wisdom, nor have the knowledge of the holy”. Brenton’s Greek says God has taught the speaker wisdom and that he knows the knowledge of the holy.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 30:2–3: “Surely I am more brutish than any man, and have not the understanding of a man.” “I neither learned wisdom, nor have the knowledge of the holy.” Hebrew (WLC) 30:3: וְלֹֽא־לָמַ֥דְתִּי חָכְמָ֑ה וְדַ֖עַת קְדֹשִׁ֣ים אֵדָֽע. Brenton’s Greek 24:22g: Ἀφρονέστατος γάρ εἰμι ἁπάντων ἀνθρώπων. Brenton’s Greek 24:22h: Θεὸς δεδίδαχέ με σοφίαν, καὶ γνῶσιν ἁγίων ἔγνωκα. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO24.htm) prints: “God has taught me wisdom, and I know the knowledge of the holy”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/30/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both texts have the speaker say in the verse before that he is the most foolish or brutish of men. In the next verse the KJV has two negations: he did not learn wisdom and does not have the knowledge of the holy. The Greek has two affirmations: God has taught him wisdom and he knows the knowledge of the holy. So the two texts state opposite things about what the speaker has.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 30:3. Brenton’s Greek prints the Hebrew chapter 30 and 31:1–9 inside its own chapter 24, under the lettered verses 24:22f–24:53 and the verses 24:54–24:62; the Hebrew and the KJV number these verses 30:1–33 and 31:1–9. The record does not say how a reader should reconcile the verse with the verse before it in either text. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
