    "title": "“The words of king Lemuel” — the Greek has “My words have been spoken by God”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 31:1 names “king Lemuel” and calls the words “the prophecy that his mother taught him”. Brenton’s Greek prints no name; it says “My words have been spoken by God”, and calls them the oracular answer of a king whom his mother instructed.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 31:1: “The words of king Lemuel, the prophecy that his mother taught him.” Hebrew (WLC) 31:1: דִּ֭בְרֵי לְמוּאֵ֣ל מֶ֑לֶךְ. Brenton’s Greek 24:54: Οἱ ἐμοὶ λόγοι εἴρηνται ὑπὸ Θεοῦ, βασιλέως χρηματισμὸς. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO24.htm) prints: “My words have been spoken by God—the oracular answer of a king, whom his mother instructed”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/31/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO24.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV introduces the words as those of a named king, Lemuel, taught by his mother. The Greek introduces them as words spoken by God (ὑπὸ Θεοῦ) and as the answer of a king, with no name, and again says the mother instructed him. In the KJV the king is the speaker; in the Greek the words are said to be spoken by God and the king is described as the one whose mother instructed him.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 31:1; the KJV’s “O Lemuel” at 31:4 is a second place where it prints the name that the Greek page does not print. Brenton’s Greek prints the Hebrew chapter 30 and 31:1–9 inside its own chapter 24, under the lettered verses 24:22f–24:53 and the verses 24:54–24:62; the Hebrew and the KJV number these verses 30:1–33 and 31:1–9. The record does not say what the Hebrew word printed second in the verse means or whether it is a name. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
