    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 26:10 names “the great God that formed all things” as the one who rewards the fool and transgressors. Brenton’s Greek says that all the flesh of fools endures much hardship, because their fury is brought to nought.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 26:10: “The great God that formed all things both rewardeth the fool, and rewardeth transgressors.” Hebrew (WLC) 26:10: רַ֥ב מְחֹֽולֵֽל־כֹּ֑ל וְשֹׂכֵ֥ר כְּ֝סִ֗יל וְשֹׂכֵ֥ר עֹבְרִֽים. Brenton’s Greek 26:10: Πολλὰ χειμάζεται πᾶσα σὰρξ ἀφρόνων. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO26.htm) prints: “All the flesh of fools endures much hardship; for their fury is brought to nought”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/26/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO26.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s verse has a subject, “the great God that formed all things”, and says he rewards the fool and rewards transgressors. The Greek verse has no such subject: it says that the whole flesh of fools is much tossed about, and gives a reason. So the KJV says that God rewards the fool and transgressors, and the Greek says that fools endure hardship.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 26:10. The record does not say what the first Hebrew word of the verse means or how the clause is to be read in Hebrew. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
