    "title": "“The words of a talebearer are as wounds” — the Greek of 18:8 has “Fear casts down the slothful”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Proverbs 18:8 says “The words of a talebearer are as wounds”. Brenton’s Greek of 18:8 says “Fear casts down the slothful; and the souls of the effeminate shall hunger”. The Hebrew of 26:22 repeats the words of 18:8, and Brenton’s Greek of 26:22 is worded differently from its 18:8.",
    "text": "KJV Proverbs 18:8: “The words of a talebearer are as wounds, and they go down into the innermost parts of the belly.” Hebrew (WLC) 18:8: דִּבְרֵ֣י נִ֭רְגָּן כְּמִֽתְלַהֲמִ֑ים וְ֝הֵ֗ם יָרְד֥וּ חַדְרֵי־בָֽטֶן. Brenton’s Greek 18:8: Ὀκνηροὺς καταβάλλει φόβος. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO18.htm) prints: “Fear casts down the slothful; and the souls of the effeminate shall hunger”. KJV Proverbs 26:22: “The words of a talebearer are as wounds, and they go down into the innermost parts of the belly.” Hebrew (WLC) 26:22: דִּבְרֵ֣י נִ֭רְגָּן כְּמִֽתְלַהֲמִ֑ים וְ֝הֵ֗ם יָרְד֥וּ חַדְרֵי־בָֽטֶן. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PRO26.htm) prints at 26:22: “The words of cunning knaves are soft”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/18/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/20/26/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PRO18.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s 18:8 has a subject, the talebearer’s words, and a comparison with wounds. The Greek of 18:8 has no talebearer and no wounds: it names fear as what casts down the slothful and says what happens to the souls of the effeminate. The Hebrew of 26:22 prints the same seven words as the Hebrew of 18:8, and the Greek of 26:22 has a sentence about the words of cunning persons being soft, so the Greek renders the repeated Hebrew sentence in two different ways. The finding is the difference at 18:8.",
    "scope": "T applies at Proverbs 18:8 only; 26:22 is quoted to show that the Greek of the repeated Hebrew wording is worded differently there. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is verse and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
